{"parentUuid":"d9dbadf4-e99b-4093-b300-ee9295250767","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac44742970c7341bd","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffciLbCuAqo42RwJRHSP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UE8WHKJ1Hw71RGarNkTWKj","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging; cat review_supertraining_ch5b_opus.md","description":"Read round-1 review"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5560,"cache_read_input_tokens":50287,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5560,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01UE8WHKJ1Hw71RGarNkTWKj":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging; cat review_supertraining_ch5b_opus.md","description":"Read round-1 review"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffciLJqRkmBji12iUrfg","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"d9d6924f-c42c-40e8-b20d-e33f108b2ad9","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:26:21.129Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"d9d6924f-c42c-40e8-b20d-e33f108b2ad9","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"0b81aa46-3ac1-41a7-bee9-ff3aba05ec92","agentId":"ac44742970c7341bd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UE8WHKJ1Hw71RGarNkTWKj","type":"tool_result","content":"# Review: Supertraining Ch 5 batch 5b (5.2.4-5.2.5), Opus independent reviewer\n\nScope: the 30 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch5b.txt`. Source: *Supertraining* 6th Expanded, PDF idx 278-298 (printed 267-287). I rendered and opened every page image in that range (`staging/_renders/r5b_p278.png` to `r5b_p298.png`) and checked each number below against the image, not the text layer. I also checked the Ch4 cite at PDF 261-262 in the text layer. Every \"Eric's baseline:\" quote was checked against `docs/method-baseline.md` with its line number.\n\nSummary: **17 GO, 13 FIX, 0 NO-GO.** No entry is rated `proven`. The four `untested` ratings are right. Of the 8 `contested` entries, 2 are not real contradictions (surface, high-volume) and should be `supported`. One more (ceiling) overstates its conflict. The other 5 hold.\n\nPhysics and arithmetic checks (all correct, all labelled Inference in the entries):\n- 9.8 × √4.9 = 21.6932, so P = 21.693·B·√H = B·g·√(gH/2), which is body weight times the mean speed of a free fall from rest through H. 100 kg at 0.50 m gives 1533.9 W; at 0.55 m it gives 1608.8 W.\n- Contact momentum is m·√(2gh). 100 kg from 0.5 m gives 313.2 kg·m/s; from 1.0 m it gives 442.9 kg·m/s, a ratio of 1.414 (√2).\n- Dursenev and Raevsky: 1500 kgf / 20 = 75 kg, so \"over 20 times bodyweight\" at the low end needs a bodymass under 75 kg.\n\n## Entry table\n\n| File | Verdict | Problem (entry vs source, page) | Exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| case-study--soviet-sport-specific-strength-endurance-prescriptions | GO | Checked on PDF 297 / p.286: 65% (Chistyakov); 55-60% bodymass, 8-10 reps, 60-85% BM, one-third BM, 18-20 reps (Likachyevskaya & Kovalchuk); 60-80% (Popov 1966); 80-85%, 4-12 reps, 2-4 min, 15-25 reps, 3-5 sets, 5-8 min (Mikhailov & Panov); 50-80% and 30-40% (Ulrich); 18-20 kg, 40%, 35-50 kg, 2-3 reps, 28% (Chumakova); squats +20-30 a week, from 50 up to 200-300 reps over 2-3 months. The \"28%\" ambiguity is correctly kept. | None. |\n| claim--depth-landings-from-2-to-3-2-m-produce-forces-over-20-times-bodyweight | GO | Checked on PDF 288 / p.277: \"Dursenev and Raevsky (1978)\", 2 m, \"soft gymnastic mats\", 0.028-0.061 s, 1500-3500 kgf, \"over 20 times bodyweight\", 3.2 m, the caveats, \"firm shock-absorbing surfaces\". | Optional wording fix: \"over 20 times bodyweight at 1500 kgf needs a bodymass under 75 kg\", not \"holds for a 75 kg subject\". |\n| claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers | FIX | All numbers check on PDF 288 / p.277: 475 jumps, 12 weeks, 1472 push-offs, 90-95%, 70-80%, 30-40%, 93 tons. But \"When to apply\" turns \"provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions\" into \"a direct sequencing rule\" without noting the batch-5c finding on PDF 299 / p.288: for novices, strength-then-speed-strength order showed \"no statistically significant difference\". | Add to When NOT to apply: \"The resistance-first rule is untested for novices; on PDF 299 / p.288 order made no significant difference in novices (see [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]]).\" Add that link to Related. Title: the source says \"93 tons\". |\n| claim--heavy-squats-and-light-fast-lifts-miss-the-eccentric-concentric-switch | GO | Checked on PDF 278 / p.267: quasi-isometric, spine load, \"squat thrusts with a 60kg barbell\", \"vicious cycle\". Baseline line 104 quote is exact. | None. |\n| claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance | FIX | `contested` rests on the \"futile to rely predominantly\" vs \"plyometrics should be the main method\" conflict (PDF 288-289). That conflict is about a neighbouring sentence on explosive strength, not this entry's claim, which is Nicol et al. 1997 on high-volume fatigue resistance. Nothing in the book contradicts the Nicol claim, and it is stated the same way twice (PDF 280 / p.269 and PDF 288 / p.277, both checked). Baseline line 122 quote is exact. | Change confidence to `supported` (single undescribed citation). Cut the \"Internal conflict, recorded\" block down to one Related pointer to [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]], which owns that conflict. |\n| claim--lewis-formula-estimates-jump-power-from-bodymass-and-height | FIX | Formula checked on the image of PDF 288 / p.277: \"(21.693) B√H\". The arithmetic and the falling-body reading are correct and labelled Inference. But the title states the Inference as fact: \"...It Computes a Falling-Body Power, Not Take-Off Power\". The baseline pointer to line 77 is a paraphrase, and that line is tagged \"Inferred (protocol; author uncertain)\". | Retitle: \"The Lewis Formula P = 21.693 x B x √H Tracks Jump Progress (Inference: It Is Weight Times Mean Free-Fall Speed, Not Take-Off Power)\". After the line-77 pointer, add \"(Inferred, protocol)\". |\n| claim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method | FIX | Checked on PDF 280 / p.269: all five cartilage cites and years, Swanepoel 1998, Nicol 1997, overload and overuse. On PDF 281 / p.270 the right margin is cut in the scan: the image shows \"adequ[...]\" and \"running a[...] jumping\". The entry quotes \"adequate restoration regimes\" and \"running or jumping\". \"or\" is not on the page; \"a...\" most likely reads \"and\". | Quote as printed: \"adequ[ate] restoration regimes\" and \"running a[nd] jumping (right margin cut in the scan, PDF 281)\". |\n| claim--popular-plyometric-drills-work-through-other-mechanisms | GO | Checked on PDF 293 / p.282: Chu 1992, Radcliffe & Farantinos 1985, the medicine-ball amortisation sentence. Checked on PDF 294 / p.283 (image, not just the text layer): the \"other mechanisms\" list and the PNF jerk. Checked on PDF 284: pseudo-plyometrics. `untested` fits. | Optional: change \"p. 294 from the text layer\" to \"verified against the page image\". |\n| claim--strength-endurance-hypertrophy-and-cyclic-endurance | GO | Checked on PDF 297 / p.286: Maisuradze 1960, 25% sandbag, Maison & Broeker 1941, McMorris & Elkins 1954, Chernyaev 1965, R = 0.77 (Zhalei 1964). Checked on PDF 298 / p.287: \"In no cases...\" rule. The Ch4 cite \"contributes more to the largest improvements in distance sports\" is at PDF 261-262 as stated. The contradiction is real but partly about scope (strength training in general vs named exercises); the entry says so. | Optional: lead with the stronger same-page tension (squats \"hinder\" cyclic endurance, while Mikhailov & Panov prescribe 50-300-rep squats for skaters), which is now only in When NOT to apply. |\n| claim--squat-1-5-to-2-bodymass-rule-applies-to-maximal-depth-jumps-only | GO | Checked on PDF 293 / p.282: \"1.5 - 2 times\", \"6 times bodyweight\", italics kept. Checked on PDF 294 / p.283: \"apply religiously to submaximal plyometrics\". | None. |\n| claim--plyometric-skill-is-cognitive-and-state-dependent | GO | Checked on the images of PDF 294-295 / pp.283-284: cognitive vs non-cognitive, \"1 RM movements\", Skinner 1938, Wise (Sci Am Oct 1988: 23), Calvin (Oct 1994), state-dependent learning. `untested` fits. | Optional: change \"text layer\" to \"verified against the page image\". |\n| claim--zatsiorsky-and-hansen-show-strength-endurance-training-specificity | GO | Checked on PDF 296 / p.285: 84%, 10 times a day, 5 weeks, 60%, 93%, \"28 repetitions per minute\" (faint but legible as 28), 5 s on and 2 s off, 219%. Checked on PDF 297: Hansen 1963. The 219% attribution ambiguity is correctly flagged. | None. |\n| claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task | GO | Checked on PDF 281-282: Wilson 1990 (4 s), Chapman & Caldwell 1985 (0.25 s), Wilson 1991 (0.37 s), the 0.2 s summary, Bosco 1982a. Fig 5.6's caption reads \"Wilson et al, 1991\", confirmed. The curve starts near 0.3 s, is about 50% at about 0.85 s, about 80% at 2 s and 100% at about 4 s. Fig 5.7 runs 0.1-0.25 s, close to linear. `contested` is justified: the book itself calls it an \"apparent contradiction\", and the caption and text years clash. | Optional \"Eric's review\" item: the bench-press delay study is probably Wilson, Elliott & Wood, *Med Sci Sports Exerc* 1991; 23:364-370. If so, the caption year is right and the text's \"1990\" is wrong. That is my recollection, not checked; verify before adding. |\n| framework--five-phases-of-a-plyometric-action | FIX | Fig 5.5 description checked on PDF 281 / p.270 and correct. But the entry calls the caption and text definitions of EM delay a conflict the book \"does not reconcile\". The caption (\"between signal to terminate initial momentum phase and instant when eccentric contraction begins\") and item 2 (from the onset of the action potential to the onset of contraction) describe the same interval in different words. That creates a contradiction the book does not have. | Replace with: \"The caption words the EM delay differently (signal to terminate the initial momentum phase, to the start of eccentric contraction); Inference: both describe the electrical-to-mechanical interval.\" In When NOT to apply, drop \"without noting the caption and text differ\". |\n| framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function | FIX | The Fig 5.8 description checks against PDF 284 / p.273, including the text-figure mismatch the entry notes. Voice is wrong: the passage uses Siff's term \"powermetrics\" (\"suggested elsewhere in this text\", PDF 279 / p.268), and Fig 5.8 is captioned \"(powermetric) training\", so this is Siff's scheme. The entry says \"The authors replace...\" and \"The authors' own\". | Change \"the authors\" to \"the text (Siff's classification; it uses his term 'powermetrics', PDF 279 / p.268)\" throughout. |\n| framework--non-impact-plyometrics-ericson-and-martial-arts | GO | Checked on the images of PDF 290-292: Supersitting, SMART, 240 beats per minute, 60 minutes, karate comparison, thrust-kick sentence, Pretoria 1995, NSCA 1994. Voice (Siff) is correct. `untested` fits. | Optional: \"on p. 281\" should read \"printed p. 281 / PDF 292\". |\n| framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation | GO | Checked on PDF 296 / p.285: all 8 methods, the cautions, the see-7.1 pointer. The mRSI reference (lines 74-75) is labelled Inference. | None. |\n| heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact | FIX | Rules check on PDF 285-287 (items 1, 5, 8 and the strength-fitness sentence). Fig 5.10 matches the image. Two problems: (1) \"Eric's baseline: the department reads reactive ability through mRSI\" misstates line 74, which makes mRSI the daily readiness marker, not a reactive-ability read. (2) \"considerably lower\" is quoted with no locator; it is Ch4, printed p.222, not this section. | (1) Change to: \"Eric's baseline: mRSI from daily CMJ testing 'was deliberately chosen as the daily athlete-readiness marker' (line 74). Inference: a drop-jump RSI at stepped heights would be a separate test of the authors' transition rule; the baseline has no drop-jump test.\" (2) Add \"(Ch4.2.3, printed p.222)\". |\n| heuristic--depth-jump-execution-cues-landing-gaze-breath | GO | Checked on PDF 284-287: items 2, 3, 9, 10 and 12, the landing paragraph, \"pneumatic shock absorption\", \"Forced exhalation\", Fig 5.9 range sentence and caption (\"using resistance and other apparatus\"). The breathing tension is fairly recorded. | None. |\n| heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size | FIX | Quotes, 40%/30% and 80% check on PDF 289 / p.278. `contested` holds: \"futile to rely predominantly\" (PDF 288-289) against \"main method\" (PDF 289) is a real textual clash, though partly about scope (sport-specific skill vs a physical quality). Three problems: (1) The DSI baseline omits the tag that line 76 requires: \"[contested; the ratio tracks IMTP strength; one controlled trial (Pleša 2024, basketball, split at 0.90) supported only the strength arm; no football data]\". (2) The Example's \"jumping from depth\" is not in the p.278 passage. (3) The \"resistance movements precede\" rule has the same novice caveat as in the 475-jumps row. | (1) Add the line-76 tag verbatim after the Q21 quote. (2) Rewrite the Example as \"Olympic weightlifting (large resistance) vs situations where 'the resistance is small and the working effect depends upon the reactive ability'\", or label the depth-jump part Inference. (3) Add the PDF 299 novice note and the link to [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]]. |\n| heuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement | GO | Checked on PDF 286-287: items 3, 4, 5 and 15 (once or twice a week, 3 × 2 × 10, 3-4 days, 10-14 days, 10 days, 5-6 days). `contested` (item 3 vs item 4) is defensible, and the entry already calls item 3 an \"exception\". Baseline line 105 quote is exact. | Suggested: line 105 is tagged Inferred and unclaimed. Add line 186 (Explicit, Q15: lifting and hard practice end 72 h before a Saturday game) as the in-season anchor. |\n| heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest | FIX | All numbers check on PDF 283-287: 2-4 min, 10-12 min, about 10 min, 5-8 reps, sets of 10, \"1-15 minutes\" (printed so), 4×10, 2-3×5-8, 10-15 min, 3×2×10, \"over 100\", \"1 RM movements\". Problems: (1) Fig 5.11 (PDF 287 / p.276, caption \"Jumping exercises for developing reactive ability\") shows ground-based vertical, tuck and split jumps with no box. Item 1's \"Each exercise is executed in sets of 10 repetitions, with 1-15 minutes\" governs that complex, so it is not a depth-jump dose and should not support Model 2. (2) Voice: Model 1 is Siff's text (the powermetrics and 1RM-analogy passages), while Model 2 is the \"prolific experience\" list from Verkhoshansky 1977. The entry calls both \"the authors'\". The core conflict (item 2's 4×10 energetic depth jumps vs Model 1) is real, so `contested` stays. | (1) Move item 1 out of Model 2 into a note: \"Fig 5.11 shows ground jumps without a box; the sets of 10 and '1-15 minutes' apply to that complex (PDF 286-287).\" Remove \"1-15\" from the depth-jump rest list. (2) Label Model 1 \"Siff's text\" and Model 2 \"the depth-jump recommendations (Verkhoshansky 1977 figures)\". Optional: replace the line-105 anchor with line 186, as in the previous row. |\n| heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern | GO | Checked on PDF 279 / p.268: 0.15 s, \"red hot\", feedforward, Zanon 1966, Siff voice. Checked on PDF 283 / p.272: jogging counter-example, RFD. Baseline line 96 quote is exact. No duplicate of the Ch1 terminology entry, which does not hold the 0.15 s rule. | None. |\n| heuristic--match-plyometric-drills-to-limb-symmetry-and-rotational-dominance | GO | Checked on the images of PDF 289-290: rotational dominance, the uncited \"Research has shown\" sentence, alternate-asymmetric and limb-synchronisation methods, Witwatersrand. `untested` fits and is not too high. | Optional: change \"p. 290 from the text layer\" to \"verified against the page image\". |\n| heuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus | FIX | Quotes check on PDF 284, 288 and 289. The \"internal tension\" is a misreading, not a contradiction. On p.278 the text reiterates the p.273 point (\"as alluded to earlier ... soft-soled shoes and very resilient jumping surfaces\") before adding the sand finding, so the author sets them side by side on purpose. The soft mats on p.277 belong to a landing-only experiment, and \"suitably firm shock-absorbing\" is one balanced instruction. | Change confidence to `supported`. Replace the \"Internal tension\" block with: \"Inference: these are different aims (firm, not very soft surfaces for maximal rebounds; sand for short-term jumping-skill gains; mats for no-rebound depth-landing experiments); the text places sand beside the soft-surface warning on purpose.\" Keep the swing and pogo-stick ideas flagged as without data. |\n| heuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height | FIX | Checked on PDF 283 / p.272: \"m.ΔV\", \"CFmax\". Checked on PDF 285 / p.274: items 1 and 4. Physics verified (see above), labelled Inference. Voice: the intensity scale is in Siff's \"powermetrics\" passage, but the entry says \"The authors propose\". | Change to \"The text (Siff, using his term 'powermetrics') proposes...\". Items 1 and 4 (Verkhoshansky 1977 prescription list) can stay \"the authors'\". |\n| heuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps | GO | Checked on PDF 286-288: items 1, 6, 7, 13 and 14 (several months, about 25 reps, about 20 reps), the eccentric and explosive-isometric bullet, static start, the Lewis pointer. Baseline line 101 quote is exact (Explicit Q1). | None. |\n| heuristic--resisted-and-water-plyometrics | FIX | Checked on the images of PDF 292-293. The printed sentence spans the page break as \"No resistance / methods should not use heavy weights or stiff elastic cords\" (a double negative). The entry silently rewrites it as \"Resistance methods 'should not use...'\". | Quote as printed: \"No resistance methods should not use heavy weights or stiff elastic cords during depth jumps\" (sic, PDF 292-293 / pp.281-282; the intended sense is clearly 'should not'). Change \"p. 292 from the text layer\" to \"verified\". |\n| heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling | FIX | Checked on PDF 296-298: 8 determinants, 25-50%, 60-120 reps a minute, 60% of work time, 75-80% (Zatsiorsky 1966), summary rule. Problems: (1) The skaters' 80-85% is a real clash with the ceiling, but \"Male rowers ... 50-80%\" sits at or under it, so it is not a conflict. (2) \"to-fatigue strength-endurance sets would add glycolytic load the department deliberately does not program\" contradicts line 117: Eric (Q19), \"our staff does program it in the off-season\". | (1) Drop the rowers from the conflict; keep the skaters. (2) Rewrite: \"Eric's baseline: glycolytic work is a 'worst case scenario' in season (line 116) but staff do program it in the off-season (line 117, Q19). Inference: to-fatigue strength-endurance sets fit the off-season, not in-season weeks.\" |\n| heuristic--strength-endurance-under-demanding-sport-like-conditions | GO | Checked on PDF 297 / p.286: Popov 1966, Zhilei 1964 vs Zhalei 1964 (both spellings on the page), Chuprun 1966, 57.1%, 27.8%, 9.1%. Checked on PDF 278 / p.267: the 5.2.3 speed-strength link. | None. |\n\n## Contested entries: are they real?\n\n| Entry | Real contradiction? |\n|---|---|\n| prestretch-benefit-decay | Yes. The book calls it an \"apparent contradiction\", and the Fig 5.6 caption year (1991) clashes with the text (1990). |\n| brief-maximal-tension | Yes, textually (\"futile to rely predominantly\" vs \"main method\", half a page apart). It is partly about scope; Inference: probably a Siff insertion next to the original text. |\n| depth-jump-weekly-frequency | Defensible (item 4 spacing vs item 3's three sessions a week); could also be read as a deliberate exception. |\n| maximal-plyometric-volume-and-rest | Yes for item 2 (4×10 energetic depth jumps) vs Model 1. No for item 1 (Fig 5.11 shows ground jumps). |\n| strength-endurance-ceiling | Yes for skaters 80-85%; no for rowers 50-80%. |\n| strength-endurance-hypertrophy | Yes, partly about scope; the same-page squat tension is stronger than the Ch4 one. |\n| high-volume-plyometrics | No. The conflict belongs to a neighbouring sentence. Change to `supported`. |\n| surface-footwear | No. Different aims, placed side by side on purpose. Change to `supported`. |\n\n## Duplicates\n\nNo duplicates found. I checked the vault supertraining folder, the staging folder (including batches 5a and 5c) and the vault research papers for depth jump, plyometric, strength-endurance, Lewis, inertia and state-dependent. The overlaps already link to the earlier entries: Ch1 shock-method terminology, Ch1.7 fibre-type prescription, Ch4.2.3 optimal drop height, and Ch3.2.2 strength-endurance taxonomy. Missing cross-batch link: the 5c entry `case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments` should link both ways to `claim--depth-jump-training-beat-...` and `heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-...` (resistance-before-plyometrics rule).\n\n## Missing ideas (in the covered pages, not captured)\n\n1. The low-inertia vs high-inertia training effect (PDF 284 / p.273): with equal force from F = ma, \"Explosive, low inertia training targets involuntary, neuromuscular and central nervous processes more strongly than high inertia training, which has a greater effect on static strength development and muscle hypertrophy.\" Check first whether `principle--equal-force-different-loading-methods` (Ch1) already holds it.\n2. Strength-endurance specificity (PDF 296 / p.285): it \"is expressed to a lesser degree than the specificity of speed ... while its carry-over from one activity to another is greater.\"\n3. Fig 5.9 (PDF 285 / p.274, Verkhoshansky 1977): a catalogue of apparatus-based plyometric exercises for upper body, trunk and legs (drop push-ups between blocks, pulley-resisted kicks, incline sled). It is only cited for the range-of-motion sentence.\n4. Upper-body transfer of the depth-jump principles (PDF 288 / p.277): medicine ball catching and throwing, plyometric press-ups and bench presses, loaded swings. Only one line in the preparatory entry; relevant to linemen's hand work.\n5. \"Avoid the term plyometric drills\" (PDF 281 / p.270): drills suggest \"militarism and authoritarianism rather than individual flow\". Minor, voice-of-Siff coaching stance.\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** Required fixes:\n1. high-volume: change `contested` to `supported`; point to the brief-maximal-tension entry for the conflict.\n2. surface-footwear: change `contested` to `supported`; rewrite the tension as different aims.\n3. strength-endurance-ceiling: drop rowers from the conflict; correct the glycolytic inference using baseline line 117.\n4. brief-maximal-tension: add the line-76 DSI tag verbatim; fix the \"jumping from depth\" Example; add the novice-sequencing caveat and link.\n5. 475-jumps: add the novice-sequencing caveat and link; title \"tons\".\n6. depth-jump-height: correct the mRSI baseline statement (readiness marker, line 74); add the Ch4 p.222 locator for \"considerably lower\".\n7. volume-and-rest: move item 1 and \"1-15 min\" out of the depth-jump model (Fig 5.11 shows ground jumps); label voices (Siff vs Verkhoshansky 1977).\n8. five-phases: soften the EM-delay \"conflict\" to a wording difference.\n9. classification and momentum-intensity: change the voice to Siff for the powermetrics passages.\n10. Lewis: take the Inference out of the title or label it; mark line 77 as Inferred.\n11. injury-risk: quote the PDF 281 margin-cut words as printed (\"adequ[ate]\", \"running a[nd] jumping\").\n12. resisted-water: quote the \"No resistance methods should not use\" double negative as printed.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"8ee3b67b-43e4-49bb-a44b-27cad6f86518","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:26:21.441Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# Review: Supertraining Ch 5 batch 5b (5.2.4-5.2.5), Opus independent reviewer\n\nScope: the 30 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch5b.txt`. Source: *Supertraining* 6th Expanded, PDF idx 278-298 (printed 267-287). I rendered and opened every page image in that range (`staging/_renders/r5b_p278.png` to `r5b_p298.png`) and checked each number below against the image, not the text layer. I also checked the Ch4 cite at PDF 261-262 in the text layer. Every \"Eric's baseline:\" quote was checked against `docs/method-baseline.md` with its line number.\n\nSummary: **17 GO, 13 FIX, 0 NO-GO.** No entry is rated `proven`. The four `untested` ratings are right. Of the 8 `contested` entries, 2 are not real contradictions (surface, high-volume) and should be `supported`. One more (ceiling) overstates its conflict. The other 5 hold.\n\nPhysics and arithmetic checks (all correct, all labelled Inference in the entries):\n- 9.8 × √4.9 = 21.6932, so P = 21.693·B·√H = B·g·√(gH/2), which is body weight times the mean speed of a free fall from rest through H. 100 kg at 0.50 m gives 1533.9 W; at 0.55 m it gives 1608.8 W.\n- Contact momentum is m·√(2gh). 100 kg from 0.5 m gives 313.2 kg·m/s; from 1.0 m it gives 442.9 kg·m/s, a ratio of 1.414 (√2).\n- Dursenev and Raevsky: 1500 kgf / 20 = 75 kg, so \"over 20 times bodyweight\" at the low end needs a bodymass under 75 kg.\n\n## Entry table\n\n| File | Verdict | Problem (entry vs source, page) | Exact correction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| case-study--soviet-sport-specific-strength-endurance-prescriptions | GO | Checked on PDF 297 / p.286: 65% (Chistyakov); 55-60% bodymass, 8-10 reps, 60-85% BM, one-third BM, 18-20 reps (Likachyevskaya & Kovalchuk); 60-80% (Popov 1966); 80-85%, 4-12 reps, 2-4 min, 15-25 reps, 3-5 sets, 5-8 min (Mikhailov & Panov); 50-80% and 30-40% (Ulrich); 18-20 kg, 40%, 35-50 kg, 2-3 reps, 28% (Chumakova); squats +20-30 a week, from 50 up to 200-300 reps over 2-3 months. The \"28%\" ambiguity is correctly kept. | None. |\n| claim--depth-landings-from-2-to-3-2-m-produce-forces-over-20-times-bodyweight | GO | Checked on PDF 288 / p.277: \"Dursenev and Raevsky (1978)\", 2 m, \"soft gymnastic mats\", 0.028-0.061 s, 1500-3500 kgf, \"over 20 times bodyweight\", 3.2 m, the caveats, \"firm shock-absorbing surfaces\". | Optional wording fix: \"over 20 times bodyweight at 1500 kgf needs a bodymass under 75 kg\", not \"holds for a 75 kg subject\". |\n| claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers | FIX | All numbers check on PDF 288 / p.277: 475 jumps, 12 weeks, 1472 push-offs, 90-95%, 70-80%, 30-40%, 93 tons. But \"When to apply\" turns \"provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions\" into \"a direct sequencing rule\" without noting the batch-5c finding on PDF 299 / p.288: for novices, strength-then-speed-strength order showed \"no statistically significant difference\". | Add to When NOT to apply: \"The resistance-first rule is untested for novices; on PDF 299 / p.288 order made no significant difference in novices (see [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]]).\" Add that link to Related. Title: the source says \"93 tons\". |\n| claim--heavy-squats-and-light-fast-lifts-miss-the-eccentric-concentric-switch | GO | Checked on PDF 278 / p.267: quasi-isometric, spine load, \"squat thrusts with a 60kg barbell\", \"vicious cycle\". Baseline line 104 quote is exact. | None. |\n| claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance | FIX | `contested` rests on the \"futile to rely predominantly\" vs \"plyometrics should be the main method\" conflict (PDF 288-289). That conflict is about a neighbouring sentence on explosive strength, not this entry's claim, which is Nicol et al. 1997 on high-volume fatigue resistance. Nothing in the book contradicts the Nicol claim, and it is stated the same way twice (PDF 280 / p.269 and PDF 288 / p.277, both checked). Baseline line 122 quote is exact. | Change confidence to `supported` (single undescribed citation). Cut the \"Internal conflict, recorded\" block down to one Related pointer to [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]], which owns that conflict. |\n| claim--lewis-formula-estimates-jump-power-from-bodymass-and-height | FIX | Formula checked on the image of PDF 288 / p.277: \"(21.693) B√H\". The arithmetic and the falling-body reading are correct and labelled Inference. But the title states the Inference as fact: \"...It Computes a Falling-Body Power, Not Take-Off Power\". The baseline pointer to line 77 is a paraphrase, and that line is tagged \"Inferred (protocol; author uncertain)\". | Retitle: \"The Lewis Formula P = 21.693 x B x √H Tracks Jump Progress (Inference: It Is Weight Times Mean Free-Fall Speed, Not Take-Off Power)\". After the line-77 pointer, add \"(Inferred, protocol)\". |\n| claim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method | FIX | Checked on PDF 280 / p.269: all five cartilage cites and years, Swanepoel 1998, Nicol 1997, overload and overuse. On PDF 281 / p.270 the right margin is cut in the scan: the image shows \"adequ[...]\" and \"running a[...] jumping\". The entry quotes \"adequate restoration regimes\" and \"running or jumping\". \"or\" is not on the page; \"a...\" most likely reads \"and\". | Quote as printed: \"adequ[ate] restoration regimes\" and \"running a[nd] jumping (right margin cut in the scan, PDF 281)\". |\n| claim--popular-plyometric-drills-work-through-other-mechanisms | GO | Checked on PDF 293 / p.282: Chu 1992, Radcliffe & Farantinos 1985, the medicine-ball amortisation sentence. Checked on PDF 294 / p.283 (image, not just the text layer): the \"other mechanisms\" list and the PNF jerk. Checked on PDF 284: pseudo-plyometrics. `untested` fits. | Optional: change \"p. 294 from the text layer\" to \"verified against the page image\". |\n| claim--strength-endurance-hypertrophy-and-cyclic-endurance | GO | Checked on PDF 297 / p.286: Maisuradze 1960, 25% sandbag, Maison & Broeker 1941, McMorris & Elkins 1954, Chernyaev 1965, R = 0.77 (Zhalei 1964). Checked on PDF 298 / p.287: \"In no cases...\" rule. The Ch4 cite \"contributes more to the largest improvements in distance sports\" is at PDF 261-262 as stated. The contradiction is real but partly about scope (strength training in general vs named exercises); the entry says so. | Optional: lead with the stronger same-page tension (squats \"hinder\" cyclic endurance, while Mikhailov & Panov prescribe 50-300-rep squats for skaters), which is now only in When NOT to apply. |\n| claim--squat-1-5-to-2-bodymass-rule-applies-to-maximal-depth-jumps-only | GO | Checked on PDF 293 / p.282: \"1.5 - 2 times\", \"6 times bodyweight\", italics kept. Checked on PDF 294 / p.283: \"apply religiously to submaximal plyometrics\". | None. |\n| claim--plyometric-skill-is-cognitive-and-state-dependent | GO | Checked on the images of PDF 294-295 / pp.283-284: cognitive vs non-cognitive, \"1 RM movements\", Skinner 1938, Wise (Sci Am Oct 1988: 23), Calvin (Oct 1994), state-dependent learning. `untested` fits. | Optional: change \"text layer\" to \"verified against the page image\". |\n| claim--zatsiorsky-and-hansen-show-strength-endurance-training-specificity | GO | Checked on PDF 296 / p.285: 84%, 10 times a day, 5 weeks, 60%, 93%, \"28 repetitions per minute\" (faint but legible as 28), 5 s on and 2 s off, 219%. Checked on PDF 297: Hansen 1963. The 219% attribution ambiguity is correctly flagged. | None. |\n| claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task | GO | Checked on PDF 281-282: Wilson 1990 (4 s), Chapman & Caldwell 1985 (0.25 s), Wilson 1991 (0.37 s), the 0.2 s summary, Bosco 1982a. Fig 5.6's caption reads \"Wilson et al, 1991\", confirmed. The curve starts near 0.3 s, is about 50% at about 0.85 s, about 80% at 2 s and 100% at about 4 s. Fig 5.7 runs 0.1-0.25 s, close to linear. `contested` is justified: the book itself calls it an \"apparent contradiction\", and the caption and text years clash. | Optional \"Eric's review\" item: the bench-press delay study is probably Wilson, Elliott & Wood, *Med Sci Sports Exerc* 1991; 23:364-370. If so, the caption year is right and the text's \"1990\" is wrong. That is my recollection, not checked; verify before adding. |\n| framework--five-phases-of-a-plyometric-action | FIX | Fig 5.5 description checked on PDF 281 / p.270 and correct. But the entry calls the caption and text definitions of EM delay a conflict the book \"does not reconcile\". The caption (\"between signal to terminate initial momentum phase and instant when eccentric contraction begins\") and item 2 (from the onset of the action potential to the onset of contraction) describe the same interval in different words. That creates a contradiction the book does not have. | Replace with: \"The caption words the EM delay differently (signal to terminate the initial momentum phase, to the start of eccentric contraction); Inference: both describe the electrical-to-mechanical interval.\" In When NOT to apply, drop \"without noting the caption and text differ\". |\n| framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function | FIX | The Fig 5.8 description checks against PDF 284 / p.273, including the text-figure mismatch the entry notes. Voice is wrong: the passage uses Siff's term \"powermetrics\" (\"suggested elsewhere in this text\", PDF 279 / p.268), and Fig 5.8 is captioned \"(powermetric) training\", so this is Siff's scheme. The entry says \"The authors replace...\" and \"The authors' own\". | Change \"the authors\" to \"the text (Siff's classification; it uses his term 'powermetrics', PDF 279 / p.268)\" throughout. |\n| framework--non-impact-plyometrics-ericson-and-martial-arts | GO | Checked on the images of PDF 290-292: Supersitting, SMART, 240 beats per minute, 60 minutes, karate comparison, thrust-kick sentence, Pretoria 1995, NSCA 1994. Voice (Siff) is correct. `untested` fits. | Optional: \"on p. 281\" should read \"printed p. 281 / PDF 292\". |\n| framework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation | GO | Checked on PDF 296 / p.285: all 8 methods, the cautions, the see-7.1 pointer. The mRSI reference (lines 74-75) is labelled Inference. | None. |\n| heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact | FIX | Rules check on PDF 285-287 (items 1, 5, 8 and the strength-fitness sentence). Fig 5.10 matches the image. Two problems: (1) \"Eric's baseline: the department reads reactive ability through mRSI\" misstates line 74, which makes mRSI the daily readiness marker, not a reactive-ability read. (2) \"considerably lower\" is quoted with no locator; it is Ch4, printed p.222, not this section. | (1) Change to: \"Eric's baseline: mRSI from daily CMJ testing 'was deliberately chosen as the daily athlete-readiness marker' (line 74). Inference: a drop-jump RSI at stepped heights would be a separate test of the authors' transition rule; the baseline has no drop-jump test.\" (2) Add \"(Ch4.2.3, printed p.222)\". |\n| heuristic--depth-jump-execution-cues-landing-gaze-breath | GO | Checked on PDF 284-287: items 2, 3, 9, 10 and 12, the landing paragraph, \"pneumatic shock absorption\", \"Forced exhalation\", Fig 5.9 range sentence and caption (\"using resistance and other apparatus\"). The breathing tension is fairly recorded. | None. |\n| heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size | FIX | Quotes, 40%/30% and 80% check on PDF 289 / p.278. `contested` holds: \"futile to rely predominantly\" (PDF 288-289) against \"main method\" (PDF 289) is a real textual clash, though partly about scope (sport-specific skill vs a physical quality). Three problems: (1) The DSI baseline omits the tag that line 76 requires: \"[contested; the ratio tracks IMTP strength; one controlled trial (Pleša 2024, basketball, split at 0.90) supported only the strength arm; no football data]\". (2) The Example's \"jumping from depth\" is not in the p.278 passage. (3) The \"resistance movements precede\" rule has the same novice caveat as in the 475-jumps row. | (1) Add the line-76 tag verbatim after the Q21 quote. (2) Rewrite the Example as \"Olympic weightlifting (large resistance) vs situations where 'the resistance is small and the working effect depends upon the reactive ability'\", or label the depth-jump part Inference. (3) Add the PDF 299 novice note and the link to [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]]. |\n| heuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement | GO | Checked on PDF 286-287: items 3, 4, 5 and 15 (once or twice a week, 3 × 2 × 10, 3-4 days, 10-14 days, 10 days, 5-6 days). `contested` (item 3 vs item 4) is defensible, and the entry already calls item 3 an \"exception\". Baseline line 105 quote is exact. | Suggested: line 105 is tagged Inferred and unclaimed. Add line 186 (Explicit, Q15: lifting and hard practice end 72 h before a Saturday game) as the in-season anchor. |\n| heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest | FIX | All numbers check on PDF 283-287: 2-4 min, 10-12 min, about 10 min, 5-8 reps, sets of 10, \"1-15 minutes\" (printed so), 4×10, 2-3×5-8, 10-15 min, 3×2×10, \"over 100\", \"1 RM movements\". Problems: (1) Fig 5.11 (PDF 287 / p.276, caption \"Jumping exercises for developing reactive ability\") shows ground-based vertical, tuck and split jumps with no box. Item 1's \"Each exercise is executed in sets of 10 repetitions, with 1-15 minutes\" governs that complex, so it is not a depth-jump dose and should not support Model 2. (2) Voice: Model 1 is Siff's text (the powermetrics and 1RM-analogy passages), while Model 2 is the \"prolific experience\" list from Verkhoshansky 1977. The entry calls both \"the authors'\". The core conflict (item 2's 4×10 energetic depth jumps vs Model 1) is real, so `contested` stays. | (1) Move item 1 out of Model 2 into a note: \"Fig 5.11 shows ground jumps without a box; the sets of 10 and '1-15 minutes' apply to that complex (PDF 286-287).\" Remove \"1-15\" from the depth-jump rest list. (2) Label Model 1 \"Siff's text\" and Model 2 \"the depth-jump recommendations (Verkhoshansky 1977 figures)\". Optional: replace the line-105 anchor with line 186, as in the previous row. |\n| heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern | GO | Checked on PDF 279 / p.268: 0.15 s, \"red hot\", feedforward, Zanon 1966, Siff voice. Checked on PDF 283 / p.272: jogging counter-example, RFD. Baseline line 96 quote is exact. No duplicate of the Ch1 terminology entry, which does not hold the 0.15 s rule. | None. |\n| heuristic--match-plyometric-drills-to-limb-symmetry-and-rotational-dominance | GO | Checked on the images of PDF 289-290: rotational dominance, the uncited \"Research has shown\" sentence, alternate-asymmetric and limb-synchronisation methods, Witwatersrand. `untested` fits and is not too high. | Optional: change \"p. 290 from the text layer\" to \"verified against the page image\". |\n| heuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus | FIX | Quotes check on PDF 284, 288 and 289. The \"internal tension\" is a misreading, not a contradiction. On p.278 the text reiterates the p.273 point (\"as alluded to earlier ... soft-soled shoes and very resilient jumping surfaces\") before adding the sand finding, so the author sets them side by side on purpose. The soft mats on p.277 belong to a landing-only experiment, and \"suitably firm shock-absorbing\" is one balanced instruction. | Change confidence to `supported`. Replace the \"Internal tension\" block with: \"Inference: these are different aims (firm, not very soft surfaces for maximal rebounds; sand for short-term jumping-skill gains; mats for no-rebound depth-landing experiments); the text places sand beside the soft-surface warning on purpose.\" Keep the swing and pogo-stick ideas flagged as without data. |\n| heuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height | FIX | Checked on PDF 283 / p.272: \"m.ΔV\", \"CFmax\". Checked on PDF 285 / p.274: items 1 and 4. Physics verified (see above), labelled Inference. Voice: the intensity scale is in Siff's \"powermetrics\" passage, but the entry says \"The authors propose\". | Change to \"The text (Siff, using his term 'powermetrics') proposes...\". Items 1 and 4 (Verkhoshansky 1977 prescription list) can stay \"the authors'\". |\n| heuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps | GO | Checked on PDF 286-288: items 1, 6, 7, 13 and 14 (several months, about 25 reps, about 20 reps), the eccentric and explosive-isometric bullet, static start, the Lewis pointer. Baseline line 101 quote is exact (Explicit Q1). | None. |\n| heuristic--resisted-and-water-plyometrics | FIX | Checked on the images of PDF 292-293. The printed sentence spans the page break as \"No resistance / methods should not use heavy weights or stiff elastic cords\" (a double negative). The entry silently rewrites it as \"Resistance methods 'should not use...'\". | Quote as printed: \"No resistance methods should not use heavy weights or stiff elastic cords during depth jumps\" (sic, PDF 292-293 / pp.281-282; the intended sense is clearly 'should not'). Change \"p. 292 from the text layer\" to \"verified\". |\n| heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling | FIX | Checked on PDF 296-298: 8 determinants, 25-50%, 60-120 reps a minute, 60% of work time, 75-80% (Zatsiorsky 1966), summary rule. Problems: (1) The skaters' 80-85% is a real clash with the ceiling, but \"Male rowers ... 50-80%\" sits at or under it, so it is not a conflict. (2) \"to-fatigue strength-endurance sets would add glycolytic load the department deliberately does not program\" contradicts line 117: Eric (Q19), \"our staff does program it in the off-season\". | (1) Drop the rowers from the conflict; keep the skaters. (2) Rewrite: \"Eric's baseline: glycolytic work is a 'worst case scenario' in season (line 116) but staff do program it in the off-season (line 117, Q19). Inference: to-fatigue strength-endurance sets fit the off-season, not in-season weeks.\" |\n| heuristic--strength-endurance-under-demanding-sport-like-conditions | GO | Checked on PDF 297 / p.286: Popov 1966, Zhilei 1964 vs Zhalei 1964 (both spellings on the page), Chuprun 1966, 57.1%, 27.8%, 9.1%. Checked on PDF 278 / p.267: the 5.2.3 speed-strength link. | None. |\n\n## Contested entries: are they real?\n\n| Entry | Real contradiction? |\n|---|---|\n| prestretch-benefit-decay | Yes. The book calls it an \"apparent contradiction\", and the Fig 5.6 caption year (1991) clashes with the text (1990). |\n| brief-maximal-tension | Yes, textually (\"futile to rely predominantly\" vs \"main method\", half a page apart). It is partly about scope; Inference: probably a Siff insertion next to the original text. |\n| depth-jump-weekly-frequency | Defensible (item 4 spacing vs item 3's three sessions a week); could also be read as a deliberate exception. |\n| maximal-plyometric-volume-and-rest | Yes for item 2 (4×10 energetic depth jumps) vs Model 1. No for item 1 (Fig 5.11 shows ground jumps). |\n| strength-endurance-ceiling | Yes for skaters 80-85%; no for rowers 50-80%. |\n| strength-endurance-hypertrophy | Yes, partly about scope; the same-page squat tension is stronger than the Ch4 one. |\n| high-volume-plyometrics | No. The conflict belongs to a neighbouring sentence. Change to `supported`. |\n| surface-footwear | No. Different aims, placed side by side on purpose. Change to `supported`. |\n\n## Duplicates\n\nNo duplicates found. I checked the vault supertraining folder, the staging folder (including batches 5a and 5c) and the vault research papers for depth jump, plyometric, strength-endurance, Lewis, inertia and state-dependent. The overlaps already link to the earlier entries: Ch1 shock-method terminology, Ch1.7 fibre-type prescription, Ch4.2.3 optimal drop height, and Ch3.2.2 strength-endurance taxonomy. Missing cross-batch link: the 5c entry `case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments` should link both ways to `claim--depth-jump-training-beat-...` and `heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-...` (resistance-before-plyometrics rule).\n\n## Missing ideas (in the covered pages, not captured)\n\n1. The low-inertia vs high-inertia training effect (PDF 284 / p.273): with equal force from F = ma, \"Explosive, low inertia training targets involuntary, neuromuscular and central nervous processes more strongly than high inertia training, which has a greater effect on static strength development and muscle hypertrophy.\" Check first whether `principle--equal-force-different-loading-methods` (Ch1) already holds it.\n2. Strength-endurance specificity (PDF 296 / p.285): it \"is expressed to a lesser degree than the specificity of speed ... while its carry-over from one activity to another is greater.\"\n3. Fig 5.9 (PDF 285 / p.274, Verkhoshansky 1977): a catalogue of apparatus-based plyometric exercises for upper body, trunk and legs (drop push-ups between blocks, pulley-resisted kicks, incline sled). It is only cited for the range-of-motion sentence.\n4. Upper-body transfer of the depth-jump principles (PDF 288 / p.277): medicine ball catching and throwing, plyometric press-ups and bench presses, loaded swings. Only one line in the preparatory entry; relevant to linemen's hand work.\n5. \"Avoid the term plyometric drills\" (PDF 281 / p.270): drills suggest \"militarism and authoritarianism rather than individual flow\". Minor, voice-of-Siff coaching stance.\n\n## Overall verdict\n\n**GO AFTER FIXES.** Required fixes:\n1. high-volume: change `contested` to `supported`; point to the brief-maximal-tension entry for the conflict.\n2. surface-footwear: change `contested` to `supported`; rewrite the tension as different aims.\n3. strength-endurance-ceiling: drop rowers from the conflict; correct the glycolytic inference using baseline line 117.\n4. brief-maximal-tension: add the line-76 DSI tag verbatim; fix the \"jumping from depth\" Example; add the novice-sequencing caveat and link.\n5. 475-jumps: add the novice-sequencing caveat and link; title \"tons\".\n6. depth-jump-height: correct the mRSI baseline statement (readiness marker, line 74); add the Ch4 p.222 locator for \"considerably lower\".\n7. volume-and-rest: move item 1 and \"1-15 min\" out of the depth-jump model (Fig 5.11 shows ground jumps); label voices (Siff vs Verkhoshansky 1977).\n8. five-phases: soften the EM-delay \"conflict\" to a wording difference.\n9. classification and momentum-intensity: change the voice to Siff for the powermetrics passages.\n10. Lewis: take the Inference out of the title or label it; mark line 77 as Inferred.\n11. injury-risk: quote the PDF 281 margin-cut words as printed (\"adequ[ate]\", \"running a[nd] jumping\").\n12. resisted-water: quote the \"No resistance methods should not use\" double negative as printed.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d9d6924f-c42c-40e8-b20d-e33f108b2ad9","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"8ee3b67b-43e4-49bb-a44b-27cad6f86518","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac44742970c7341bd","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffciLbCuAqo42RwJRHSP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014t3HCjGV97Eg7WMJztYgfj","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging; cat fixes_supertraining_ch5b_applied.md; echo; echo SCOPE; cat _scope_supertraining_ch5b.txt","description":"Read fix log and scope"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5560,"cache_read_input_tokens":50287,"output_tokens":258,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5560},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":258,"cache_read_input_tokens":50287,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5560,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5560,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_014t3HCjGV97Eg7WMJztYgfj":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging; cat fixes_supertraining_ch5b_applied.md; echo; echo SCOPE; cat _scope_supertraining_ch5b.txt","description":"Read fix log and scope"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffciLJqRkmBji12iUrfg","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"f06d4b36-33f4-4908-9248-9e04c857a9ce","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:26:21.662Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"f06d4b36-33f4-4908-9248-9e04c857a9ce","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"0b81aa46-3ac1-41a7-bee9-ff3aba05ec92","agentId":"ac44742970c7341bd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014t3HCjGV97Eg7WMJztYgfj","type":"tool_result","content":"# Fixes applied: Supertraining 5.2.4-5.2.5 (Ch5b), 30 entries\n\nFixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-10-03. Review: `staging/review_supertraining_ch5b_opus.md` (round 1, GO AFTER FIXES: 13 FIX rows, 12 numbered fixes in the verdict list, 5 missing ideas, a Wilson 1990/1991 citation question).\nSource checked: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`. I extracted the text of PDF idx 233 and 278-299 with PyMuPDF (`staging/_tmp/f5b_text.txt`). I rendered PDF 233, 279, 280, 281, 283, 284, 285, 286, 287, 288, 289, 292, 293, 296, 297 and 299 to `staging/_renders/f5b_p<N>.png`. I opened PDF 233 (\"considerably lower\"), 280 (safety passage), 281 (margin cut, EM-delay caption), 286 (item 1 \"1-15 minutes\", Fig 5.10), 296 (strength-endurance specificity, \"carry-over\" hyphenated across a line break), 284 (Fig 5.8, low vs high inertia), 285 (Fig 5.9, prescription list), 287 (Fig 5.11), 292-293 (the \"No resistance / methods should not\" page break) and 299 (novice experiments). Page key: PDF idx = printed + 11.\nScope: the 30 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch5b.txt`. I edited the 13 flagged entries plus `claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task.md` for the Wilson source note. No new entries, so the scope file is unchanged (30 lines). No renames. I did not edit `_book.md`, any 5a or 5c file, or the vault.\n\n| file | what changed |\n|---|---|\n| claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance.md | **Fix 1.** `confidence: contested` is now `supported`. The \"Internal conflict, recorded\" block is replaced by one sentence: the \"futile to rely predominantly\" passage is contradicted half a page later, but \"that conflict concerns explosive strength, not this entry's Nicol et al (1997) claim, and is recorded in [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]]\". When NOT now says the rating is `supported` because the claim rests on one undescribed citation and nothing in the book contradicts it. The baseline line 122 citation now carries its tag, \"Explicit\". |\n| heuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus.md | **Fix 2.** `contested` is now `supported`. The \"Internal tension\" block is replaced with a labelled Inference. It says these are different aims: firm surfaces for maximal rebounds (p. 273), sand for short-term skill gains (p. 278) and soft mats for the no-rebound landing experiment (p. 277). It quotes the text's \"as alluded to earlier ...\" (PDF 289) to show the sand finding sits beside the soft-surface warning on purpose. It reads \"suitably firm shock-absorbing surfaces\" (PDF 288) as one instruction. The swing and pogo-stick \"no data\" flag in When NOT is kept. |\n| heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md | **Fix 3.** The conflict is now the skater example only (80-85%, Mikhailov & Panov 1975). The rowers' \"50-80% of 1RM\" is recorded as \"at or under the ceiling, so it is not part of the conflict\". When NOT now reads \"without the 80-85% skater example beside it\". The glycolytic inference is rewritten: line 116 (Explicit) \"worst case scenario\", then \"staff do program it in the off-season\" (line 117, Explicit, interview 2026-09-28, Q19). \"Inference: to-fatigue strength-endurance sets fit the off-season, not in-season weeks.\" **Missing idea 2** is added as a **Specificity** bullet quoting PDF 296 / p.285, with a pointer to the Ch3.2.2 taxonomy entry, which already holds the same point from Ch3. |\n| heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md | **Fix 4.** (1) The line 76 citation now reads \"Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q21)\", followed by the baseline's tag verbatim: \"[contested; the ratio tracks IMTP strength; one controlled trial (Pleša 2024, basketball, split at 0.90) supported only the strength arm; no football data]\". (2) The Example now quotes the p.278 wording, \"the resistance is small and the working effect depends upon the reactive ability of the muscles\", and adds \"The passage names no drill for the second case\". \"jumping from depth\" is removed. (3) When NOT now opens with the novice caveat, scoped to the **within-workout** experiment on PDF 299 (\"There was no statistically significant difference ... first two groups\"; the depth-jump group gained most). It also notes the separate across-stage experiment on the same page (weights first, then depth jumps, was more effective) and the authors' hedge that \"the validity of this hypothesis can be established conclusively only by careful experimentation\". Related now links [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]]. |\n| claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers.md | **Fix 5.** The title now says \"93 Tons\" (the source's word), not \"Tonnes\". When NOT adds the novice caveat, scoped to the within-workout experiment (PDF 299 / p.288), with the authors' \"careful experimentation\" hedge. When to apply's sequencing Inference now ends \"(for novices, see When NOT to apply)\". Related now links the 5c novice entry. |\n| heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact.md | **Fix 6.** (1) The baseline sentence now reads: \"Eric's baseline: mRSI from daily CMJ testing 'was deliberately chosen as the daily athlete-readiness marker' (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 74, Explicit). Inference: a drop-jump RSI ... at stepped heights would be a separate test of the authors' ... rule; the baseline has no drop-jump test.\" (2) \"considerably lower\" is now the full quote \"using considerably lower jump heights with anyone who is unused to this type of training\" with the locator (Ch4.2.3, PDF p. 233 / printed p. 222, verified against the page image). |\n| heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md | **Fix 7.** (1) Depth-jump item 1 is moved out of Model 2 into its own paragraph, \"Depth-jump item 1, kept out of Model 2\". It quotes the \"complex of vertical jumps (Fig 5.11)\" sentence and the Fig 5.11 caption, and says the figure shows ground jumps with no box (verified against the image of PDF 287). It adds \"Inference: 'each exercise' most plausibly refers to that complex of ground jumps\". \"1-15 minutes\" is removed from the rest list in The conflict; it stays recorded as printed in the item 1 paragraph and in When NOT. (2) Voices are now labelled per item. Model 1's PDF 283, 293 and 294 statements are \"Siff's text\" (his term \"powermetrics\", PDF 279), and item 6 is from the authors' prescription list. In Model 2, item 4 is from the authors' prescription list. Items 2 and 3 are from the \"prolific experience\" recommendations, whose figures are credited to Verkhoshansky (1977); the list itself carries no citation. (3) Baseline: line 105 now carries its tag, \"Inferred (transcript + shared slides)\", and the baseline's \"Not claimed in the interview\". Line 186 is added, \"lifting and hard practice end 72 hours before a Saturday game\" (Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)), with an Inference tying maximal depth-jump sessions to that cut-off. `contested` stays. |\n| framework--five-phases-of-a-plyometric-action.md | **Fix 8.** The caption-versus-text paragraph now reads \"The caption words the EM delay differently ...\", quotes both, and ends \"Inference: both describe the electrical-to-mechanical interval.\" \"the book does not reconcile them\" is removed. In When NOT, \"Do not quote the EM delay definition without noting the caption and text differ\" is removed. |\n| framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function.md | **Fix 9 (classification).** Every \"the authors\" is now Siff. What it is opens \"The text (Siff's classification; it uses his term 'powermetrics', PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268) replaces ...\". Why it works, When to apply, When NOT and Example say \"Siff's\"; the count of \"authors\" in the file is 0. **Missing idea 1** is added: the full F = ma paragraph from PDF 284 / p.273 (verified against the image), ending \"Explosive, low inertia training targets involuntary, neuromuscular and central nervous processes more strongly than high inertia training, which has a greater effect on static strength development and muscle hypertrophy\". The entry notes that no study is given and that Ch1's [[principle--equal-force-different-loading-methods]] holds the general equal-force point but not this split. That link is added to Related. |\n| heuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height.md | **Fix 9 (momentum).** \"The authors propose\" is now \"The text (Siff, using his term 'powermetrics', PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268) proposes\". \"The authors give no numeric scale bands\" is now \"Siff gives no ...\", and \"The authors' reasoning\" in Why it works is now \"Siff's reasoning\". Items 1 and 4 stay \"the authors'\" (\"The authors' prescription list adds\"). **Missing idea 3** is added: the Fig 5.9 caption as printed, the text's \"Practical application ... to different muscle groups\" and \"select the necessary complex of exercises personally\" sentences, and a description read from the image and marked approximate (drop push-ups between blocks; pulley and weight-stack pushes, pulls, kicks and rows; incline-board work; a seated kick against suspended apparatus). It ends with a labelled Inference that the falling weight stack supplies item 1's \"weight and the height of its free-fall\". |\n| claim--lewis-formula-estimates-jump-power-from-bodymass-and-height.md | **Fix 10.** The title is now \"The Lewis Formula P = 21.693 x B x √H Tracks Jump Progress (Inference: It Is Weight Times Mean Free-Fall Speed, Not Take-Off Power)\". The line 77 pointer now carries the baseline's full tag: \"Inferred (protocol; author uncertain)\". |\n| claim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method.md | **Fix 11.** The quote is now \"adequ[ate] restoration regimes ... running a[nd] jumping\" (PDF pp. 280-281 / printed pp. 269-270). It notes that the right margin is cut in the scan on PDF 281 (verified against the image), so the bracketed letters are not printed. **Missing idea 5** is added as a bullet: \"it might be preferable to avoid use of the popular te[rm] plyometric *drills*, because drills tend to be associated with militarism and authoritarianism rather than wi[th] individual flow, sensitivity, flair and nuances of personal interpretation\" (PDF 281 / p.270, cut letters bracketed), labelled \"Voice: Siff\". |\n| heuristic--resisted-and-water-plyometrics.md | **Fix 12.** The sentence is now quoted as printed: \"No resistance methods should not use heavy weights or stiff elastic cords during depth jumps ...\", followed by \"(sic: the sentence runs across the page break as 'No resistance / methods should not use', PDF pp. 292-293 / printed pp. 281-282. Inference: the intended sense is 'should not')\". The locator line now says both pages were verified against the page images. |\n| claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task.md | **Wilson.** A \"Source note\" is added after the internal-conflict paragraph with the reference: Wilson, G.J., Elliott, B.C. & Wood, G.A. (1991), *Med Sci Sports Exerc* 23(3):364-370, with the abstract's design and its \"half-life of 0.85 s\". It says the abstract does not give the 4 s figure. It adds an Inference that 0.85 s matches the Fig 5.6 curve, so this is probably the bench-press study and the caption's 1991 is right. It says the 0.37 s Wilson (1991) result was not checked. `contested` is kept: the book itself calls the decay results an \"apparent contradiction\". |\n\n## Missing ideas: placement\n\n| # | idea | placement |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Low vs high inertia training effect (PDF 284) | Added to the classification entry (flagged; same page as Fig 5.8). Ch1 `principle--equal-force-different-loading-methods` has F = ma and \"different training effect\" but not the low-inertia/neural vs high-inertia/hypertrophy split, so the 5b passage is new. |\n| 2 | Strength-endurance specificity is weaker than speed's, carry-over greater (PDF 296) | Added to the ceiling entry (flagged; the sentence sits just before the ceiling quote). The Ch3.2.2 taxonomy entry already quotes the Ch3 version, and the new bullet links it. |\n| 3 | Fig 5.9 apparatus catalogue (PDF 285) | Added to the momentum-intensity entry (flagged; Fig 5.9 introduces the same prescription list that entry quotes). |\n| 4 | Upper-body transfer (PDF 288) | **Not added: already captured.** See Disagreements. |\n| 5 | Avoid the term \"drills\" (PDF 281) | Added to the injury-risk entry (flagged; the same paragraph, Siff's voice). |\n\nNo new entry was written, so `_scope_supertraining_ch5b.txt` is unchanged.\n\n## Wilson citation\n\nThe Zotero MCP (`mcp__zotero__search`) returned \"MCP tenant gate is not configured\", so the check went through the web. A web search found the paper. I then fetched the journal's own abstract page (journals.lww.com, redirecting to ovid.com, accession 00005768-199103000-00016). Confirmed: Wilson GJ, Elliott BC, Wood GA. The effect on performance of imposing a delay during a stretch-shorten cycle movement. Med Sci Sports Exerc 1991;23(3):364-370. Twelve experienced male weight lifters, bench press at 95% of maximum, decay fitted by a negative exponential with a half-life of 0.85 s. That matches the Fig 5.6 curve, so the caption's 1991 is probably right and the text's 1990 probably wrong. This is recorded in the entry as a source note, with the match labelled Inference. The abstract does not state the 4 s endpoint, and the entry says so.\n\n## Disagreements with the review\n\n1. **Missing idea 4 (upper-body transfer) is not missing.** `heuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps.md` already quotes the PDF 288 / p.277 sentence (medicine ball catching and throwing, plyometric press-ups and bench presses). The source gives that point in one sentence, and the entry gives it one line. No edit was made. Only \"loaded swings or other specialised apparatus\" is left out, and the Fig 5.9 addition now covers apparatus.\n2. **Fix 4(3) and fix 5: the review's wording \"order made no significant difference in novices\" is too broad.** PDF 299 holds three relevant findings. (a) Across the preparatory period, order \"has no real significance for novices\". (b) In an across-stage experiment, weights first, then depth jumps, \"was more effective\". (c) In the within-workout experiment, which is the one that matches \"resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions\", there was \"no statistically significant difference\". The authors then say they expect the effect in well-trained athletes, but that this needs \"careful experimentation\". The caveat I wrote names the within-workout experiment, mentions (b), and quotes the hedge, so it does not read as \"sequencing never matters\".\n3. **Fix 7(1): the reading is stated as Inference, not fact.** The source sentence is \"Good results are obtained with depth-jumps using a complex of vertical jumps (Fig 5.11). Each exercise is executed in sets of 10 ...\". Fig 5.11 shows ground jumps (confirmed on the image), but \"each exercise\" could grammatically take in the depth jumps. I moved item 1 out of Model 2 as asked, labelled the reading \"Inference\", and kept it out of the conflict count.\n4. **Fix 7(2): voice labels are per item, not per model.** Model 1 includes prescription item 6 and Model 2 includes prescription item 4. Both come from the PDF 285 list, which the review itself treats as \"the authors'\" (momentum row). So I labelled the Siff passages Siff, the prescription items the authors', and the PDF 286 list \"credited to 'prolific experience' ... whose figures are credited to Verkhoshansky (1977); the list itself carries no citation\". I did not call the list Verkhoshansky's.\n5. **Fix 10: the full baseline tag is used.** The review gives \"(Inferred, protocol)\". Line 77 prints \"**Inferred (protocol; author uncertain)**\", and the brief asks for the tag verbatim.\n6. **Beyond the review: baseline tags were added to every baseline citation in the edited files** (lines 74, 76, 105, 116, 117, 122, 186). Line 105 needed this most: it is Inferred and \"Not claimed in the interview\", and the entry called it \"the stated rule\" with no tag.\n7. **Optional items not applied.** The review's optional rows for GO entries (Dursenev wording, \"text layer\" to \"verified\" in three GO entries, the cyclic-endurance lead, the frequency entry's line 186 anchor, the non-impact locator) are outside the \"flagged files only\" rule and were not applied. Exception: the line 186 anchor was added to the volume-and-rest entry, which is flagged; the review offered it there as optional.\n\nEvery other review claim held up on the page: the PDF 281 margin cut (\"adequ\", \"running a\"), the \"No resistance / methods should not\" page break, the Fig 5.11 ground jumps, the absence of \"jumping from depth\" in the p.278 passage, the PDF 233 \"considerably lower\" locator, the baseline lines 74/76/77/116/117, Siff's \"powermetrics\" on PDF 279 and in the Fig 5.8 caption, and the rowers' 50-80% versus the skaters' 80-85% on PDF 297.\n\n## For the coordinator\n\n- Reciprocal links (5c, not edited by me): `case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments.md` should link back to [[claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers]] and [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]].\n- Confidence tallies change: `contested` 8 to 6, `supported` up by 2 (high-volume, surface-footwear).\n\n## Checks\n\nGrep proof, run from the book folder (one or more lines per fixed file):\n\n```\nclaim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance.md:9:confidence: supported\nclaim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance.md:20:that conflict concerns explosive strength, not this entry's Nicol et al (1997) claim\nheuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus.md:9:confidence: supported\nheuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus.md:22:Inference: these are different aims, not a contradiction\nheuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md:24:sits at or under the ceiling, so it is not part of the conflict\nheuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md:28:to-fatigue strength-endurance sets fit the off-season, not in-season weeks\nheuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md:19:expressed to a lesser degree than the specificity of speed\nheuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md:27:one controlled trial (Pleša 2024, basketball, split at 0.90) supported only the strength arm; no football data]\nheuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md:31:The passage names no drill for the second case\nheuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md:29:The resistance-first rule for combined sessions is not supported for novices\nclaim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers.md:14:93 Tons\nclaim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers.md:24:The resistance-first rule is untested for novices\nheuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact.md:26:the baseline has no drop-jump test\nheuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact.md:28:(Ch4.2.3, PDF p. 233 / printed p. 222\nheuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md:29:Depth-jump item 1, kept out of Model 2\nheuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md:18:are Siff's text\nheuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md:35:line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides)\nframework--five-phases-of-a-plyometric-action.md:25:Inference: both describe the electrical-to-mechanical interval\nframework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function.md:16:The text (Siff's classification; it uses his term \"powermetrics\"\nframework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function.md:24:Explosive, low inertia training targets involuntary\nheuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height.md:16:The text (Siff, using his term \"powermetrics\"\nheuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height.md:20:The prescription list is introduced by Fig 5.9\nclaim--lewis-formula-estimates-jump-power-from-bodymass-and-height.md:14:(Inference: It Is Weight Times Mean Free-Fall Speed, Not Take-Off Power)\nclaim--lewis-formula-estimates-jump-power-from-bodymass-and-height.md:20:Inferred (protocol; author uncertain)\nclaim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method.md:21:adequ[ate] restoration regimes\nclaim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method.md:21:running a[nd] jumping\nclaim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method.md:22:militarism and authoritarianism\nheuristic--resisted-and-water-plyometrics.md:18:\"No resistance methods should not use heavy weights\nclaim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task.md:27:Source note: Wilson, G.J., Elliott, B.C. & Wood, G.A. (1991)\n```\n\nOld phrases, count of scoped files still holding each (all 0):\n\n```\nInternal conflict, recorded:** the \"futile: 0\nInternal tension, recorded: 0\nMale rowers obtain good results: 0\nthe department deliberately does not program: 0\nversus jumping from depth: 0\n93 Tonnes: 0\nreads reactive ability through mRSI: 0\ncredited to \"prolific experience\").**: 0\nnoting the caption and text differ: 0\nThe authors replace: 0\nThe authors propose: 0\nComputes a Falling-Body Power: 0\n\"adequate restoration: 0\nrunning or jumping: 0\n- Resistance methods \"should not: 0\n\"reconcile\" in framework--five-phases-of-a-plyometric-action.md: 0\n```\n\nMechanical greps over all 30 scoped files (run under `/bin/bash` with `F=$(cat ../../../_scope_supertraining_ch5b.txt | tr -d \"\\r\")`; zsh does not word-split `$F`):\n\n```\n== em dashes not in section headers:\n(end)\n== em dashes in titles:\n(end)\n== review-history words:\n(end)\n== wrapped wikilinks:\n(end)\n== header count per file:\n  30 6\n== CR endings:\n       0\n```\n\nNew link targets exist in the book folder: `case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments`, `principle--equal-force-different-loading-methods`, `framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial`.\n\nLint, run from the Flint root:\n\n```\n$ ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 114, 115]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1\n```\n\nThe one issue is the known `_book.md` false positive (title and table dashes), outside my edits and outside scope.\n\nSCOPE\ncase-study--soviet-sport-specific-strength-endurance-prescriptions.md\nclaim--depth-landings-from-2-to-3-2-m-produce-forces-over-20-times-bodyweight.md\nclaim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers.md\nclaim--heavy-squats-and-light-fast-lifts-miss-the-eccentric-concentric-switch.md\nclaim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance.md\nclaim--lewis-formula-estimates-jump-power-from-bodymass-and-height.md\nclaim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method.md\nclaim--popular-plyometric-drills-work-through-other-mechanisms.md\nclaim--strength-endurance-hypertrophy-and-cyclic-endurance.md\nclaim--squat-1-5-to-2-bodymass-rule-applies-to-maximal-depth-jumps-only.md\nclaim--plyometric-skill-is-cognitive-and-state-dependent.md\nclaim--zatsiorsky-and-hansen-show-strength-endurance-training-specificity.md\nclaim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task.md\nframework--five-phases-of-a-plyometric-action.md\nframework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function.md\nframework--non-impact-plyometrics-ericson-and-martial-arts.md\nframework--shock-methods-to-break-training-stagnation.md\nheuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact.md\nheuristic--depth-jump-execution-cues-landing-gaze-breath.md\nheuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md\nheuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement.md\nheuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md\nheuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern.md\nheuristic--match-plyometric-drills-to-limb-symmetry-and-rotational-dominance.md\nheuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus.md\nheuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height.md\nheuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps.md\nheuristic--resisted-and-water-plyometrics.md\nheuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md\nheuristic--strength-endurance-under-demanding-sport-like-conditions.md","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"606ebe20-a8cc-4e34-a4d9-cc3d97704d85","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:26:21.847Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# Fixes applied: Supertraining 5.2.4-5.2.5 (Ch5b), 30 entries\n\nFixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-10-03. Review: `staging/review_supertraining_ch5b_opus.md` (round 1, GO AFTER FIXES: 13 FIX rows, 12 numbered fixes in the verdict list, 5 missing ideas, a Wilson 1990/1991 citation question).\nSource checked: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`. I extracted the text of PDF idx 233 and 278-299 with PyMuPDF (`staging/_tmp/f5b_text.txt`). I rendered PDF 233, 279, 280, 281, 283, 284, 285, 286, 287, 288, 289, 292, 293, 296, 297 and 299 to `staging/_renders/f5b_p<N>.png`. I opened PDF 233 (\"considerably lower\"), 280 (safety passage), 281 (margin cut, EM-delay caption), 286 (item 1 \"1-15 minutes\", Fig 5.10), 296 (strength-endurance specificity, \"carry-over\" hyphenated across a line break), 284 (Fig 5.8, low vs high inertia), 285 (Fig 5.9, prescription list), 287 (Fig 5.11), 292-293 (the \"No resistance / methods should not\" page break) and 299 (novice experiments). Page key: PDF idx = printed + 11.\nScope: the 30 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch5b.txt`. I edited the 13 flagged entries plus `claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task.md` for the Wilson source note. No new entries, so the scope file is unchanged (30 lines). No renames. I did not edit `_book.md`, any 5a or 5c file, or the vault.\n\n| file | what changed |\n|---|---|\n| claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance.md | **Fix 1.** `confidence: contested` is now `supported`. The \"Internal conflict, recorded\" block is replaced by one sentence: the \"futile to rely predominantly\" passage is contradicted half a page later, but \"that conflict concerns explosive strength, not this entry's Nicol et al (1997) claim, and is recorded in [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]]\". When NOT now says the rating is `supported` because the claim rests on one undescribed citation and nothing in the book contradicts it. The baseline line 122 citation now carries its tag, \"Explicit\". |\n| heuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus.md | **Fix 2.** `contested` is now `supported`. The \"Internal tension\" block is replaced with a labelled Inference. It says these are different aims: firm surfaces for maximal rebounds (p. 273), sand for short-term skill gains (p. 278) and soft mats for the no-rebound landing experiment (p. 277). It quotes the text's \"as alluded to earlier ...\" (PDF 289) to show the sand finding sits beside the soft-surface warning on purpose. It reads \"suitably firm shock-absorbing surfaces\" (PDF 288) as one instruction. The swing and pogo-stick \"no data\" flag in When NOT is kept. |\n| heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md | **Fix 3.** The conflict is now the skater example only (80-85%, Mikhailov & Panov 1975). The rowers' \"50-80% of 1RM\" is recorded as \"at or under the ceiling, so it is not part of the conflict\". When NOT now reads \"without the 80-85% skater example beside it\". The glycolytic inference is rewritten: line 116 (Explicit) \"worst case scenario\", then \"staff do program it in the off-season\" (line 117, Explicit, interview 2026-09-28, Q19). \"Inference: to-fatigue strength-endurance sets fit the off-season, not in-season weeks.\" **Missing idea 2** is added as a **Specificity** bullet quoting PDF 296 / p.285, with a pointer to the Ch3.2.2 taxonomy entry, which already holds the same point from Ch3. |\n| heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md | **Fix 4.** (1) The line 76 citation now reads \"Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q21)\", followed by the baseline's tag verbatim: \"[contested; the ratio tracks IMTP strength; one controlled trial (Pleša 2024, basketball, split at 0.90) supported only the strength arm; no football data]\". (2) The Example now quotes the p.278 wording, \"the resistance is small and the working effect depends upon the reactive ability of the muscles\", and adds \"The passage names no drill for the second case\". \"jumping from depth\" is removed. (3) When NOT now opens with the novice caveat, scoped to the **within-workout** experiment on PDF 299 (\"There was no statistically significant difference ... first two groups\"; the depth-jump group gained most). It also notes the separate across-stage experiment on the same page (weights first, then depth jumps, was more effective) and the authors' hedge that \"the validity of this hypothesis can be established conclusively only by careful experimentation\". Related now links [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]]. |\n| claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers.md | **Fix 5.** The title now says \"93 Tons\" (the source's word), not \"Tonnes\". When NOT adds the novice caveat, scoped to the within-workout experiment (PDF 299 / p.288), with the authors' \"careful experimentation\" hedge. When to apply's sequencing Inference now ends \"(for novices, see When NOT to apply)\". Related now links the 5c novice entry. |\n| heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact.md | **Fix 6.** (1) The baseline sentence now reads: \"Eric's baseline: mRSI from daily CMJ testing 'was deliberately chosen as the daily athlete-readiness marker' (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 74, Explicit). Inference: a drop-jump RSI ... at stepped heights would be a separate test of the authors' ... rule; the baseline has no drop-jump test.\" (2) \"considerably lower\" is now the full quote \"using considerably lower jump heights with anyone who is unused to this type of training\" with the locator (Ch4.2.3, PDF p. 233 / printed p. 222, verified against the page image). |\n| heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md | **Fix 7.** (1) Depth-jump item 1 is moved out of Model 2 into its own paragraph, \"Depth-jump item 1, kept out of Model 2\". It quotes the \"complex of vertical jumps (Fig 5.11)\" sentence and the Fig 5.11 caption, and says the figure shows ground jumps with no box (verified against the image of PDF 287). It adds \"Inference: 'each exercise' most plausibly refers to that complex of ground jumps\". \"1-15 minutes\" is removed from the rest list in The conflict; it stays recorded as printed in the item 1 paragraph and in When NOT. (2) Voices are now labelled per item. Model 1's PDF 283, 293 and 294 statements are \"Siff's text\" (his term \"powermetrics\", PDF 279), and item 6 is from the authors' prescription list. In Model 2, item 4 is from the authors' prescription list. Items 2 and 3 are from the \"prolific experience\" recommendations, whose figures are credited to Verkhoshansky (1977); the list itself carries no citation. (3) Baseline: line 105 now carries its tag, \"Inferred (transcript + shared slides)\", and the baseline's \"Not claimed in the interview\". Line 186 is added, \"lifting and hard practice end 72 hours before a Saturday game\" (Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)), with an Inference tying maximal depth-jump sessions to that cut-off. `contested` stays. |\n| framework--five-phases-of-a-plyometric-action.md | **Fix 8.** The caption-versus-text paragraph now reads \"The caption words the EM delay differently ...\", quotes both, and ends \"Inference: both describe the electrical-to-mechanical interval.\" \"the book does not reconcile them\" is removed. In When NOT, \"Do not quote the EM delay definition without noting the caption and text differ\" is removed. |\n| framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function.md | **Fix 9 (classification).** Every \"the authors\" is now Siff. What it is opens \"The text (Siff's classification; it uses his term 'powermetrics', PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268) replaces ...\". Why it works, When to apply, When NOT and Example say \"Siff's\"; the count of \"authors\" in the file is 0. **Missing idea 1** is added: the full F = ma paragraph from PDF 284 / p.273 (verified against the image), ending \"Explosive, low inertia training targets involuntary, neuromuscular and central nervous processes more strongly than high inertia training, which has a greater effect on static strength development and muscle hypertrophy\". The entry notes that no study is given and that Ch1's [[principle--equal-force-different-loading-methods]] holds the general equal-force point but not this split. That link is added to Related. |\n| heuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height.md | **Fix 9 (momentum).** \"The authors propose\" is now \"The text (Siff, using his term 'powermetrics', PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268) proposes\". \"The authors give no numeric scale bands\" is now \"Siff gives no ...\", and \"The authors' reasoning\" in Why it works is now \"Siff's reasoning\". Items 1 and 4 stay \"the authors'\" (\"The authors' prescription list adds\"). **Missing idea 3** is added: the Fig 5.9 caption as printed, the text's \"Practical application ... to different muscle groups\" and \"select the necessary complex of exercises personally\" sentences, and a description read from the image and marked approximate (drop push-ups between blocks; pulley and weight-stack pushes, pulls, kicks and rows; incline-board work; a seated kick against suspended apparatus). It ends with a labelled Inference that the falling weight stack supplies item 1's \"weight and the height of its free-fall\". |\n| claim--lewis-formula-estimates-jump-power-from-bodymass-and-height.md | **Fix 10.** The title is now \"The Lewis Formula P = 21.693 x B x √H Tracks Jump Progress (Inference: It Is Weight Times Mean Free-Fall Speed, Not Take-Off Power)\". The line 77 pointer now carries the baseline's full tag: \"Inferred (protocol; author uncertain)\". |\n| claim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method.md | **Fix 11.** The quote is now \"adequ[ate] restoration regimes ... running a[nd] jumping\" (PDF pp. 280-281 / printed pp. 269-270). It notes that the right margin is cut in the scan on PDF 281 (verified against the image), so the bracketed letters are not printed. **Missing idea 5** is added as a bullet: \"it might be preferable to avoid use of the popular te[rm] plyometric *drills*, because drills tend to be associated with militarism and authoritarianism rather than wi[th] individual flow, sensitivity, flair and nuances of personal interpretation\" (PDF 281 / p.270, cut letters bracketed), labelled \"Voice: Siff\". |\n| heuristic--resisted-and-water-plyometrics.md | **Fix 12.** The sentence is now quoted as printed: \"No resistance methods should not use heavy weights or stiff elastic cords during depth jumps ...\", followed by \"(sic: the sentence runs across the page break as 'No resistance / methods should not use', PDF pp. 292-293 / printed pp. 281-282. Inference: the intended sense is 'should not')\". The locator line now says both pages were verified against the page images. |\n| claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task.md | **Wilson.** A \"Source note\" is added after the internal-conflict paragraph with the reference: Wilson, G.J., Elliott, B.C. & Wood, G.A. (1991), *Med Sci Sports Exerc* 23(3):364-370, with the abstract's design and its \"half-life of 0.85 s\". It says the abstract does not give the 4 s figure. It adds an Inference that 0.85 s matches the Fig 5.6 curve, so this is probably the bench-press study and the caption's 1991 is right. It says the 0.37 s Wilson (1991) result was not checked. `contested` is kept: the book itself calls the decay results an \"apparent contradiction\". |\n\n## Missing ideas: placement\n\n| # | idea | placement |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Low vs high inertia training effect (PDF 284) | Added to the classification entry (flagged; same page as Fig 5.8). Ch1 `principle--equal-force-different-loading-methods` has F = ma and \"different training effect\" but not the low-inertia/neural vs high-inertia/hypertrophy split, so the 5b passage is new. |\n| 2 | Strength-endurance specificity is weaker than speed's, carry-over greater (PDF 296) | Added to the ceiling entry (flagged; the sentence sits just before the ceiling quote). The Ch3.2.2 taxonomy entry already quotes the Ch3 version, and the new bullet links it. |\n| 3 | Fig 5.9 apparatus catalogue (PDF 285) | Added to the momentum-intensity entry (flagged; Fig 5.9 introduces the same prescription list that entry quotes). |\n| 4 | Upper-body transfer (PDF 288) | **Not added: already captured.** See Disagreements. |\n| 5 | Avoid the term \"drills\" (PDF 281) | Added to the injury-risk entry (flagged; the same paragraph, Siff's voice). |\n\nNo new entry was written, so `_scope_supertraining_ch5b.txt` is unchanged.\n\n## Wilson citation\n\nThe Zotero MCP (`mcp__zotero__search`) returned \"MCP tenant gate is not configured\", so the check went through the web. A web search found the paper. I then fetched the journal's own abstract page (journals.lww.com, redirecting to ovid.com, accession 00005768-199103000-00016). Confirmed: Wilson GJ, Elliott BC, Wood GA. The effect on performance of imposing a delay during a stretch-shorten cycle movement. Med Sci Sports Exerc 1991;23(3):364-370. Twelve experienced male weight lifters, bench press at 95% of maximum, decay fitted by a negative exponential with a half-life of 0.85 s. That matches the Fig 5.6 curve, so the caption's 1991 is probably right and the text's 1990 probably wrong. This is recorded in the entry as a source note, with the match labelled Inference. The abstract does not state the 4 s endpoint, and the entry says so.\n\n## Disagreements with the review\n\n1. **Missing idea 4 (upper-body transfer) is not missing.** `heuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps.md` already quotes the PDF 288 / p.277 sentence (medicine ball catching and throwing, plyometric press-ups and bench presses). The source gives that point in one sentence, and the entry gives it one line. No edit was made. Only \"loaded swings or other specialised apparatus\" is left out, and the Fig 5.9 addition now covers apparatus.\n2. **Fix 4(3) and fix 5: the review's wording \"order made no significant difference in novices\" is too broad.** PDF 299 holds three relevant findings. (a) Across the preparatory period, order \"has no real significance for novices\". (b) In an across-stage experiment, weights first, then depth jumps, \"was more effective\". (c) In the within-workout experiment, which is the one that matches \"resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions\", there was \"no statistically significant difference\". The authors then say they expect the effect in well-trained athletes, but that this needs \"careful experimentation\". The caveat I wrote names the within-workout experiment, mentions (b), and quotes the hedge, so it does not read as \"sequencing never matters\".\n3. **Fix 7(1): the reading is stated as Inference, not fact.** The source sentence is \"Good results are obtained with depth-jumps using a complex of vertical jumps (Fig 5.11). Each exercise is executed in sets of 10 ...\". Fig 5.11 shows ground jumps (confirmed on the image), but \"each exercise\" could grammatically take in the depth jumps. I moved item 1 out of Model 2 as asked, labelled the reading \"Inference\", and kept it out of the conflict count.\n4. **Fix 7(2): voice labels are per item, not per model.** Model 1 includes prescription item 6 and Model 2 includes prescription item 4. Both come from the PDF 285 list, which the review itself treats as \"the authors'\" (momentum row). So I labelled the Siff passages Siff, the prescription items the authors', and the PDF 286 list \"credited to 'prolific experience' ... whose figures are credited to Verkhoshansky (1977); the list itself carries no citation\". I did not call the list Verkhoshansky's.\n5. **Fix 10: the full baseline tag is used.** The review gives \"(Inferred, protocol)\". Line 77 prints \"**Inferred (protocol; author uncertain)**\", and the brief asks for the tag verbatim.\n6. **Beyond the review: baseline tags were added to every baseline citation in the edited files** (lines 74, 76, 105, 116, 117, 122, 186). Line 105 needed this most: it is Inferred and \"Not claimed in the interview\", and the entry called it \"the stated rule\" with no tag.\n7. **Optional items not applied.** The review's optional rows for GO entries (Dursenev wording, \"text layer\" to \"verified\" in three GO entries, the cyclic-endurance lead, the frequency entry's line 186 anchor, the non-impact locator) are outside the \"flagged files only\" rule and were not applied. Exception: the line 186 anchor was added to the volume-and-rest entry, which is flagged; the review offered it there as optional.\n\nEvery other review claim held up on the page: the PDF 281 margin cut (\"adequ\", \"running a\"), the \"No resistance / methods should not\" page break, the Fig 5.11 ground jumps, the absence of \"jumping from depth\" in the p.278 passage, the PDF 233 \"considerably lower\" locator, the baseline lines 74/76/77/116/117, Siff's \"powermetrics\" on PDF 279 and in the Fig 5.8 caption, and the rowers' 50-80% versus the skaters' 80-85% on PDF 297.\n\n## For the coordinator\n\n- Reciprocal links (5c, not edited by me): `case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments.md` should link back to [[claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers]] and [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]].\n- Confidence tallies change: `contested` 8 to 6, `supported` up by 2 (high-volume, surface-footwear).\n\n## Checks\n\nGrep proof, run from the book folder (one or more lines per fixed file):\n\n```\nclaim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance.md:9:confidence: supported\nclaim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance.md:20:that conflict concerns explosive strength, not this entry's Nicol et al (1997) claim\nheuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus.md:9:confidence: supported\nheuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus.md:22:Inference: these are different aims, not a contradiction\nheuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md:24:sits at or under the ceiling, so it is not part of the conflict\nheuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md:28:to-fatigue strength-endurance sets fit the off-season, not in-season weeks\nheuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md:19:expressed to a lesser degree than the specificity of speed\nheuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md:27:one controlled trial (Pleša 2024, basketball, split at 0.90) supported only the strength arm; no football data]\nheuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md:31:The passage names no drill for the second case\nheuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md:29:The resistance-first rule for combined sessions is not supported for novices\nclaim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers.md:14:93 Tons\nclaim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers.md:24:The resistance-first rule is untested for novices\nheuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact.md:26:the baseline has no drop-jump test\nheuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact.md:28:(Ch4.2.3, PDF p. 233 / printed p. 222\nheuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md:29:Depth-jump item 1, kept out of Model 2\nheuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md:18:are Siff's text\nheuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md:35:line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides)\nframework--five-phases-of-a-plyometric-action.md:25:Inference: both describe the electrical-to-mechanical interval\nframework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function.md:16:The text (Siff's classification; it uses his term \"powermetrics\"\nframework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function.md:24:Explosive, low inertia training targets involuntary\nheuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height.md:16:The text (Siff, using his term \"powermetrics\"\nheuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height.md:20:The prescription list is introduced by Fig 5.9\nclaim--