{"parentUuid":"d63e34fa-abf2-437d-a9fc-151ef1431230","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac44742970c7341bd","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffciXLrrENjNQz9k56fZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CXz9bukWj4sBcxrX6ErwLt","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint; for n in 74 75 76 77 96 101 104 105 116 117 122 186; do echo \"--- L$n\"; sed -n \"${n}p\" docs/method-baseline.md; done; echo; grep -n -i \"baseline\\|tag\\|verbatim\" docs/ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md | head -40","description":"Show baseline lines and brief rules"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19686,"cache_read_input_tokens":55847,"output_tokens":225,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":19686},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":225,"cache_read_input_tokens":55847,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19686,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":19686,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01CXz9bukWj4sBcxrX6ErwLt":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint; for n in 74 75 76 77 96 101 104 105 116 117 122 186; do echo \"--- L$n\"; sed -n \"${n}p\" docs/method-baseline.md; done; echo; grep -n -i \"baseline\\|tag\\|verbatim\" docs/ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md | head -40","description":"Show baseline lines and brief rules"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffciX41X1QMffGvbtD8Y","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"4fea193a-3cf1-4bd2-8147-706470d97aa6","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:26:24.243Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"4fea193a-3cf1-4bd2-8147-706470d97aa6","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"0b81aa46-3ac1-41a7-bee9-ff3aba05ec92","agentId":"ac44742970c7341bd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CXz9bukWj4sBcxrX6ErwLt","type":"tool_result","content":"--- L74\n- Modified reactive strength index (mRSI) from daily CMJ testing was deliberately chosen as the daily athlete-readiness marker in place of subjective wellness questionnaires, because it is \"objective, consistent, and actionable in a way the wellness surveys never quite were.\" **Explicit** [2026-02-19-sportsmith-6otb-er.md]\n--- L75\n- The mRSI decision read happens on Wednesdays, against individualized thresholds, not a single cut-off or position-group thresholds. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q2): \"Wednesdays, the thresholds should be individualized.\" Individualized against: Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q2a): \"Percent change from baseline or a rolling average. We've used standard deviations and SWC to diagnose signal versus noise.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q2, Q2a)** Inference: \"should be\" may describe the intended rule rather than a fully running one; no numeric threshold was given. See Tension 12 (daily testing versus a Wednesday read), resolved 2026-10-02: daily jumps build the baseline and SWC, Wednesday is the decision read.\n--- L76\n- The dynamic strength index (DSI: CMJ against isometric mid-thigh pull peak force) chooses between plyometric and max-strength emphasis. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q21): \"we incorporate the countermovement jump and the isometric mid-thigh pull to get a dynamic strength index, to determine whether we need more plyometric training or more max-strength training.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q21)** This is not the SJ-CMJ gap the question asked about. Evidence check done 2026-09-29 (`docs/synthesis/_work/dsi-evidence-check.md`): cite it as Eric's practice, tagged [contested; the ratio tracks IMTP strength; one controlled trial (Pleša 2024, basketball, split at 0.90) supported only the strength arm; no football data]. See Tension 17.\n--- L77\n- The standard CMJ family run on ForceDecks: hands-free squat, squat jump (quarter-squat start, 3s hold), countermovement jump (x3), hop test / repeated CMJ (10 reps total, n=5 pairs), and single-leg CMJ (x3 per leg) — each with a scripted weigh-in, stabilization, and rep protocol. **Inferred (protocol; author uncertain)** [revised-force-plate-testing-v3.md]\n--- L96\n- Speed is taught as the quality with the shortest ground-contact-time demand (~0.08s male, ~0.10s female at max velocity) and is described as harder to train and more genetically constrained than strength or power, but as the biggest separator between good and great athletes at the elite level. **Inferred (transcript)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.11]\n--- L101\n- The Vermeil hierarchy of athletic development (work capacity, then strength, then power, then speed, then sport skill, each level a required foundation for the next) is repeatedly described as the department's programming \"compass.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**; was Inferred (transcript + shared course material) [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.9-10; week1-lecture.md pp.23,26]\n--- L104\n- Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints); single-joint assistance work is kept to a small share of total volume. **Inferred (transcript + slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.13; week1-lecture.md p.31]\n--- L105\n- Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work. In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule. **Inferred (transcript + shared slides)** [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md pp.20-21; hhpr-5355-class-8-2026.md p.21] Not claimed in the interview. The in-season dose itself is unknown: Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q18): \"I don't have the specific sets and reps.\"\n--- L116\n- For football specifically, the stated priority stack is: Goal 1, alactic and aerobic capacity/power for fall-camp demands; Goal 2, the same two systems for game demands; glycolytic conditioning is treated as a \"worst case scenario\" to be absorbed incidentally through camp and practice rather than programmed as a primary target. **Explicit** [class-4-2026.md pp.34, 39]; claimed again in the interview, **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)**\n--- L117\n- Glycolytic conditioning is still not a primary target in season, but staff do program it in the off-season. Eric (interview 2026-09-28, Q19): \"It does hold up, but our staff does program it in the off-season.\" **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q19)**\n--- L122\n- \"Everything we do is some form of conditioning\": lifting, agility work, and skill drills each load a specific energy system (power vs. capacity, alactic/glycolytic/aerobic) and should be classified and counted as conditioning, not treated as separate from it. **Explicit** [class-4-2026.md p.31, tables pp.35-42]\n--- L186\n- The in-season game week: Sunday off; Monday heavy lift; Tuesday and Wednesday heavy practice, with a lighter lift Wednesday; lifting and hard practice end 72 hours before a Saturday game. **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)**\n\n14:- **Eric's views** (from `docs/method-baseline.md` or his reviews) are prefixed \"Eric's baseline:\" or \"Eric's review:\".\n32:- **No review history in entries.** \"Prior version\", \"corrected from a NO-GO finding\" and similar belong in `staging/fixes_*_applied.md`, never in the entry body.\n40:- OCR sources (`staging/ocr/*.md`): pages listed as low_confidence in the header must be checked against the page image before any number from them is cited. Use the Read tool on the PDF page, or render it.\n50: