brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size; do echo \"=========== $f\"; cat -n $f.md; done","description":"Read first four edited entries"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffciqEtdPetpQgN8FbWV","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"fa7fb6cb-2bc3-4811-8aa1-d62e1014371f","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:26:29.222Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"fa7fb6cb-2bc3-4811-8aa1-d62e1014371f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"0b81aa46-3ac1-41a7-bee9-ff3aba05ec92","agentId":"ac44742970c7341bd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ESQ4sf8Jorwrxmr1kCy5Mr","type":"tool_result","content":"=========== claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: claim\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n     7\ttopic: plyometric volume and fatigue resistance\n     8\tcategory: [plyometrics, volume, fatigue, motor-control]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Many Sets and Reps of Plyometrics Do Not Build Fatigue Resistance for Repeated Rebounding; Movement Efficiency Does (Nicol et al, 1997)\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — Stated twice in 5.2.4:\n    17\t- \"Many repetitions of plyometric drills frequently are prescribed for some sports because of assumptions that this sort of training will improve fatigue resistance in repeated actions such as rebounding in basketball, yet research has shown this to be untrue (Nicol et al, 1997)\" (PDF p. 280 / printed p. 269, verified against the page image).\n    18\t- \"The use of many repetitions and sets of plyometric drills in an attempt to facilitate adaptation to cope with exhaustive bouts of stretch-shortening activities such as rebounding in basketball is not supported by research (Nicol et al, 1997). Instead any improvement in such situations is better mediated by the use of training to enhance movement efficiency. This is a very important point which concerns all explosive and ballistic activities: it is futile to rely predominantly on plyometric drills to enhance sport specific explosive strength and power, because efficiency of motor control is fundamental to all skilled sporting performance\" (PDF pp. 288-289 / printed pp. 277-278, verified against the page images).\n    19\t\n    20\tThe \"futile to rely predominantly\" sentence sits in a passage the book contradicts half a page later on whether plyometrics should be the main method; that conflict concerns explosive strength, not this entry's Nicol et al (1997) claim, and is recorded in [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]].\n    21\t\n    22\t**Why it works** — The text's stated reason is \"efficiency of motor control is fundamental to all skilled sporting performance.\" It does not describe the Nicol et al (1997) study design or measures, and the paper was not checked for this entry, so what it measured is unverified here.\n    23\t\n    24\t**When to apply** — When someone proposes high-rep plyometric circuits to make athletes \"fresher\" in the fourth quarter. Eric's baseline: \"Everything we do is some form of conditioning\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 122, Explicit). Inference: the text's alternative for repeat-effort tolerance is better movement efficiency in the sport skill itself, not more drill volume; the book also allows \"much shorter intervals with submaximal plyometrics to develop explosive-strength endurance\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, item 6), so submaximal plyometric endurance work is not ruled out.\n    25\t\n    26\t**When NOT to apply** — Not evidence against plyometrics for explosive strength; the same section calls the shock method's training effect \"very large.\" The claim rests on one citation the book does not describe, so it is rated `supported`, not higher; nothing in the book contradicts it.\n    27\t\n    28\t**Example** — The text's own: basketball rebounding as the repeated stretch-shortening task that high plyometric volume was assumed, wrongly, to prepare (PDF p. 280 / printed p. 269).\n    29\t\n    30\t**Related** — [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]; [[claim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method]]; [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]]; [[claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more]]\n=========== heuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: heuristic\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n     7\ttopic: plyometric surfaces and apparatus\n     8\tcategory: [plyometrics, surfaces, footwear, equipment]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Plyometric Surfaces and Footwear: Not Too Soft for Rebounds, Yet Sand and Soft Mats Have Their Uses; Swings and Pogo Sticks as Cheap Powermetric Tools\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — Surface and apparatus statements in 5.2.4:\n    17\t- \"Footwear or the floor covering should not be very soft or excessively shock absorbent, since this may impair ankle stability, diminish the storage of elastic energy in the SEC (e.g. tendons) and delay the triggering of the positive supportive reaction of the foot (see 6.1.2) when contacting the floor\" (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273, verified against the page image).\n    18\t- The surface \"can also have a significant effect on the outcome of the exercise, as alluded to earlier in comments made about the alterations in motor skill and proprioceptive sensitivity associated with the use of soft-soled shoes and very resilient jumping surfaces. A little known finding is that plyometric and jumping drills performed according to carefully planned progressive patterns in sand can significantly enhance jumping skills in a short period, even as short as 5 days in duration (Montagni & Cardinale, 1996)\" (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278, verified against the page image).\n    19\t- For depth landings: Dursenev and Raevsky used \"soft gymnastic mats\", and the authors advise landing \"on suitably firm shock-absorbing surfaces\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277, verified against the page image).\n    20\t- Simple apparatus: a children's swing near a wall, where the feet push off the wall after swinging from \"a suitable height\", progressed by raising the backswing; longer ropes give a slower repetition rate; uprights in front allow upper-body rebounds. The \"Pogo-Stick\", with intensity \"determined by the height of rebound\", and boxers' skipping are also offered as \"powermetric\" tools (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278, verified against the page image).\n    21\t\n    22\tInference: these are different aims, not a contradiction: firm, not very soft surfaces for maximal rebounds (p. 273); sand for short-term jumping-skill gains (p. 278); soft mats for the no-rebound depth-landing experiment (p. 277). The text places the sand finding beside the soft-surface warning on purpose (\"as alluded to earlier ... soft-soled shoes and very resilient jumping surfaces\", PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278), and \"suitably firm shock-absorbing surfaces\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277) is one instruction, not two.\n    23\t\n    24\t**Why it works** — The p. 273 reasons are stated (ankle stability, SEC elastic storage, the foot's positive supportive reaction). No mechanism is given for the sand result. The swing physics is stated by the authors as rope length setting the oscillation time.\n    25\t\n    26\t**When to apply** — Choosing where to run maximal rebound work: a firm, not very soft surface and non-cushioned footwear, per p. 273. Inference: sand is an option for early-phase or high-volume submaximal jumping, consistent with the p. 278 finding, but not for maximal depth jumps.\n    27\t\n    28\t**When NOT to apply** — The Montagni & Cardinale (1996) result is cited without design or measures; \"jumping skills\" is not defined. The swing and pogo stick suggestions are the text's ideas, with no data.\n    29\t\n    30\t**Example** — The text's own: beach volleyball and beach handball as examples of the value of sand training (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278).\n    31\t\n    32\t**Related** — [[heuristic--depth-jump-execution-cues-landing-gaze-breath]]; [[claim--depth-landings-from-2-to-3-2-m-produce-forces-over-20-times-bodyweight]]; [[heuristic--resisted-and-water-plyometrics]]\n=========== heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: heuristic\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"5.2.5\"\n     7\ttopic: strength-endurance loading\n     8\tcategory: [strength-endurance, programming, load, tempo]\n     9\tconfidence: contested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Strength-Endurance: 25-50% 1RM at 60-120 Reps per Minute, Ideally to Fatigue; Above 75-80% 1RM Trains Strength Instead (but the Book Also Prescribes 80-85%)\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — Section 5.2.5 (PDF pp. 296-298 / printed pp. 285-287; pp. 296-298 verified against the page images):\n    17\t- **Eight determinants.** Training for strength-endurance \"is determined predominantly by: 1. The magnitude of the load 2. The tempo of movement 3. The duration of each movement and each phase of the movements 4. The rest interval between training sessions 5. The length of the training period 6. The initial levels of strength-endurance of the athlete 7. The kinesiological structure of the movement 8. The relative involvement of static and dynamic strength-endurance\" (PDF p. 296 / printed p. 285).\n    18\t- **Basic prescription.** \"Repetition work with weights 25-50% of 1RM at a moderate tempo (from 60-120 repetitions/minute) is used for developing strength-endurance. The effectiveness ... will be higher if the work is executed to complete fatigue, although work of shorter duration (60% of the maximal work capacity time) also will yield satisfactory results (Agolinsky, 1953; Korobkov, 1953; Monogarov, 1958; Diachkov, 1961)\" (same page).\n    19\t- **Specificity.** \"Strength-endurance, like the other characteristics of muscular activity, is specific. However, the specificity of strength-endurance is expressed to a lesser degree than the specificity of speed, for example, while its carry-over from one activity to another is greater\" (same page; the Ch3.2.2 entry [[framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial]] quotes the same point from Ch3).\n    20\t- **Ceiling.** \"When the activity involves repetitively overcoming significant resistance (exceeding 75-80% of 1RM), one cannot enhance endurance, since one becomes constrained to developing strength predominantly (Zatsiorsky, 1966)\" (same page).\n    21\t- **Progression.** \"It is necessary to increase the amount of weight and the number of repetitions as strength-endurance increases\" (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286).\n    22\t- **Summary rule.** \"multiple repetition exercises with various weights should be considered the fundamental method for developing strength-endurance, with the load being determined by the dynamics of the given exercise. In cases requiring intense effort, an optimally heavy weight should be used together with light weights or with exercises simulating the given sport activity. If the special exercise is associated with the prolonged production of moderate force, then it is useful to work with light weights in repeated sets to the point of fatigue or failure\" (PDF p. 298 / printed p. 287).\n    23\t\n    24\t**Internal conflict, recorded:** the 75-80% ceiling (PDF p. 296 / printed p. 285) conflicts with the skater example a page later: \"Exercises with heavy weights (80-85% of 1RM) ... are recommended for developing strength-endurance in skaters\", 4-12 reps per set to complete fatigue with 2-4 min rest (Mikhailov & Panov, 1975) (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286). The rowers' \"50-80% of 1RM\" (Ulrich et al, 1966, same page) sits at or under the ceiling, so it is not part of the conflict. The summary rule on p. 287 (\"an optimally heavy weight ... together with light weights\" where intense effort is needed) sits closer to the skater example than to the ceiling. The book does not reconcile them.\n    25\t\n    26\t**Why it works** — The ceiling's stated reason: above 75-80% the work is \"constrained to developing strength predominantly.\" The load-to-fatigue advantage is reported from the cited Soviet studies, without a stated mechanism.\n    27\t\n    28\t**When to apply** — Inference: for football, where strength-endurance means repeated forceful efforts (blocking, pushing a sled), the summary rule fits better than the 25-50% band: a heavy-enough load mixed with lighter or sport-simulating work. The 60-120 reps per minute tempo describes cyclic-sport endurance work more than gridiron work. Eric's baseline: for football the priority stack is alactic and aerobic capacity and power, with glycolytic conditioning treated as a \"worst case scenario\" absorbed through camp and practice (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 116, Explicit), but \"staff do program it in the off-season\" (line 117, Explicit, interview 2026-09-28, Q19). Inference: to-fatigue strength-endurance sets fit the off-season, not in-season weeks.\n    29\t\n    30\t**When NOT to apply** — Do not quote the 75-80% ceiling as a firm rule without the 80-85% skater example beside it. The determinants list has no weightings. \"60% of the maximal work capacity time\" is not defined further (Inference: 60% of the time to failure is the plain reading).\n    31\t\n    32\t**Example** — The authors' own: 25-50% 1RM at 60-120 reps per minute to fatigue, or to 60% of the maximal work time (PDF p. 296 / printed p. 285).\n    33\t\n    34\t**Related** — [[framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial]] (Ch3.2.2, the dynamic/static and absolute/partial distinctions this section restates); [[claim--zatsiorsky-and-hansen-show-strength-endurance-training-specificity]]; [[case-study--soviet-sport-specific-strength-endurance-prescriptions]]; [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]] (Ch1 Table 1.1, endurance at 40-60% 1RM)\n=========== heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: heuristic\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n     7\ttopic: choosing between heavy and plyometric methods\n     8\tcategory: [explosive-strength, plyometrics, method-selection, sequencing]\n     9\tconfidence: contested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Large Resistance Calls for the Brief Maximal Tension Method, Small Resistance and Reactive Demand Call for Plyometrics; the Book Disagrees With Itself on Whether Plyometrics Should Be the Main Method\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — The authors' method-selection rules (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278, verified against the page image unless marked):\n    17\t- \"In those cases where explosive force is associated with the overcoming of large resistance, preference should be given to the brief maximal tension method, as used commonly in Olympic weightlifting. In situations where the resistance is small and the working effect depends upon the reactive ability of the muscles, plyometric methods should be given preference.\"\n    18\t- Staging: \"Although it should form an integral part of the training programme, plyometrics should not be combined in the beginning stages with resistance exercises such as the brief maximal tension method.\" \"In subsequent stages, plyometrics should be the main method in the training of highly qualified athletes for developing explosive strength and reactive ability.\"\n    19\t- Concentric explosive work: \"exercises with maximum effort and mixed regimes using weights equal to 40% of 1RM, or 30% of 1RM (Verkhoshansky, 1963; Papysheva, 1966). Explosive-isometric tension up to 80% of maximum should be used in conjunction with this routine.\"\n    20\t- In combined sessions, resistance work goes first: combined programmes beat plyometrics alone \"provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277).\n    21\t- Testing decides: \"To ascertain whether an athlete needs plyometric or heavy resistance training depends on measuring the *strength deficit* ... Without such testing, prescription of plyometric training tends to remain hit-and-miss\" (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273; repeated at PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282 in a grammatically broken sentence).\n    22\t\n    23\t**Internal conflict, recorded:** \"plyometrics should be the main method in the training of highly qualified athletes\" (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278) against, half a page earlier, \"it is futile to rely predominantly on plyometric drills to enhance sport specific explosive strength and power, because efficiency of motor control is fundamental to all skilled sporting performance\" (PDF pp. 288-289 / printed pp. 277-278). The second follows a Nicol et al (1997) citation. Inference: the first reads like the original shock-method programme and the second like a later addition, but the book marks neither passage by author. The book does not reconcile them.\n    24\t\n    25\t**Why it works** — The authors tie each method to the force demand: heavy brief tension for large resistance, reactive shock loading for small resistance. The strength deficit decides which an individual needs (Ch1; see [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]], which records that the book also states the deficit rule two ways).\n    26\t\n    27\t**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: \"we incorporate the countermovement jump and the isometric mid-thigh pull to get a dynamic strength index, to determine whether we need more plyometric training or more max-strength training\" (Eric, interview 2026-09-28, Q21; `docs/method-baseline.md` line 76, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q21)), with the baseline's own tag on that line: [contested; the ratio tracks IMTP strength; one controlled trial (Pleša 2024, basketball, split at 0.90) supported only the strength arm; no football data]. Inference: DSI plays the role the authors give the strength deficit (both compare a fast expression of force with a maximal one to choose plyometric versus heavy work), but they are different measures; Eric's baseline Tension 17 records that the DSI cutoffs were never validated and that IMTP peak force drives most of the ratio (line 261). Inference: for linemen, whose sport force is against large resistance, the authors' rule favours brief maximal tension; for skill positions, whose force is against bodymass, it favours plyometrics.\n    28\t\n    29\t**When NOT to apply** — The resistance-first rule for combined sessions is not supported for novices: in a within-workout experiment on novices (strength then speed-strength work versus the reverse, pauses lengthened slightly), \"There was no statistically significant difference in speed-strength of the subjects in the first two groups\", and a depth-jump group gained most (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288; see [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]]). The same page reports a separate across-stage experiment in which weights first, then depth jumps, was more effective, and the authors expect the within-session effect to hold for well-trained athletes, but \"the validity of this hypothesis can be established conclusively only by careful experimentation.\" Do not cite Supertraining as saying plyometrics should be the main method without the \"futile to rely predominantly\" sentence, or the reverse. \"Beginning stages\" and \"highly qualified\" are not defined. The 40% and 30% of 1RM loads and the 80% explosive-isometric tension are given without sets, reps or rest.\n    30\t\n    31\t**Example** — The authors' own contrast: Olympic weightlifting (large resistance, brief maximal tension) versus situations where \"the resistance is small and the working effect depends upon the reactive ability of the muscles\" (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278). The passage names no drill for the second case.\n    32\t\n    33\t**Related** — [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]]; [[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]; [[claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance]]; [[claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers]]; [[claim--heavy-squats-and-light-fast-lifts-miss-the-eccentric-concentric-switch]]; [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]] (Ch5.3, novice sequencing)","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"9529a234-aa32-4743-be88-f7cf0f6e1692","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:26:29.448Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"=========== claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: claim\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n     7\ttopic: plyometric volume and fatigue resistance\n     8\tcategory: [plyometrics, volume, fatigue, motor-control]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Many Sets and Reps of Plyometrics Do Not Build Fatigue Resistance for Repeated Rebounding; Movement Efficiency Does (Nicol et al, 1997)\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — Stated twice in 5.2.4:\n    17\t- \"Many repetitions of plyometric drills frequently are prescribed for some sports because of assumptions that this sort of training will improve fatigue resistance in repeated actions such as rebounding in basketball, yet research has shown this to be untrue (Nicol et al, 1997)\" (PDF p. 280 / printed p. 269, verified against the page image).\n    18\t- \"The use of many repetitions and sets of plyometric drills in an attempt to facilitate adaptation to cope with exhaustive bouts of stretch-shortening activities such as rebounding in basketball is not supported by research (Nicol et al, 1997). Instead any improvement in such situations is better mediated by the use of training to enhance movement efficiency. This is a very important point which concerns all explosive and ballistic activities: it is futile to rely predominantly on plyometric drills to enhance sport specific explosive strength and power, because efficiency of motor control is fundamental to all skilled sporting performance\" (PDF pp. 288-289 / printed pp. 277-278, verified against the page images).\n    19\t\n    20\tThe \"futile to rely predominantly\" sentence sits in a passage the book contradicts half a page later on whether plyometrics should be the main method; that conflict concerns explosive strength, not this entry's Nicol et al (1997) claim, and is recorded in [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]].\n    21\t\n    22\t**Why it works** — The text's stated reason is \"efficiency of motor control is fundamental to all skilled sporting performance.\" It does not describe the Nicol et al (1997) study design or measures, and the paper was not checked for this entry, so what it measured is unverified here.\n    23\t\n    24\t**When to apply** — When someone proposes high-rep plyometric circuits to make athletes \"fresher\" in the fourth quarter. Eric's baseline: \"Everything we do is some form of conditioning\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 122, Explicit). Inference: the text's alternative for repeat-effort tolerance is better movement efficiency in the sport skill itself, not more drill volume; the book also allows \"much shorter intervals with submaximal plyometrics to develop explosive-strength endurance\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, item 6), so submaximal plyometric endurance work is not ruled out.\n    25\t\n    26\t**When NOT to apply** — Not evidence against plyometrics for explosive strength; the same section calls the shock method's training effect \"very large.\" The claim rests on one citation the book does not describe, so it is rated `supported`, not higher; nothing in the book contradicts it.\n    27\t\n    28\t**Example** — The text's own: basketball rebounding as the repeated stretch-shortening task that high plyometric volume was assumed, wrongly, to prepare (PDF p. 280 / printed p. 269).\n    29\t\n    30\t**Related** — [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]; [[claim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method]]; [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]]; [[claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more]]\n=========== heuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: heuristic\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n     7\ttopic: plyometric surfaces and apparatus\n     8\tcategory: [plyometrics, surfaces, footwear, equipment]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Plyometric Surfaces and Footwear: Not Too Soft for Rebounds, Yet Sand and Soft Mats Have Their Uses; Swings and Pogo Sticks as Cheap Powermetric Tools\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — Surface and apparatus statements in 5.2.4:\n    17\t- \"Footwear or the floor covering should not be very soft or excessively shock absorbent, since this may impair ankle stability, diminish the storage of elastic energy in the SEC (e.g. tendons) and delay the triggering of the positive supportive reaction of the foot (see 6.1.2) when contacting the floor\" (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273, verified against the page image).\n    18\t- The surface \"can also have a significant effect on the outcome of the exercise, as alluded to earlier in comments made about the alterations in motor skill and proprioceptive sensitivity associated with the use of soft-soled shoes and very resilient jumping surfaces. A little known finding is that plyometric and jumping drills performed according to carefully planned progressive patterns in sand can significantly enhance jumping skills in a short period, even as short as 5 days in duration (Montagni & Cardinale, 1996)\" (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278, verified against the page image).\n    19\t- For depth landings: Dursenev and Raevsky used \"soft gymnastic mats\", and the authors advise landing \"on suitably firm shock-absorbing surfaces\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277, verified against the page image).\n    20\t- Simple apparatus: a children's swing near a wall, where the feet push off the wall after swinging from \"a suitable height\", progressed by raising the backswing; longer ropes give a slower repetition rate; uprights in front allow upper-body rebounds. The \"Pogo-Stick\", with intensity \"determined by the height of rebound\", and boxers' skipping are also offered as \"powermetric\" tools (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278, verified against the page image).\n    21\t\n    22\tInference: these are different aims, not a contradiction: firm, not very soft surfaces for maximal rebounds (p. 273); sand for short-term jumping-skill gains (p. 278); soft mats for the no-rebound depth-landing experiment (p. 277). The text places the sand finding beside the soft-surface warning on purpose (\"as alluded to earlier ... soft-soled shoes and very resilient jumping surfaces\", PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278), and \"suitably firm shock-absorbing surfaces\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277) is one instruction, not two.\n    23\t\n    24\t**Why it works** — The p. 273 reasons are stated (ankle stability, SEC elastic storage, the foot's positive supportive reaction). No mechanism is given for the sand result. The swing physics is stated by the authors as rope length setting the oscillation time.\n    25\t\n    26\t**When to apply** — Choosing where to run maximal rebound work: a firm, not very soft surface and non-cushioned footwear, per p. 273. Inference: sand is an option for early-phase or high-volume submaximal jumping, consistent with the p. 278 finding, but not for maximal depth jumps.\n    27\t\n    28\t**When NOT to apply** — The Montagni & Cardinale (1996) result is cited without design or measures; \"jumping skills\" is not defined. The swing and pogo stick suggestions are the text's ideas, with no data.\n    29\t\n    30\t**Example** — The text's own: beach volleyball and beach handball as examples of the value of sand training (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278).\n    31\t\n    32\t**Related** — [[heuristic--depth-jump-execution-cues-landing-gaze-breath]]; [[claim--depth-landings-from-2-to-3-2-m-produce-forces-over-20-times-bodyweight]]; [[heuristic--resisted-and-water-plyometrics]]\n=========== heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: heuristic\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"5.2.5\"\n     7\ttopic: strength-endurance loading\n     8\tcategory: [strength-endurance, programming, load, tempo]\n     9\tconfidence: contested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Strength-Endurance: 25-50% 1RM at 60-120 Reps per Minute, Ideally to Fatigue; Above 75-80% 1RM Trains Strength Instead (but the Book Also Prescribes 80-85%)\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — Section 5.2.5 (PDF pp. 296-298 / printed pp. 285-287; pp. 296-298 verified against the page images):\n    17\t- **Eight determinants.** Training for strength-endurance \"is determined predominantly by: 1. The magnitude of the load 2. The tempo of movement 3. The duration of each movement and each phase of the movements 4. The rest interval between training sessions 5. The length of the training period 6. The initial levels of strength-endurance of the athlete 7. The kinesiological structure of the movement 8. The relative involvement of static and dynamic strength-endurance\" (PDF p. 296 / printed p. 285).\n    18\t- **Basic prescription.** \"Repetition work with weights 25-50% of 1RM at a moderate tempo (from 60-120 repetitions/minute) is used for developing strength-endurance. The effectiveness ... will be higher if the work is executed to complete fatigue, although work of shorter duration (60% of the maximal work capacity time) also will yield satisfactory results (Agolinsky, 1953; Korobkov, 1953; Monogarov, 1958; Diachkov, 1961)\" (same page).\n    19\t- **Specificity.** \"Strength-endurance, like the other characteristics of muscular activity, is specific. However, the specificity of strength-endurance is expressed to a lesser degree than the specificity of speed, for example, while its carry-over from one activity to another is greater\" (same page; the Ch3.2.2 entry [[framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial]] quotes the same point from Ch3).\n    20\t- **Ceiling.** \"When the activity involves repetitively overcoming significant resistance (exceeding 75-80% of 1RM), one cannot enhance endurance, since one becomes constrained to developing strength predominantly (Zatsiorsky, 1966)\" (same page).\n    21\t- **Progression.** \"It is necessary to increase the amount of weight and the number of repetitions as strength-endurance increases\" (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286).\n    22\t- **Summary rule.** \"multiple repetition exercises with various weights should be considered the fundamental method for developing strength-endurance, with the load being determined by the dynamics of the given exercise. In cases requiring intense effort, an optimally heavy weight should be used together with light weights or with exercises simulating the given sport activity. If the special exercise is associated with the prolonged production of moderate force, then it is useful to work with light weights in repeated sets to the point of fatigue or failure\" (PDF p. 298 / printed p. 287).\n    23\t\n    24\t**Internal conflict, recorded:** the 75-80% ceiling (PDF p. 296 / printed p. 285) conflicts with the skater example a page later: \"Exercises with heavy weights (80-85% of 1RM) ... are recommended for developing strength-endurance in skaters\", 4-12 reps per set to complete fatigue with 2-4 min rest (Mikhailov & Panov, 1975) (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286). The rowers' \"50-80% of 1RM\" (Ulrich et al, 1966, same page) sits at or under the ceiling, so it is not part of the conflict. The summary rule on p. 287 (\"an optimally heavy weight ... together with light weights\" where intense effort is needed) sits closer to the skater example than to the ceiling. The book does not reconcile them.\n    25\t\n    26\t**Why it works** — The ceiling's stated reason: above 75-80% the work is \"constrained to developing strength predominantly.\" The load-to-fatigue advantage is reported from the cited Soviet studies, without a stated mechanism.\n    27\t\n    28\t**When to apply** — Inference: for football, where strength-endurance means repeated forceful efforts (blocking, pushing a sled), the summary rule fits better than the 25-50% band: a heavy-enough load mixed with lighter or sport-simulating work. The 60-120 reps per minute tempo describes cyclic-sport endurance work more than gridiron work. Eric's baseline: for football the priority stack is alactic and aerobic capacity and power, with glycolytic conditioning treated as a \"worst case scenario\" absorbed through camp and practice (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 116, Explicit), but \"staff do program it in the off-season\" (line 117, Explicit, interview 2026-09-28, Q19). Inference: to-fatigue strength-endurance sets fit the off-season, not in-season weeks.\n    29\t\n    30\t**When NOT to apply** — Do not quote the 75-80% ceiling as a firm rule without the 80-85% skater example beside it. The determinants list has no weightings. \"60% of the maximal work capacity time\" is not defined further (Inference: 60% of the time to failure is the plain reading).\n    31\t\n    32\t**Example** — The authors' own: 25-50% 1RM at 60-120 reps per minute to fatigue, or to 60% of the maximal work time (PDF p. 296 / printed p. 285).\n    33\t\n    34\t**Related** — [[framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial]] (Ch3.2.2, the dynamic/static and absolute/partial distinctions this section restates); [[claim--zatsiorsky-and-hansen-show-strength-endurance-training-specificity]]; [[case-study--soviet-sport-specific-strength-endurance-prescriptions]]; [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]] (Ch1 Table 1.1, endurance at 40-60% 1RM)\n=========== heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: heuristic\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n     7\ttopic: choosing between heavy and plyometric methods\n     8\tcategory: [explosive-strength, plyometrics, method-selection, sequencing]\n     9\tconfidence: contested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Large Resistance Calls for the Brief Maximal Tension Method, Small Resistance and Reactive Demand Call for Plyometrics; the Book Disagrees With Itself on Whether Plyometrics Should Be the Main Method\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — The authors' method-selection rules (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278, verified against the page image unless marked):\n    17\t- \"In those cases where explosive force is associated with the overcoming of large resistance, preference should be given to the brief maximal tension method, as used commonly in Olympic weightlifting. In situations where the resistance is small and the working effect depends upon the reactive ability of the muscles, plyometric methods should be given preference.\"\n    18\t- Staging: \"Although it should form an integral part of the training programme, plyometrics should not be combined in the beginning stages with resistance exercises such as the brief maximal tension method.\" \"In subsequent stages, plyometrics should be the main method in the training of highly qualified athletes for developing explosive strength and reactive ability.\"\n    19\t- Concentric explosive work: \"exercises with maximum effort and mixed regimes using weights equal to 40% of 1RM, or 30% of 1RM (Verkhoshansky, 1963; Papysheva, 1966). Explosive-isometric tension up to 80% of maximum should be used in conjunction with this routine.\"\n    20\t- In combined sessions, resistance work goes first: combined programmes beat plyometrics alone \"provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277).\n    21\t- Testing decides: \"To ascertain whether an athlete needs plyometric or heavy resistance training depends on measuring the *strength deficit* ... Without such testing, prescription of plyometric training tends to remain hit-and-miss\" (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273; repeated at PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282 in a grammatically broken sentence).\n    22\t\n    23\t**Internal conflict, recorded:** \"plyometrics should be the main method in the training of highly qualified athletes\" (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278) against, half a page earlier, \"it is futile to rely predominantly on plyometric drills to enhance sport specific explosive strength and power, because efficiency of motor control is fundamental to all skilled sporting performance\" (PDF pp. 288-289 / printed pp. 277-278). The second follows a Nicol et al (1997) citation. Inference: the first reads like the original shock-method programme and the second like a later addition, but the book marks neither passage by author. The book does not reconcile them.\n    24\t\n    25\t**Why it works** — The authors tie each method to the force demand: heavy brief tension for large resistance, reactive shock loading for small resistance. The strength deficit decides which an individual needs (Ch1; see [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]], which records that the book also states the deficit rule two ways).\n    26\t\n    27\t**When to apply** — Eric's baseline: \"we incorporate the countermovement jump and the isometric mid-thigh pull to get a dynamic strength index, to determine whether we need more plyometric training or more max-strength training\" (Eric, interview 2026-09-28, Q21; `docs/method-baseline.md` line 76, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q21)), with the baseline's own tag on that line: [contested; the ratio tracks IMTP strength; one controlled trial (Pleša 2024, basketball, split at 0.90) supported only the strength arm; no football data]. Inference: DSI plays the role the authors give the strength deficit (both compare a fast expression of force with a maximal one to choose plyometric versus heavy work), but they are different measures; Eric's baseline Tension 17 records that the DSI cutoffs were never validated and that IMTP peak force drives most of the ratio (line 261). Inference: for linemen, whose sport force is against large resistance, the authors' rule favours brief maximal tension; for skill positions, whose force is against bodymass, it favours plyometrics.\n    28\t\n    29\t**When NOT to apply** — The resistance-first rule for combined sessions is not supported for novices: in a within-workout experiment on novices (strength then speed-strength work versus the reverse, pauses lengthened slightly), \"There was no statistically significant difference in speed-strength of the subjects in the first two groups\", and a depth-jump group gained most (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288; see [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]]). The same page reports a separate across-stage experiment in which weights first, then depth jumps, was more effective, and the authors expect the within-session effect to hold for well-trained athletes, but \"the validity of this hypothesis can be established conclusively only by careful experimentation.\" Do not cite Supertraining as saying plyometrics should be the main method without the \"futile to rely predominantly\" sentence, or the reverse. \"Beginning stages\" and \"highly qualified\" are not defined. The 40% and 30% of 1RM loads and the 80% explosive-isometric tension are given without sets, reps or rest.\n    30\t\n    31\t**Example** — The authors' own contrast: Olympic weightlifting (large resistance, brief maximal tension) versus situations where \"the resistance is small and the working effect depends upon the reactive ability of the muscles\" (PDF p. 289 / printed p. 278). The passage names no drill for the second case.\n    32\t\n    33\t**Related** — [[heuristic--strength-deficit-sets-training-method-choice]]; [[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]; [[claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance]]; [[claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers]]; [[claim--heavy-squats-and-light-fast-lifts-miss-the-eccentric-concentric-switch]]; [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]] (Ch5.3, novice sequencing)","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"fa7fb6cb-2bc3-4811-8aa1-d62e1014371f","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
