{"parentUuid":"25830359-1762-4b01-9201-352dea7976ae","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac44742970c7341bd","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffcj5uSn6cUtC1GKJMKb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018oxoyPABqBdY7Y947afaCN","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/; for f in claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest framework--five-phases-of-a-plyometric-action; do echo \"=========== $f\"; cat -n $f.md; done","description":"Read next four edited entries"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7759,"cache_read_input_tokens":80952,"output_tokens":305,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":47},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7759},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":305,"cache_read_input_tokens":80952,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7759,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7759,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_018oxoyPABqBdY7Y947afaCN":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/; for f in claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest framework--five-phases-of-a-plyometric-action; do echo \"=========== $f\"; cat -n $f.md; done","description":"Read next four edited entries"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffcj5eoyQrJajXQfaYZz","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"9f98fcd0-5af9-4d42-8737-b6e20a670026","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:26:32.129Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"9f98fcd0-5af9-4d42-8737-b6e20a670026","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"0b81aa46-3ac1-41a7-bee9-ff3aba05ec92","agentId":"ac44742970c7341bd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018oxoyPABqBdY7Y947afaCN","type":"tool_result","content":"=========== claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: claim\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n     7\ttopic: training economy of depth jumps\n     8\tcategory: [plyometrics, depth-jumps, reactive-ability, training-economy]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Jumpers Doing 475 Depth Jumps in 12 Weeks Gained More Reactive Ability Than a Group Doing 1472 Barbell Push-Offs and 93 Tons\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — \"The plyometric method of developing explosive strength and reactive ability solves to a significant degree the problem of economising training, since it ensures that a high level of special conditioning is attained in the minimum time. Thus, a comparative experiment revealed that a group of track-and-field jumpers executing primarily depth-jumps (all of them did 475 jumps) over a 12 week period in the preparation phase showed greater improvement in reactive ability than the group which trained with traditional methods and executed 1472 general push-offs (squats, jumping and hopping with a barbell weighing 90-95%, 70-80% and 30-40% of 1RM) and lifted a total volume of 93 tons!\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277, verified against the page image).\n    17\t\n    18\tTwo sentences around it: \"The training effect of depth-jumps or shock-loading for developing explosive strength is very large, having little equal when compared with the other means of strength training, especially when combined in a complex with other forms of intensive strength training\", corroborated by Semyenov 1971, Tatyan 1974, Dobrovolsky 1972, Savin 1974, Khodykin 1974 and Deniskin 1976 (same page). And: \"Carefully periodised training programmes which combine resistance exercises with plyometrics can develop explosive strength even more effectively than plyometrics alone, provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions\" (same page).\n    19\t\n    20\t**Why it works** — The authors give the result as evidence of \"economising training\"; no mechanism is stated here beyond the shock-method rationale elsewhere in 5.2.4.\n    21\t\n    22\t**When to apply** — Making the case to coaches that a small, well-placed depth-jump dose can do more for reactive ability than a large barbell volume. Inference: the companion sentence (resistance work before plyometrics in the same session) is a direct sequencing rule for combined sessions (for novices, see When NOT to apply).\n    23\t\n    24\t**When NOT to apply** — Unclear points in the source: \"all of them did 475 jumps\" does not say whether 475 is per athlete or the group total; the experiment has no citation, group sizes, statistics or outcome test named beyond \"reactive ability\"; the comparison group's \"1472 general push-offs\" is also unclear per athlete or total. Supported only as a reported Soviet-era comparison. The result is for track-and-field jumpers in a preparation phase. The resistance-first rule is untested for novices: in a within-workout experiment on novices (strength then speed-strength work versus the reverse), \"There was no statistically significant difference in speed-strength of the subjects in the first two groups\" (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288; see [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]]). The authors expect the strength-first effect to hold for well-trained athletes, but say \"the validity of this hypothesis can be established conclusively only by careful experimentation\" (same page).\n    25\t\n    26\t**Example** — The authors' own experiment as quoted above (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277).\n    27\t\n    28\t**Related** — [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]]; [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]; [[heuristic--structured-load-progression-beats-volume-alone-or-random-combination]]; [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]]; [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]] (Ch5.3, novice sequencing)\n=========== heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: heuristic\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n     7\ttopic: choosing depth jump height\n     8\tcategory: [plyometrics, depth-jumps, progression, coaching-cues]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Set Depth-Jump Height by Strength Fitness: Too High Once the Transition Slows or the Heels Are Driven to the Ground; Raise Speed Before Height\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — The authors' rules for choosing drop height in training (all on pages verified against the page image):\n    17\t- \"The optimal depth of the jump is determined by the athlete's strength fitness, while the athlete should ensure that significant dynamic force is developed without slowing down the transition from eccentric to concentric work by the muscles involved\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274).\n    18\t- \"The optimal height of a depth jump does not result in a landing where the heel is forced to the ground by momentum\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276, item 8).\n    19\t- \"One should aim to increase the speed and acceleration of movement before increasing the drop height or take-off height\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, item 5).\n    20\t- \"One should begin at a relatively low height and increase it gradually to the optimal height. It makes sense to begin by jumping upward and forward and only after sufficient training, to jump strictly upward\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275, item 1).\n    21\t- Height is preferred over added weight, and a force plate can find the height for a required force (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, item 1).\n    22\t- For jumping power \"one can work without an additional load and utilise only falling bodymass\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274). Fig 5.10 (Verkhoshansky, 1977) shows three variants: two under \"Horizontal Projection\" (after the drop, the athlete rebounds forward over low objects drawn on the ground) and one under \"Vertical Projection\" (rebound upward toward a hanging marker) (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275, read from the image; the drawings are small, so the object details are approximate).\n    23\t\n    24\t**Why it works** — The authors' reason is the transition: height is only useful while it raises dynamic force without \"slowing down the transition from eccentric to concentric work\". Heels forced to the ground is their visible sign that momentum has overwhelmed the athlete. Inference: a heel strike means the ankle has been driven through its range, which lengthens the amortisation the authors want kept short; the book states only the rule.\n    25\t\n    26\t**When to apply** — Inference: with a force plate or contact mat, find each athlete's height by stepping the box up until contact time rises or rebound height falls, and stop below that point; on the field, watch for heels hitting the ground. This individual approach is consistent with the authors' \"determined by the athlete's strength fitness.\" Eric's baseline: mRSI from daily CMJ testing \"was deliberately chosen as the daily athlete-readiness marker\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 74, Explicit). Inference: a drop-jump RSI (jump height over contact time) at stepped heights would be a separate test of the authors' \"without slowing down the transition\" rule; the baseline has no drop-jump test.\n    27\t\n    28\t**When NOT to apply** — The Ch4.2.3 figures put the optimum for advanced jumpers at 0.75-1.15 m ([[claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]]); the rules here give no number, and the authors stress \"using considerably lower jump heights with anyone who is unused to this type of training\" (Ch4.2.3, PDF p. 233 / printed p. 222, verified against the page image). The heel rule is a coaching sign, not a measured threshold. Upward-and-forward before strictly upward is the authors' order; they give no reason.\n    29\t\n    30\t**Example** — The authors' own: the three Fig 5.10 depth-jump variants, and the progression from upward-and-forward to strictly upward jumps (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275).\n    31\t\n    32\t**Related** — [[claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]]; [[framework--reactivity-coefficient-and-depth-jump-test]]; [[heuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height]]; [[heuristic--depth-jump-execution-cues-landing-gaze-breath]]; [[heuristic--match-plyometric-prescription-to-joint-angle-and-fiber-type]]\n=========== heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: heuristic\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n     7\ttopic: depth jump volume and rest\n     8\tcategory: [plyometrics, depth-jumps, programming, volume, rest-intervals]\n     9\tconfidence: contested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Depth-Jump and Maximal-Plyometric Volume and Rest: The Book Gives Two Incompatible Dosing Models\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — Section 5.2.4 gives the following dosing statements. They do not agree, so all are listed with locators.\n    17\t\n    18\t**Model 1: maximal plyometrics dosed like a maximal lift.** The first, third and fourth statements are Siff's text (the powermetrics and 1RM-analogy passages; the section uses his term \"powermetrics\", PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268); prescription item 6 is from the authors' prescription list.\n    19\t- \"*Maximal plyometrics* essentially are low-repetition activities ... Just like maximal strength training with weights, these powerful impulses are not meant to be imposed on the musculature every workout, nor are multiple repetitions even possible or advisable. Moreover, a rest interval of 2-4 minutes is recommended between successive repetitions and as much as 10-12 minutes between sets\" (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272, verified against the page image).\n    20\t- Prescription item 6: \"The rest interval between sets of maximal plyometric exercises should be about 10 minutes for power development (strength-speed and speed-strength) and much shorter intervals with submaximal plyometrics to develop explosive-strength endurance\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, verified against the page image).\n    21\t- \"several books advocate sessions with over 100 repetitions of plyometric movements ... This volume of *maximal plyometrics* (unlike sub-maximal plyometrics) is neither productive nor safe for any athlete. Maximal plyometrics, like maximal weight lifting, involves single all-out repetitions separated by a few minutes of rest\" (PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282, verified against the page image).\n    22\t- \"classical plyometrics are 1 RM movements which require as much as several minutes of rest before recovery is complete\" (PDF p. 294 / printed p. 283, text layer).\n    23\t\n    24\t**Model 2: depth jumps in sets of 5-10.** Prescription item 4 is from the authors' prescription list (PDF p. 285); items 2 and 3 are from the depth-jump recommendations credited to \"prolific experience\" (PDF p. 286), a list whose figures (Figs 5.10, 5.11) are credited to Verkhoshansky (1977); the list itself carries no citation.\n    25\t- Prescription item 4: \"As an initial guideline, the dosage of plyometric exercises should not exceed 5-8 repetitions in one set\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, verified against the page image).\n    26\t- Depth-jump item 2: \"The optimal dosage of depth-jumps with an energetic vertical take-off in one training session should not exceed 4 sets of 10 jumps for well-conditioned athletes and 2-3 sets of 5-8 jumps for lesser conditioned athletes. Easy running and relaxation exercises for a period of 10-15 minutes should be done between sets\" (same page, verified against the page image).\n    27\t- Depth-jump item 3: \"Well-conditioned athletes can execute depth-jumps 3 times per week for 2 sets of 10 jumps at the completion of technical training\" (same page, verified against the page image).\n    28\t\n    29\t**Depth-jump item 1, kept out of Model 2:** \"Good results are obtained with depth-jumps using a complex of vertical jumps (Fig 5.11). Each exercise is executed in sets of 10 repetitions, with 1-15 minutes of rest between sets\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275; the page image prints \"1-15\", recorded as printed). Fig 5.11, \"Jumping exercises for developing reactive ability (Verkhoshansky, 1977)\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276, verified against the page image), shows ground-based vertical, tuck and split jumps with no box. Inference: \"each exercise\" most plausibly refers to that complex of ground jumps, so the sets of 10 and \"1-15 minutes\" are not a depth-jump dose.\n    30\t\n    31\t**The conflict.** Model 1 says multiple repetitions of maximal plyometrics are not \"possible or advisable\" and separates single reps by 2-4 minutes. Model 2 prescribes sets of 10 energetic depth jumps, up to 40 per session, with rest given only between sets. Inference: Model 2 might be read as submaximal work, but depth-jump item 2 specifies \"an energetic vertical take-off\", and the 0.75-1.15 m heights in Ch4 are for advanced jumpers. Rest between sets of maximal or depth-jump work also varies: about 10 min (p. 274), 10-12 min (p. 272) and 10-15 min of easy running (p. 275). The book does not reconcile them.\n    32\t\n    33\t**Why it works** — Model 1's stated reasons: maximal plyometrics are \"extremely demanding on the central nervous system\" and impose \"great mechanical strain on the tendons, in particular\" (PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282). Model 2's stated basis is \"prolific experience with depth-jumps for developing jumping power\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275). Neither gives fatigue or recovery data.\n    34\t\n    35\t**When to apply** — Inference: one way to use both is to treat Model 1 as the rule for truly maximal efforts (maximal height, rebound to a marker, near-maximal tension) and Model 2 as an upper volume cap for depth-jump sessions, keeping per-session counts at or under 2-3 × 5-8 for less-conditioned athletes and 4 × 10 for well-conditioned ones. The book does not make this split. For any athlete, the authors' quality rule holds: \"It is the quality of the plyometric exercises, not the quantity, which is important\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276, item 11). Eric's baseline: in-season \"the stated rule is 'maintain intensity, reduce volume' rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Eric's baseline: \"lifting and hard practice end 72 hours before a Saturday game\" (line 186, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)). Inference: Model 1's low-rep, long-rest structure fits the line-105 rule more directly than Model 2's sets of 10, and any maximal depth-jump session in season falls before that 72-hour cut-off.\n    36\t\n    37\t**When NOT to apply** — Do not cite \"4 × 10 depth jumps\" as Supertraining's recommendation without the Model 1 statements beside it, or the reverse. \"1-15 minutes\" may be a misprint (Inference: 1.5 or 10-15 are possible readings), but the page prints 1-15, so do not quote a corrected value. None of these numbers comes with a cited study, population or drop height (except the Ch4 heights for advanced jumpers). The \"over 100 repetitions\" warning targets maximal plyometrics; the authors say submaximal plyometrics differ.\n    38\t\n    39\t**Example** — The authors' own figures: lesser-conditioned athletes 2-3 sets of 5-8 energetic depth jumps with 10-15 minutes of easy running between sets (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275), against maximal rebounds as single reps 2-4 minutes apart with 10-12 minutes between sets (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272).\n    40\t\n    41\t**Related** — [[heuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement]]; [[claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]]; [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance]]; [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]]\n=========== framework--five-phases-of-a-plyometric-action\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: framework\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n     7\ttopic: phases of a plyometric action\n     8\tcategory: [plyometrics, stretch-shortening-cycle, biomechanics]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# The Five Phases of a Plyometric Action (Fig 5.5), With Coupling Time as the Explosive-Isometric Interval\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — Under \"The Fundamental Theory of Plyometrics\" the authors list five phases (PDF p. 281 / printed p. 270; Fig 5.5 verified against the page image):\n    17\t1. **Initial momentum phase:** the body or limb moves \"because of kinetic energy (KE) it has accumulated from a preceding action.\"\n    18\t2. **Electromechanical delay phase:** contact with a surface stops the limb and \"provokes the muscles to contract\"; the delay is \"the time elapsing between the onset of the action potential in the motor nerves and the onset of the muscle contraction.\" The authors note some workers include lengthening of the SEC (series elastic component) in this phase, but \"electromechanical\" was coined for the electrical-to-mechanical interval, \"which does not include the passive stretching of connective tissue.\"\n    19\t3. **Amortisation phase:** the KE \"produces a powerful myotatic stretch reflex which leads to eccentric muscle contraction accompanied by explosive isometric contraction\" and stretching of the SEC (e.g. tendons). \"The explosive isometric phase between the end of the eccentric action and the beginning of the concentric action lasts for a period known as the *coupling time*.\"\n    20\t4. **Rebound phase:** release of elastic energy from the SEC, with involuntary concentric contraction evoked by the stretch reflex, \"as well as possible local change in the muscle fibre mechanics\"; it \"may include the contribution added by voluntary concentric contraction.\"\n    21\t5. **Final momentum phase:** after the concentric contraction, the body or limb continues on the KE imparted by contraction and elastic recoil.\n    22\t\n    23\tFig 5.5, as drawn: boxes for initial momentum, amortisation, rebound and final momentum phases; between them, bars for \"EM delay\", \"eccentric contraction\" and \"concentric contraction\", joined at the bottom by a box \"explosive isometrics\" labelled \"Coupling Time\".\n    24\t\n    25\tThe caption words the EM delay differently: \"Electromechanical delay between signal to terminate initial momentum phase and instant when eccentric contraction begins\" (caption), against \"between the onset of the action potential in the motor nerves and the onset of the muscle contraction\" (text item 2), both on PDF p. 281 / printed p. 270. Inference: both describe the electrical-to-mechanical interval.\n    26\t\n    27\t**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism is in the phase descriptions: stretch reflex plus SEC elastic energy, released in the rebound. Elsewhere they add that the process is not only spinal reflex and elastic recoil (see [[claim--plyometric-skill-is-cognitive-and-state-dependent]]).\n    28\t\n    29\t**When to apply** — As a shared vocabulary for coaching and for reading a force-time trace of a depth jump or rebound: the coupling interval is the part the authors' 0.15 s guide applies to (see [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]]). Inference: on a force plate, the eccentric (braking) and concentric (propulsive) phases of a drop jump map onto phases 3 and 4; the authors do not give a force-plate method for isolating the isometric coupling interval.\n    30\t\n    31\t**When NOT to apply** — The phase list is a descriptive model, not measured timing; the book gives no durations for phases 1, 2, 4 or 5. The rebound phase's \"possible local change in the muscle fibre mechanics\" is stated as possible, not shown.\n    32\t\n    33\t**Example** — Inference: in a depth jump from a box, phase 1 is the fall, phases 2-3 are the first instants of ground contact through the lowest point, phase 4 is the push-off, and phase 5 is the flight. The authors' own illustration is Fig 5.5.\n    34\t\n    35\t**Related** — [[framework--series-and-parallel-elastic-components]]; [[principle--triphasic-nature-of-muscle-action]]; [[principle--short-amortization-preserves-elastic-energy]]; [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]]","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"72fbf552-2775-4757-a7f9-e731ab50538c","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:26:32.339Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"=========== claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: claim\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n     7\ttopic: training economy of depth jumps\n     8\tcategory: [plyometrics, depth-jumps, reactive-ability, training-economy]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Jumpers Doing 475 Depth Jumps in 12 Weeks Gained More Reactive Ability Than a Group Doing 1472 Barbell Push-Offs and 93 Tons\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — \"The plyometric method of developing explosive strength and reactive ability solves to a significant degree the problem of economising training, since it ensures that a high level of special conditioning is attained in the minimum time. Thus, a comparative experiment revealed that a group of track-and-field jumpers executing primarily depth-jumps (all of them did 475 jumps) over a 12 week period in the preparation phase showed greater improvement in reactive ability than the group which trained with traditional methods and executed 1472 general push-offs (squats, jumping and hopping with a barbell weighing 90-95%, 70-80% and 30-40% of 1RM) and lifted a total volume of 93 tons!\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277, verified against the page image).\n    17\t\n    18\tTwo sentences around it: \"The training effect of depth-jumps or shock-loading for developing explosive strength is very large, having little equal when compared with the other means of strength training, especially when combined in a complex with other forms of intensive strength training\", corroborated by Semyenov 1971, Tatyan 1974, Dobrovolsky 1972, Savin 1974, Khodykin 1974 and Deniskin 1976 (same page). And: \"Carefully periodised training programmes which combine resistance exercises with plyometrics can develop explosive strength even more effectively than plyometrics alone, provided the resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions\" (same page).\n    19\t\n    20\t**Why it works** — The authors give the result as evidence of \"economising training\"; no mechanism is stated here beyond the shock-method rationale elsewhere in 5.2.4.\n    21\t\n    22\t**When to apply** — Making the case to coaches that a small, well-placed depth-jump dose can do more for reactive ability than a large barbell volume. Inference: the companion sentence (resistance work before plyometrics in the same session) is a direct sequencing rule for combined sessions (for novices, see When NOT to apply).\n    23\t\n    24\t**When NOT to apply** — Unclear points in the source: \"all of them did 475 jumps\" does not say whether 475 is per athlete or the group total; the experiment has no citation, group sizes, statistics or outcome test named beyond \"reactive ability\"; the comparison group's \"1472 general push-offs\" is also unclear per athlete or total. Supported only as a reported Soviet-era comparison. The result is for track-and-field jumpers in a preparation phase. The resistance-first rule is untested for novices: in a within-workout experiment on novices (strength then speed-strength work versus the reverse), \"There was no statistically significant difference in speed-strength of the subjects in the first two groups\" (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288; see [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]]). The authors expect the strength-first effect to hold for well-trained athletes, but say \"the validity of this hypothesis can be established conclusively only by careful experimentation\" (same page).\n    25\t\n    26\t**Example** — The authors' own experiment as quoted above (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277).\n    27\t\n    28\t**Related** — [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]]; [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]; [[heuristic--structured-load-progression-beats-volume-alone-or-random-combination]]; [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]]; [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]] (Ch5.3, novice sequencing)\n=========== heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: heuristic\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n     7\ttopic: choosing depth jump height\n     8\tcategory: [plyometrics, depth-jumps, progression, coaching-cues]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Set Depth-Jump Height by Strength Fitness: Too High Once the Transition Slows or the Heels Are Driven to the Ground; Raise Speed Before Height\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — The authors' rules for choosing drop height in training (all on pages verified against the page image):\n    17\t- \"The optimal depth of the jump is determined by the athlete's strength fitness, while the athlete should ensure that significant dynamic force is developed without slowing down the transition from eccentric to concentric work by the muscles involved\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274).\n    18\t- \"The optimal height of a depth jump does not result in a landing where the heel is forced to the ground by momentum\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276, item 8).\n    19\t- \"One should aim to increase the speed and acceleration of movement before increasing the drop height or take-off height\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, item 5).\n    20\t- \"One should begin at a relatively low height and increase it gradually to the optimal height. It makes sense to begin by jumping upward and forward and only after sufficient training, to jump strictly upward\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275, item 1).\n    21\t- Height is preferred over added weight, and a force plate can find the height for a required force (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, item 1).\n    22\t- For jumping power \"one can work without an additional load and utilise only falling bodymass\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274). Fig 5.10 (Verkhoshansky, 1977) shows three variants: two under \"Horizontal Projection\" (after the drop, the athlete rebounds forward over low objects drawn on the ground) and one under \"Vertical Projection\" (rebound upward toward a hanging marker) (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275, read from the image; the drawings are small, so the object details are approximate).\n    23\t\n    24\t**Why it works** — The authors' reason is the transition: height is only useful while it raises dynamic force without \"slowing down the transition from eccentric to concentric work\". Heels forced to the ground is their visible sign that momentum has overwhelmed the athlete. Inference: a heel strike means the ankle has been driven through its range, which lengthens the amortisation the authors want kept short; the book states only the rule.\n    25\t\n    26\t**When to apply** — Inference: with a force plate or contact mat, find each athlete's height by stepping the box up until contact time rises or rebound height falls, and stop below that point; on the field, watch for heels hitting the ground. This individual approach is consistent with the authors' \"determined by the athlete's strength fitness.\" Eric's baseline: mRSI from daily CMJ testing \"was deliberately chosen as the daily athlete-readiness marker\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 74, Explicit). Inference: a drop-jump RSI (jump height over contact time) at stepped heights would be a separate test of the authors' \"without slowing down the transition\" rule; the baseline has no drop-jump test.\n    27\t\n    28\t**When NOT to apply** — The Ch4.2.3 figures put the optimum for advanced jumpers at 0.75-1.15 m ([[claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]]); the rules here give no number, and the authors stress \"using considerably lower jump heights with anyone who is unused to this type of training\" (Ch4.2.3, PDF p. 233 / printed p. 222, verified against the page image). The heel rule is a coaching sign, not a measured threshold. Upward-and-forward before strictly upward is the authors' order; they give no reason.\n    29\t\n    30\t**Example** — The authors' own: the three Fig 5.10 depth-jump variants, and the progression from upward-and-forward to strictly upward jumps (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275).\n    31\t\n    32\t**Related** — [[claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]]; [[framework--reactivity-coefficient-and-depth-jump-test]]; [[heuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height]]; [[heuristic--depth-jump-execution-cues-landing-gaze-breath]]; [[heuristic--match-plyometric-prescription-to-joint-angle-and-fiber-type]]\n=========== heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: heuristic\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n     7\ttopic: depth jump volume and rest\n     8\tcategory: [plyometrics, depth-jumps, programming, volume, rest-intervals]\n     9\tconfidence: contested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Depth-Jump and Maximal-Plyometric Volume and Rest: The Book Gives Two Incompatible Dosing Models\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — Section 5.2.4 gives the following dosing statements. They do not agree, so all are listed with locators.\n    17\t\n    18\t**Model 1: maximal plyometrics dosed like a maximal lift.** The first, third and fourth statements are Siff's text (the powermetrics and 1RM-analogy passages; the section uses his term \"powermetrics\", PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268); prescription item 6 is from the authors' prescription list.\n    19\t- \"*Maximal plyometrics* essentially are low-repetition activities ... Just like maximal strength training with weights, these powerful impulses are not meant to be imposed on the musculature every workout, nor are multiple repetitions even possible or advisable. Moreover, a rest interval of 2-4 minutes is recommended between successive repetitions and as much as 10-12 minutes between sets\" (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272, verified against the page image).\n    20\t- Prescription item 6: \"The rest interval between sets of maximal plyometric exercises should be about 10 minutes for power development (strength-speed and speed-strength) and much shorter intervals with submaximal plyometrics to develop explosive-strength endurance\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, verified against the page image).\n    21\t- \"several books advocate sessions with over 100 repetitions of plyometric movements ... This volume of *maximal plyometrics* (unlike sub-maximal plyometrics) is neither productive nor safe for any athlete. Maximal plyometrics, like maximal weight lifting, involves single all-out repetitions separated by a few minutes of rest\" (PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282, verified against the page image).\n    22\t- \"classical plyometrics are 1 RM movements which require as much as several minutes of rest before recovery is complete\" (PDF p. 294 / printed p. 283, text layer).\n    23\t\n    24\t**Model 2: depth jumps in sets of 5-10.** Prescription item 4 is from the authors' prescription list (PDF p. 285); items 2 and 3 are from the depth-jump recommendations credited to \"prolific experience\" (PDF p. 286), a list whose figures (Figs 5.10, 5.11) are credited to Verkhoshansky (1977); the list itself carries no citation.\n    25\t- Prescription item 4: \"As an initial guideline, the dosage of plyometric exercises should not exceed 5-8 repetitions in one set\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, verified against the page image).\n    26\t- Depth-jump item 2: \"The optimal dosage of depth-jumps with an energetic vertical take-off in one training session should not exceed 4 sets of 10 jumps for well-conditioned athletes and 2-3 sets of 5-8 jumps for lesser conditioned athletes. Easy running and relaxation exercises for a period of 10-15 minutes should be done between sets\" (same page, verified against the page image).\n    27\t- Depth-jump item 3: \"Well-conditioned athletes can execute depth-jumps 3 times per week for 2 sets of 10 jumps at the completion of technical training\" (same page, verified against the page image).\n    28\t\n    29\t**Depth-jump item 1, kept out of Model 2:** \"Good results are obtained with depth-jumps using a complex of vertical jumps (Fig 5.11). Each exercise is executed in sets of 10 repetitions, with 1-15 minutes of rest between sets\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275; the page image prints \"1-15\", recorded as printed). Fig 5.11, \"Jumping exercises for developing reactive ability (Verkhoshansky, 1977)\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276, verified against the page image), shows ground-based vertical, tuck and split jumps with no box. Inference: \"each exercise\" most plausibly refers to that complex of ground jumps, so the sets of 10 and \"1-15 minutes\" are not a depth-jump dose.\n    30\t\n    31\t**The conflict.** Model 1 says multiple repetitions of maximal plyometrics are not \"possible or advisable\" and separates single reps by 2-4 minutes. Model 2 prescribes sets of 10 energetic depth jumps, up to 40 per session, with rest given only between sets. Inference: Model 2 might be read as submaximal work, but depth-jump item 2 specifies \"an energetic vertical take-off\", and the 0.75-1.15 m heights in Ch4 are for advanced jumpers. Rest between sets of maximal or depth-jump work also varies: about 10 min (p. 274), 10-12 min (p. 272) and 10-15 min of easy running (p. 275). The book does not reconcile them.\n    32\t\n    33\t**Why it works** — Model 1's stated reasons: maximal plyometrics are \"extremely demanding on the central nervous system\" and impose \"great mechanical strain on the tendons, in particular\" (PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282). Model 2's stated basis is \"prolific experience with depth-jumps for developing jumping power\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275). Neither gives fatigue or recovery data.\n    34\t\n    35\t**When to apply** — Inference: one way to use both is to treat Model 1 as the rule for truly maximal efforts (maximal height, rebound to a marker, near-maximal tension) and Model 2 as an upper volume cap for depth-jump sessions, keeping per-session counts at or under 2-3 × 5-8 for less-conditioned athletes and 4 × 10 for well-conditioned ones. The book does not make this split. For any athlete, the authors' quality rule holds: \"It is the quality of the plyometric exercises, not the quantity, which is important\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276, item 11). Eric's baseline: in-season \"the stated rule is 'maintain intensity, reduce volume' rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Eric's baseline: \"lifting and hard practice end 72 hours before a Saturday game\" (line 186, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)). Inference: Model 1's low-rep, long-rest structure fits the line-105 rule more directly than Model 2's sets of 10, and any maximal depth-jump session in season falls before that 72-hour cut-off.\n    36\t\n    37\t**When NOT to apply** — Do not cite \"4 × 10 depth jumps\" as Supertraining's recommendation without the Model 1 statements beside it, or the reverse. \"1-15 minutes\" may be a misprint (Inference: 1.5 or 10-15 are possible readings), but the page prints 1-15, so do not quote a corrected value. None of these numbers comes with a cited study, population or drop height (except the Ch4 heights for advanced jumpers). The \"over 100 repetitions\" warning targets maximal plyometrics; the authors say submaximal plyometrics differ.\n    38\t\n    39\t**Example** — The authors' own figures: lesser-conditioned athletes 2-3 sets of 5-8 energetic depth jumps with 10-15 minutes of easy running between sets (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275), against maximal rebounds as single reps 2-4 minutes apart with 10-12 minutes between sets (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272).\n    40\t\n    41\t**Related** — [[heuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement]]; [[claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]]; [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance]]; [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]]\n=========== framework--five-phases-of-a-plyometric-action\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: framework\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n     7\ttopic: phases of a plyometric action\n     8\tcategory: [plyometrics, stretch-shortening-cycle, biomechanics]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# The Five Phases of a Plyometric Action (Fig 5.5), With Coupling Time as the Explosive-Isometric Interval\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — Under \"The Fundamental Theory of Plyometrics\" the authors list five phases (PDF p. 281 / printed p. 270; Fig 5.5 verified against the page image):\n    17\t1. **Initial momentum phase:** the body or limb moves \"because of kinetic energy (KE) it has accumulated from a preceding action.\"\n    18\t2. **Electromechanical delay phase:** contact with a surface stops the limb and \"provokes the muscles to contract\"; the delay is \"the time elapsing between the onset of the action potential in the motor nerves and the onset of the muscle contraction.\" The authors note some workers include lengthening of the SEC (series elastic component) in this phase, but \"electromechanical\" was coined for the electrical-to-mechanical interval, \"which does not include the passive stretching of connective tissue.\"\n    19\t3. **Amortisation phase:** the KE \"produces a powerful myotatic stretch reflex which leads to eccentric muscle contraction accompanied by explosive isometric contraction\" and stretching of the SEC (e.g. tendons). \"The explosive isometric phase between the end of the eccentric action and the beginning of the concentric action lasts for a period known as the *coupling time*.\"\n    20\t4. **Rebound phase:** release of elastic energy from the SEC, with involuntary concentric contraction evoked by the stretch reflex, \"as well as possible local change in the muscle fibre mechanics\"; it \"may include the contribution added by voluntary concentric contraction.\"\n    21\t5. **Final momentum phase:** after the concentric contraction, the body or limb continues on the KE imparted by contraction and elastic recoil.\n    22\t\n    23\tFig 5.5, as drawn: boxes for initial momentum, amortisation, rebound and final momentum phases; between them, bars for \"EM delay\", \"eccentric contraction\" and \"concentric contraction\", joined at the bottom by a box \"explosive isometrics\" labelled \"Coupling Time\".\n    24\t\n    25\tThe caption words the EM delay differently: \"Electromechanical delay between signal to terminate initial momentum phase and instant when eccentric contraction begins\" (caption), against \"between the onset of the action potential in the motor nerves and the onset of the muscle contraction\" (text item 2), both on PDF p. 281 / printed p. 270. Inference: both describe the electrical-to-mechanical interval.\n    26\t\n    27\t**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism is in the phase descriptions: stretch reflex plus SEC elastic energy, released in the rebound. Elsewhere they add that the process is not only spinal reflex and elastic recoil (see [[claim--plyometric-skill-is-cognitive-and-state-dependent]]).\n    28\t\n    29\t**When to apply** — As a shared vocabulary for coaching and for reading a force-time trace of a depth jump or rebound: the coupling interval is the part the authors' 0.15 s guide applies to (see [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]]). Inference: on a force plate, the eccentric (braking) and concentric (propulsive) phases of a drop jump map onto phases 3 and 4; the authors do not give a force-plate method for isolating the isometric coupling interval.\n    30\t\n    31\t**When NOT to apply** — The phase list is a descriptive model, not measured timing; the book gives no durations for phases 1, 2, 4 or 5. The rebound phase's \"possible local change in the muscle fibre mechanics\" is stated as possible, not shown.\n    32\t\n    33\t**Example** — Inference: in a depth jump from a box, phase 1 is the fall, phases 2-3 are the first instants of ground contact through the lowest point, phase 4 is the push-off, and phase 5 is the flight. The authors' own illustration is Fig 5.5.\n    34\t\n    35\t**Related** — [[framework--series-and-parallel-elastic-components]]; [[principle--triphasic-nature-of-muscle-action]]; [[principle--short-amortization-preserves-elastic-energy]]; [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9f98fcd0-5af9-4d42-8737-b6e20a670026","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
