{"parentUuid":"8fc8c5c0-baef-40e4-b31d-399c0199767b","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac44742970c7341bd","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffcjJEM8Rkye2rQCZuWj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01W8Cb2eu5JukutDh9XskozU","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/; for f in framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function heuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height claim--lewis-formula-estimates-jump-power-from-bodymass-and-height; do echo \"=========== $f\"; cat -n $f.md; done","description":"Read three more edited entries"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8119,"cache_read_input_tokens":88711,"output_tokens":839,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":606},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8119},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":839,"cache_read_input_tokens":88711,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8119,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8119,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01W8Cb2eu5JukutDh9XskozU":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/; for f in framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function heuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height claim--lewis-formula-estimates-jump-power-from-bodymass-and-height; do echo \"=========== $f\"; cat -n $f.md; done","description":"Read three more edited entries"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffcjHxEgg5K8ScqKAMka","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"a8da87ce-85f0-4f51-a683-49b786552eb2","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:26:39.346Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"a8da87ce-85f0-4f51-a683-49b786552eb2","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"0b81aa46-3ac1-41a7-bee9-ff3aba05ec92","agentId":"ac44742970c7341bd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01W8Cb2eu5JukutDh9XskozU","type":"tool_result","content":"=========== framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: framework\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n     7\ttopic: classes of plyometric training\n     8\tcategory: [plyometrics, classification, exercise-selection]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Classes of Plyometric (Powermetric) Training: Impact or Non-Impact, Maximal to Non-Plyometric, Functional or Non-Functional (Fig 5.8)\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — The text (Siff's classification; it uses his term \"powermetrics\", PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268) replaces the \"classically plyometric or not\" argument with a classification (PDF pp. 283-284 / printed pp. 272-273; Fig 5.8 verified against the page image):\n    17\t- **Impact versus non-impact.** In impact plyometrics \"the rebound is stimulated by contact with a surface or object\"; in non-impact plyometrics the recoil \"is mediated by explosive eccentric action of the muscles that produce a movement not terminated by contact with a surface.\" Depth jumps are impact; \"snap kicks or rapidly-retracted blows in boxing or the martial arts\" are non-impact, which \"may also be maximal or submaximal.\"\n    18\t- **Maximal, submaximal, non-plyometric.** *Maximal plyometrics* are \"low-repetition activities where the intensity of the depth jump or rebound exercise is such that maximal or near-maximal rebound tension is produced.\" *Sub-maximal plyometrics* \"do not produce maximal muscular tension, although they are still performed with a minimally long contact phase\": \"lower box heights are used for depth jumps and the athlete does not strive to reach maximal rebound height\"; \"rapid double-footed bouncing or low skipping on the spot\" are other examples. The three are drawn in one box \"as a reminder that there is really a continuum of intensities.\"\n    19\t- **Classical, supplementary, preparatory.** Classical plyometric exercises are *functional* (\"matched as closely as possible to the specific explosive actions of the particular sport with respect to movement pattern, muscle involvement, timing and direction of motion\") or *non-functional* (\"general training of the explosive qualities\"). Functional examples: forward bounding for long jump, backward rebounds for shot put, one-legged forward hopping for jumping, and for basketball \"both short and relatively long amortisation phases in vertical rebounds.\" Preparatory or supplementary drills are weight training for muscular strength (\"especially eccentric strength\") and connective tissue, plus jumping, swinging, throwing and catching drills \"with longer duration transition phases\"; \"Most of the drills described in popular books on plyometrics in the West are actually supplementary exercises.\"\n    20\t- **Pseudo-plyometrics.** Drills \"incorrectly labelled as plyometric because they include a sudden stop or some form of jump\", e.g. \"'power moves' (involving quick landings and delayed jumps in a semi-squat position)\" and many \"'water plyos'\" in aerobics classes; lacking \"an explosive rebound phase with a short coupling time\", they serve as \"preparatory non-plyometrics.\"\n    21\t\n    22\tFig 5.8 as drawn: Plyometric training splits into impact and non-impact; below these sit maximal plyometrics, submaximal plyometrics and non-plyometrics; under maximal sits \"classical plyometrics\" (split into functional and non-functional); under submaximal sits \"supplementary plyometrics\"; under non-plyometrics sits \"preparatory/supplementary activities\" (split into weight training and \"jumps throws etc\"). The text lists classical, supplementary and preparatory as three flat categories; the figure ties each to one intensity level.\n    23\t\n    24\tNext to Fig 5.8 the text sets plyometrics against weight training through F = ma: \"Plyometrics is a system of explosive training using relatively low inertial loading, but weight training usually relies on high inertia loading. One may produce the same force by moving a heavy load with low acceleration or a light load with a large acceleration, but the training effect is very different. Explosive, low inertia training targets involuntary, neuromuscular and central nervous processes more strongly than high inertia training, which has a greater effect on static strength development and muscle hypertrophy\" (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273, verified against the page image). The text gives no study for the last sentence. Ch1 states the general equal-force point ([[principle--equal-force-different-loading-methods]]) but not this low versus high inertia split.\n    25\t\n    26\t**Why it works** — Siff's reason for classifying rather than arguing definitions: the definitional debate \"becomes complex and academic, with no real benefit to the prescription of plyometric training\" (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272). Intensity is graded by the momentum at contact (see [[heuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height]]).\n    27\t\n    28\t**When to apply** — Labelling every jump, bound, hop and med-ball drill in a plan by class, so volume and rest rules are applied to the right class: Siff's low-rep, long-rest rules are for maximal plyometrics, while submaximal and preparatory drills tolerate more volume (see [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]). Inference: in football, a vertical depth jump is classical non-functional, while a bound or single-leg hop that matches a cut or drive phase in direction and timing would be Siff's \"functional\" class.\n    29\t\n    30\t**When NOT to apply** — The text and figure place \"supplementary\" differently (a flat list of three versus a level under submaximal; and \"preparatory or supplementary drills\" in the text against \"supplementary plyometrics\" as its own box in the figure). The class boundaries are a continuum by Siff's own statement, so the labels are not sharp cut-offs.\n    31\t\n    32\t**Example** — Siff's own: depth jumps (impact, maximal when performed for maximal rebound), low box depth jumps without striving for height (submaximal), karate snap kicks (non-impact), \"power moves\" in aerobics (pseudo-plyometric).\n    33\t\n    34\t**Related** — [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]]; [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]]; [[framework--non-impact-plyometrics-ericson-and-martial-arts]]; [[claim--popular-plyometric-drills-work-through-other-mechanisms]]; [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]; [[principle--equal-force-different-loading-methods]] (Ch1, F = ma)\n=========== heuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: heuristic\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n     7\ttopic: plyometric intensity scale\n     8\tcategory: [plyometrics, intensity, load-monitoring, biomechanics]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Grade Plyometric Intensity by Momentum at Contact (Set by Drop Height), Since a 1RM Does Not Exist; Prefer Height Over Added Weight\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — The text (Siff, using his term \"powermetrics\", PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268) proposes \"a *powermetrics intensity scale* similar to that used in weightlifting, where exercises are ranked in terms of percentages of one's 1RM.\" Because \"the concept of 1RMs is meaningless in plyometric training\", one must use estimates of muscle tension or of \"the impulse ... produced during the contact phase\". \"Impulse is equivalent to the change of momentum (m.ΔV) during the amortisation phase, so that its magnitude for a falling body depends on the height from which the body drops. Thus, the momentum just before contact (estimated from height of the drop jump or distance fallen by the object) may serve as a measure of intensity.\" At the top of the scale is maximal plyometrics, \"the equivalent of the emotionally demanding competitive maximum, CFmax\"; at the bottom, preparatory non-impact plyometrics (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272, verified against the page image).\n    17\t\n    18\tThe authors' prescription list adds: \"The magnitude of the impulsive load is determined by the weight and the height of its free-fall. The optimal combination is determined empirically in each specific case; however, preference should be given to a large height over a heavy weight. Since the resulting force is governed by Newton's Second Law (F = m.a), a force plate may be used to determine the height of drop to produce the required force\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, item 1, verified against the page image). Item 4 says rep dosage can be set more precisely \"to calculate the force involved (from bodymass and the drop height) and the athlete's level of special speed-strength\" (same page).\n    19\t\n    20\tThe prescription list is introduced by Fig 5.9, \"Plyometric exercises for strength development, using resistance and other apparatus (Verkhoshansky, 1977)\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, verified against the page image). The text says it shows \"Practical application of the plyometric method to different muscle groups\" and that, with these examples \"as a guideline, an athlete of any specialisation requiring explosive strength can select the necessary complex of exercises personally\" (same page). As drawn (small figures, so descriptions are approximate): drop push-ups between raised blocks, feet on the floor; a pulley-and-weight-stack series of arm pushes and pulls, a squat pull, leg kicks and swings, an overhead pull and a seated row; incline-board exercises with an arm push and a sliding seat; and a seated kick against a suspended or wall-mounted apparatus. Inference: in the pulley drills the falling weight stack supplies the \"weight and the height of its free-fall\" of item 1, so the same height-over-weight rule applies to upper-body and trunk work.\n    21\t\n    22\t**Why it works** — Siff's reasoning is the impulse-momentum statement quoted. Inference (physics): for a body falling from rest through height h, speed at contact is √(2gh), so momentum at contact is m·√(2gh). Momentum therefore rises with the square root of drop height, not in proportion to it: doubling the drop height raises contact momentum by a factor of √2, about 41%, and quadrupling it doubles momentum. Inference (physics): the peak force on landing is not fixed by drop height alone; for the same momentum, a shorter, stiffer arrest gives a higher average force (average net force = change in momentum divided by contact time). That is why the authors' force plate step is needed to find the height that gives a target force.\n    23\t\n    24\t**When to apply** — Progressing depth jumps or rebounds: rank drills by drop height and bodymass rather than counting all \"plyos\" as equal, and raise height before adding external load. Inference: with ForceDecks, a drop jump from a known box gives landing force and contact time for each athlete, which is the measurement the authors' item 1 asks for.\n    25\t\n    26\t**When NOT to apply** — Momentum at contact is an intensity proxy for impact drills with a fall; it does not grade non-impact or ground-based bounding drills where there is no measured drop. Siff gives no numeric scale bands (no \"% of CFmax\" per height). Item 1's \"F = m.a\" sentence does not on its own let you compute landing force from height (see the Inference above). Height also has an upper limit: past the optimum the transition slows (see [[heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact]] and [[claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]]).\n    27\t\n    28\t**Example** — Inference: a 100 kg athlete stepping off a 0.5 m box lands at about √(2 × 9.81 × 0.5) ≈ 3.1 m/s, about 313 kg·m/s of momentum; from a 1.0 m box about 4.4 m/s and about 443 kg·m/s, roughly 41% more, not double. This calculation is not in the source.\n    29\t\n    30\t**Related** — [[claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]] (Ch4.2.3, the 0.75-1.15 m range); [[framework--kinetic-energy-stimulation-mechanical-basis-and-advantages]]; [[claim--thrown-load-height-decreases-with-load-increases-with-drop-height]] (Ch4.2.3, heavier loads not beneficial); [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[heuristic--rfd-as-correspondence-criterion-and-impulse-cannot-compare-movements]]\n=========== claim--lewis-formula-estimates-jump-power-from-bodymass-and-height\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: claim\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n     7\ttopic: Lewis jump power formula\n     8\tcategory: [testing, vertical-jump, power, biomechanics]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# The Lewis Formula P = 21.693 x B x √H Tracks Jump Progress (Inference: It Is Weight Times Mean Free-Fall Speed, Not Take-Off Power)\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — In the preparatory programme, the authors suggest tracking jump progress this way: \"To gain an idea of individual progress, the power developed may be calculated from the Lewis nomogram or from the formula: Power P = (21.693) B√H watts, where B = bodymass in kg and H = height jumped in metres\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277, formula read from the page image; the text layer garbles it as \"b Vh\").\n    17\t\n    18\t**Why it works** — The book gives no derivation. Inference (physics): 21.693 = 9.8 × √4.9, so the formula is P = B·g·√(g·H/2) with g = 9.8 m/s². A body falling freely from rest through height H reaches √(2gH); its mean speed during the fall is half that, √(gH/2). So the formula is body weight (B·g) times the mean speed of a body falling through H, in watts. It is a fixed function of bodymass and jump height only.\n    19\t\n    20\t**When to apply** — As the authors use it: a cheap way to see whether an individual's bodymass-scaled jump is improving over time, when only a jump height and a scale are available. Eric's baseline: jumps are tested on ForceDecks force plates (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 77, the CMJ family protocol, Inferred (protocol; author uncertain)). Inference: where force plates are available, measured propulsive power replaces this estimate; the formula is only a fallback.\n    21\t\n    22\t**When NOT to apply** — Inference: because the formula is weight times the mean speed of a free fall through H, it is not the athlete's peak or mean propulsive power during take-off, which depends on how fast force is produced over the push-off distance; two athletes with the same mass and jump height get the same value even if one pushes off much faster. Do not compare its values with force-plate peak power. The book gives no validation data for it.\n    23\t\n    24\t**Example** — Inference: a 100 kg athlete jumping 0.50 m gives P = 21.693 × 100 × √0.50 ≈ 1,534 W; at 0.55 m, ≈ 1,609 W. This arithmetic is not in the source.\n    25\t\n    26\t**Related** — [[heuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps]]; [[claim--average-or-peak-force-times-velocity-is-not-power]] (Ch4.3, the authors' own warning about crude power estimates); [[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"2bb5eee1-f192-4a66-9121-c384f64b48af","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:26:39.572Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"=========== framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: framework\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n     7\ttopic: classes of plyometric training\n     8\tcategory: [plyometrics, classification, exercise-selection]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Classes of Plyometric (Powermetric) Training: Impact or Non-Impact, Maximal to Non-Plyometric, Functional or Non-Functional (Fig 5.8)\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — The text (Siff's classification; it uses his term \"powermetrics\", PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268) replaces the \"classically plyometric or not\" argument with a classification (PDF pp. 283-284 / printed pp. 272-273; Fig 5.8 verified against the page image):\n    17\t- **Impact versus non-impact.** In impact plyometrics \"the rebound is stimulated by contact with a surface or object\"; in non-impact plyometrics the recoil \"is mediated by explosive eccentric action of the muscles that produce a movement not terminated by contact with a surface.\" Depth jumps are impact; \"snap kicks or rapidly-retracted blows in boxing or the martial arts\" are non-impact, which \"may also be maximal or submaximal.\"\n    18\t- **Maximal, submaximal, non-plyometric.** *Maximal plyometrics* are \"low-repetition activities where the intensity of the depth jump or rebound exercise is such that maximal or near-maximal rebound tension is produced.\" *Sub-maximal plyometrics* \"do not produce maximal muscular tension, although they are still performed with a minimally long contact phase\": \"lower box heights are used for depth jumps and the athlete does not strive to reach maximal rebound height\"; \"rapid double-footed bouncing or low skipping on the spot\" are other examples. The three are drawn in one box \"as a reminder that there is really a continuum of intensities.\"\n    19\t- **Classical, supplementary, preparatory.** Classical plyometric exercises are *functional* (\"matched as closely as possible to the specific explosive actions of the particular sport with respect to movement pattern, muscle involvement, timing and direction of motion\") or *non-functional* (\"general training of the explosive qualities\"). Functional examples: forward bounding for long jump, backward rebounds for shot put, one-legged forward hopping for jumping, and for basketball \"both short and relatively long amortisation phases in vertical rebounds.\" Preparatory or supplementary drills are weight training for muscular strength (\"especially eccentric strength\") and connective tissue, plus jumping, swinging, throwing and catching drills \"with longer duration transition phases\"; \"Most of the drills described in popular books on plyometrics in the West are actually supplementary exercises.\"\n    20\t- **Pseudo-plyometrics.** Drills \"incorrectly labelled as plyometric because they include a sudden stop or some form of jump\", e.g. \"'power moves' (involving quick landings and delayed jumps in a semi-squat position)\" and many \"'water plyos'\" in aerobics classes; lacking \"an explosive rebound phase with a short coupling time\", they serve as \"preparatory non-plyometrics.\"\n    21\t\n    22\tFig 5.8 as drawn: Plyometric training splits into impact and non-impact; below these sit maximal plyometrics, submaximal plyometrics and non-plyometrics; under maximal sits \"classical plyometrics\" (split into functional and non-functional); under submaximal sits \"supplementary plyometrics\"; under non-plyometrics sits \"preparatory/supplementary activities\" (split into weight training and \"jumps throws etc\"). The text lists classical, supplementary and preparatory as three flat categories; the figure ties each to one intensity level.\n    23\t\n    24\tNext to Fig 5.8 the text sets plyometrics against weight training through F = ma: \"Plyometrics is a system of explosive training using relatively low inertial loading, but weight training usually relies on high inertia loading. One may produce the same force by moving a heavy load with low acceleration or a light load with a large acceleration, but the training effect is very different. Explosive, low inertia training targets involuntary, neuromuscular and central nervous processes more strongly than high inertia training, which has a greater effect on static strength development and muscle hypertrophy\" (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273, verified against the page image). The text gives no study for the last sentence. Ch1 states the general equal-force point ([[principle--equal-force-different-loading-methods]]) but not this low versus high inertia split.\n    25\t\n    26\t**Why it works** — Siff's reason for classifying rather than arguing definitions: the definitional debate \"becomes complex and academic, with no real benefit to the prescription of plyometric training\" (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272). Intensity is graded by the momentum at contact (see [[heuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height]]).\n    27\t\n    28\t**When to apply** — Labelling every jump, bound, hop and med-ball drill in a plan by class, so volume and rest rules are applied to the right class: Siff's low-rep, long-rest rules are for maximal plyometrics, while submaximal and preparatory drills tolerate more volume (see [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]). Inference: in football, a vertical depth jump is classical non-functional, while a bound or single-leg hop that matches a cut or drive phase in direction and timing would be Siff's \"functional\" class.\n    29\t\n    30\t**When NOT to apply** — The text and figure place \"supplementary\" differently (a flat list of three versus a level under submaximal; and \"preparatory or supplementary drills\" in the text against \"supplementary plyometrics\" as its own box in the figure). The class boundaries are a continuum by Siff's own statement, so the labels are not sharp cut-offs.\n    31\t\n    32\t**Example** — Siff's own: depth jumps (impact, maximal when performed for maximal rebound), low box depth jumps without striving for height (submaximal), karate snap kicks (non-impact), \"power moves\" in aerobics (pseudo-plyometric).\n    33\t\n    34\t**Related** — [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]]; [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]]; [[framework--non-impact-plyometrics-ericson-and-martial-arts]]; [[claim--popular-plyometric-drills-work-through-other-mechanisms]]; [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]; [[principle--equal-force-different-loading-methods]] (Ch1, F = ma)\n=========== heuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: heuristic\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n     7\ttopic: plyometric intensity scale\n     8\tcategory: [plyometrics, intensity, load-monitoring, biomechanics]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Grade Plyometric Intensity by Momentum at Contact (Set by Drop Height), Since a 1RM Does Not Exist; Prefer Height Over Added Weight\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — The text (Siff, using his term \"powermetrics\", PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268) proposes \"a *powermetrics intensity scale* similar to that used in weightlifting, where exercises are ranked in terms of percentages of one's 1RM.\" Because \"the concept of 1RMs is meaningless in plyometric training\", one must use estimates of muscle tension or of \"the impulse ... produced during the contact phase\". \"Impulse is equivalent to the change of momentum (m.ΔV) during the amortisation phase, so that its magnitude for a falling body depends on the height from which the body drops. Thus, the momentum just before contact (estimated from height of the drop jump or distance fallen by the object) may serve as a measure of intensity.\" At the top of the scale is maximal plyometrics, \"the equivalent of the emotionally demanding competitive maximum, CFmax\"; at the bottom, preparatory non-impact plyometrics (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272, verified against the page image).\n    17\t\n    18\tThe authors' prescription list adds: \"The magnitude of the impulsive load is determined by the weight and the height of its free-fall. The optimal combination is determined empirically in each specific case; however, preference should be given to a large height over a heavy weight. Since the resulting force is governed by Newton's Second Law (F = m.a), a force plate may be used to determine the height of drop to produce the required force\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, item 1, verified against the page image). Item 4 says rep dosage can be set more precisely \"to calculate the force involved (from bodymass and the drop height) and the athlete's level of special speed-strength\" (same page).\n    19\t\n    20\tThe prescription list is introduced by Fig 5.9, \"Plyometric exercises for strength development, using resistance and other apparatus (Verkhoshansky, 1977)\" (PDF p. 285 / printed p. 274, verified against the page image). The text says it shows \"Practical application of the plyometric method to different muscle groups\" and that, with these examples \"as a guideline, an athlete of any specialisation requiring explosive strength can select the necessary complex of exercises personally\" (same page). As drawn (small figures, so descriptions are approximate): drop push-ups between raised blocks, feet on the floor; a pulley-and-weight-stack series of arm pushes and pulls, a squat pull, leg kicks and swings, an overhead pull and a seated row; incline-board exercises with an arm push and a sliding seat; and a seated kick against a suspended or wall-mounted apparatus. Inference: in the pulley drills the falling weight stack supplies the \"weight and the height of its free-fall\" of item 1, so the same height-over-weight rule applies to upper-body and trunk work.\n    21\t\n    22\t**Why it works** — Siff's reasoning is the impulse-momentum statement quoted. Inference (physics): for a body falling from rest through height h, speed at contact is √(2gh), so momentum at contact is m·√(2gh). Momentum therefore rises with the square root of drop height, not in proportion to it: doubling the drop height raises contact momentum by a factor of √2, about 41%, and quadrupling it doubles momentum. Inference (physics): the peak force on landing is not fixed by drop height alone; for the same momentum, a shorter, stiffer arrest gives a higher average force (average net force = change in momentum divided by contact time). That is why the authors' force plate step is needed to find the height that gives a target force.\n    23\t\n    24\t**When to apply** — Progressing depth jumps or rebounds: rank drills by drop height and bodymass rather than counting all \"plyos\" as equal, and raise height before adding external load. Inference: with ForceDecks, a drop jump from a known box gives landing force and contact time for each athlete, which is the measurement the authors' item 1 asks for.\n    25\t\n    26\t**When NOT to apply** — Momentum at contact is an intensity proxy for impact drills with a fall; it does not grade non-impact or ground-based bounding drills where there is no measured drop. Siff gives no numeric scale bands (no \"% of CFmax\" per height). Item 1's \"F = m.a\" sentence does not on its own let you compute landing force from height (see the Inference above). Height also has an upper limit: past the optimum the transition slows (see [[heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact]] and [[claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]]).\n    27\t\n    28\t**Example** — Inference: a 100 kg athlete stepping off a 0.5 m box lands at about √(2 × 9.81 × 0.5) ≈ 3.1 m/s, about 313 kg·m/s of momentum; from a 1.0 m box about 4.4 m/s and about 443 kg·m/s, roughly 41% more, not double. This calculation is not in the source.\n    29\t\n    30\t**Related** — [[claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]] (Ch4.2.3, the 0.75-1.15 m range); [[framework--kinetic-energy-stimulation-mechanical-basis-and-advantages]]; [[claim--thrown-load-height-decreases-with-load-increases-with-drop-height]] (Ch4.2.3, heavier loads not beneficial); [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[heuristic--rfd-as-correspondence-criterion-and-impulse-cannot-compare-movements]]\n=========== claim--lewis-formula-estimates-jump-power-from-bodymass-and-height\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: claim\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n     7\ttopic: Lewis jump power formula\n     8\tcategory: [testing, vertical-jump, power, biomechanics]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# The Lewis Formula P = 21.693 x B x √H Tracks Jump Progress (Inference: It Is Weight Times Mean Free-Fall Speed, Not Take-Off Power)\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — In the preparatory programme, the authors suggest tracking jump progress this way: \"To gain an idea of individual progress, the power developed may be calculated from the Lewis nomogram or from the formula: Power P = (21.693) B√H watts, where B = bodymass in kg and H = height jumped in metres\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277, formula read from the page image; the text layer garbles it as \"b Vh\").\n    17\t\n    18\t**Why it works** — The book gives no derivation. Inference (physics): 21.693 = 9.8 × √4.9, so the formula is P = B·g·√(g·H/2) with g = 9.8 m/s². A body falling freely from rest through height H reaches √(2gH); its mean speed during the fall is half that, √(gH/2). So the formula is body weight (B·g) times the mean speed of a body falling through H, in watts. It is a fixed function of bodymass and jump height only.\n    19\t\n    20\t**When to apply** — As the authors use it: a cheap way to see whether an individual's bodymass-scaled jump is improving over time, when only a jump height and a scale are available. Eric's baseline: jumps are tested on ForceDecks force plates (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 77, the CMJ family protocol, Inferred (protocol; author uncertain)). Inference: where force plates are available, measured propulsive power replaces this estimate; the formula is only a fallback.\n    21\t\n    22\t**When NOT to apply** — Inference: because the formula is weight times the mean speed of a free fall through H, it is not the athlete's peak or mean propulsive power during take-off, which depends on how fast force is produced over the push-off distance; two athletes with the same mass and jump height get the same value even if one pushes off much faster. Do not compare its values with force-plate peak power. The book gives no validation data for it.\n    23\t\n    24\t**Example** — Inference: a 100 kg athlete jumping 0.50 m gives P = 21.693 × 100 × √0.50 ≈ 1,534 W; at 0.55 m, ≈ 1,609 W. This arithmetic is not in the source.\n    25\t\n    26\t**Related** — [[heuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps]]; [[claim--average-or-peak-force-times-velocity-is-not-power]] (Ch4.3, the authors' own warning about crude power estimates); [[framework--strength-deficit-jump-comparison-estimate]]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a8da87ce-85f0-4f51-a683-49b786552eb2","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
