{"parentUuid":"914d5c56-cb57-4e27-87fd-888d2fdf76a6","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac44742970c7341bd","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffcjqBgo5U7FcgooQjKQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019shu6UwoiAeCtzNYsEmkbx","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/; for f in claim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method heuristic--resisted-and-water-plyometrics claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task; do echo \"=========== $f\"; cat -n $f.md; done","description":"Read last three edited entries"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6487,"cache_read_input_tokens":96830,"output_tokens":403,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":185},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6487},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":403,"cache_read_input_tokens":96830,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6487,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6487,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_019shu6UwoiAeCtzNYsEmkbx":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/; for f in claim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method heuristic--resisted-and-water-plyometrics claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task; do echo \"=========== $f\"; cat -n $f.md; done","description":"Read last three edited entries"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffcjptL1Ms6pgw64xENC","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"d385638f-3e65-479d-b729-4ed1382d31b8","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:26:43.507Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"d385638f-3e65-479d-b729-4ed1382d31b8","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"0b81aa46-3ac1-41a7-bee9-ff3aba05ec92","agentId":"ac44742970c7341bd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019shu6UwoiAeCtzNYsEmkbx","type":"tool_result","content":"=========== claim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: claim\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n     7\ttopic: plyometric safety\n     8\tcategory: [plyometrics, injury-risk, cartilage, overtraining]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Plyometric Injury Risk Comes From Prescription (Overload and Overuse), Not the Method; Regularly Impact-Loaded Cartilage Is Reported as Healthier\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — In \"Plyometric Training and Safety\" (PDF pp. 280-281 / printed pp. 269-270; both verified against the page images) the text argues:\n    17\t- The claim that \"plyometric drills on their own are dangerous and useless has not been proved experimentally or clinically\"; the danger is \"inferred from the magnitude of the impulsive peak forces which have been measured via force plate research\", and an experiment isolating plyometric effects with matched controls \"is very difficult to design.\"\n    18\t- Opponents imply running and jumping \"in the context of normal sport are perfectly safe, but in the context of special plyometric drills are dangerous\", and assume joints do not follow SAID (Specific Adaptation to Imposed Demands).\n    19\t- Cartilage: \"joints subjected to heavy impact are relatively free from osteoarthritis in old age and ... those subjected to much lower loading experience a greater incidence of osteoarthritis and cartilage fibrillation (Bullough et al, 1973; Kempson et al, 1975; Seedhom & Swann, 1985; Seedhom & Wright, 1988; Seedhom et al, 1977).\" Siff's former colleague Mark Swanepoel (University of the Witwatersrand) noted that fibrillation at any age increases going up from ankle to knee, hip and lumbar spine. Regularly impulse-loaded cartilage is \"mechanically much stiffer\", while cartilage \"heavily loaded infrequently softens due to decrease in proteoglycan production\" (Swanepoel, 1998).\n    20\t- \"it is not the inherent nature of plyometrics which may produce injury, but the manner in which it is used.\" Western practice earns much of the criticism: \"encyclopaedic compilations of drills offered in extended, isolated and highly repetitive forms\"; drills often prescribed without regard to \"loading imposed by other work or play ... or the use of formal restoration\"; and \"the athlete's sport alone may offer all or most of the plyometric training that is necessary.\"\n    21\t- \"overtraining comprises two distinct components: overload (the imposition of too great a force at any given time) and overuse (the imposition of too great a volume of exercise).\" An \"individually appropriate volume and intensity (e.g. via choice of drop height)\" with \"gradual progressive overload\" and \"adequ[ate] restoration regimes need pose no greater risks than the naturally occurring plyometric actions found in running a[nd] jumping\" (PDF pp. 280-281 / printed pp. 269-270; the right margin is cut in the scan on PDF p. 281, verified against the page image, so the bracketed letters are not printed).\n    22\t- On wording: \"it might be preferable to avoid use of the popular te[rm] plyometric *drills*, because drills tend to be associated with militarism and authoritarianism rather than wi[th] individual flow, sensitivity, flair and nuances of personal interpretation\" (PDF p. 281 / printed p. 270, verified against the page image; the bracketed letters are cut at the right margin). Voice: Siff, as for the rest of this passage.\n    23\t\n    24\tVoice: Siff (the passage names \"Siff's former colleague\").\n    25\t\n    26\t**Why it works** — The stated mechanism is adaptation (SAID) and the cartilage findings cited. Inference: overload maps to drop height and rebound effort, overuse to contacts per session and week, which gives two separate dials to manage.\n    27\t\n    28\t**When to apply** — Answering \"are depth jumps dangerous?\" for coaches or medical staff: the text's position is that risk is a dose question. Inference: in football, count the plyometric loading already present in practice (cutting, jumping, sprinting) before adding drills, as the text's \"sport alone may offer all or most\" warning suggests.\n    29\t\n    30\t**When NOT to apply** — The text argues mainly from absence of proof and from cartilage studies of everyday joint loading; it cites no controlled study showing plyometric drills are safe. The \"heavy impact joints are free from osteoarthritis\" sentence compares joints and loading histories, not plyometric training programs, so do not cite it as evidence that depth jumps protect joints. The cited studies were not checked for this entry.\n    31\t\n    32\t**Example** — The text's own: the Nicol et al (1997) finding, that many repetitions do not improve fatigue resistance in basketball rebounding, as an example of a Western volume habit that is \"untrue\" (PDF p. 280 / printed p. 269; see [[claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance]]).\n    33\t\n    34\t**Related** — [[claim--collagen-loading-curve-and-injury-risk]]; [[claim--differential-tissue-adaptation-rates]]; [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]; [[claim--squat-1-5-to-2-bodymass-rule-applies-to-maximal-depth-jumps-only]]\n=========== heuristic--resisted-and-water-plyometrics\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: heuristic\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n     7\ttopic: resisted and aquatic plyometrics\n     8\tcategory: [plyometrics, resisted-training, aquatic-training, safety]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Resisted Plyometrics Only Occasionally and Never With Heavy Weights or Stiff Cords in Depth Jumps; Water Plyometrics Are a Safer Submaximal Option\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — Under \"Resisted and Water Plyometric Training\" (PDF pp. 292-293 / printed pp. 281-282; both verified against the page images):\n    17\t- Resistance can be imposed in the amortisation phase, the concentric rebound or both: light weights, or \"elastic cords attached to a harness located near the waist or hips.\" Both \"increase the landing impact and ostensibly increase the strength of reflex muscle contraction\", but \"also increase the risk of impact and overload injuries and should be applied occasionally with great caution. Even normal running or hurdling with very light weights constitutes a form of loaded plyometric training.\"\n    18\t- \"No resistance methods should not use heavy weights or stiff elastic cords during depth jumps, since this increases the coupling time and imposes unnecessarily large loads on the spine and the tendons of the lower extremities, especially during the landing or contact phase\" (sic: the sentence runs across the page break as \"No resistance / methods should not use\", PDF pp. 292-293 / printed pp. 281-282. Inference: the intended sense is \"should not\").\n    19\t- Water or \"aqua plyometrics\" decrease landing force and increase resistance in the recoil. They do \"not offer maximal or shock method plyometrics\" but serve as preparatory or submaximal plyometrics, \"especially for one-legged drills.\" Water depth sets the resistance: \"chest or shoulder high water offering greater resistance during the landing and take-off phases, less intense eccentric muscle activity, smaller impact forces and enhanced safety.\" Repeated aqua jumps for \"rebound explosive muscle endurance\" are \"considerably safer than their land-based equivalents, though they should not completely replace the latter, since they do not adequately develop the specific neuromuscular patterns or functional needs of explosive sports.\"\n    20\t\n    21\t**Why it works** — The authors' reasons are stated: heavy load lengthens the coupling time (taking the drill out of classical plyometrics) and loads spine and tendons; water reduces impact and eccentric intensity.\n    22\t\n    23\t**When to apply** — Inference: aqua jumps suit early return-to-training or single-leg plyometric progressions where impact must be limited, consistent with the authors' \"especially for one-legged drills.\" Light loads during running or hurdling are already loaded plyometrics, per the authors, so they belong in the plyometric count.\n    24\t\n    25\t**When NOT to apply** — Not a replacement for land plyometrics for explosive sport (the authors' own limit). \"Light\" and \"heavy\" weights and \"stiff\" cords are not quantified. The \"water plyos\" in aerobics classes the authors call pseudo-plyometric (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273) differ from these structured aqua drills; do not conflate them.\n    26\t\n    27\t**Example** — The authors' own: chest- or shoulder-high water for one-legged preparatory jumps (PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282).\n    28\t\n    29\t**Related** — [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[claim--thrown-load-height-decreases-with-load-increases-with-drop-height]] (Ch4.2.3, heavier loads not beneficial for kinetic-energy work); [[heuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus]]; [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]]\n=========== claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: claim\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n     7\ttopic: decay of the prestretch benefit\n     8\tcategory: [stretch-shortening-cycle, coupling-time, research-conflict]\n     9\tconfidence: contested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# How Long a Prestretch Helps Varies From About 0.25 s to About 4 s by Task, and the Book's Own Figure Attribution Conflicts\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — After stating the about 0.15 s coupling guide, the authors say it \"requires some qualification\" and list conflicting results (PDF pp. 281-282 / printed pp. 270-271; Figs 5.6 and 5.7 verified against the page image):\n    17\t- Wilson et al (1990), bench press: \"the benefits of prior stretch may endure for as long as 4 seconds, at which stage it is suggested that all stored elastic energy is lost (see Fig 5.6).\"\n    18\t- Chapman and Caldwell (1985), forearm movement: benefits \"dissipated within 0.25 second, a figure which agrees with Siff's analysis of explosive rebound elbow flexion without additional loading (Fig 5.7).\"\n    19\t- Wilson et al (1991), rebound action of the chest and arms: \"no benefits of prior stretching are evident after 0.37 second.\"\n    20\t\n    21\tSummary in the text: \"delays of as long as a second or two can still produce significant augmentation of the subsequent concentric phase for some activities, but delays as short as 0.2 second are sufficient to dissipate the benefits of prior stretch during other activities.\"\n    22\t\n    23\tFigure readings (approximate, from the image). Fig 5.6, \"% decay\" against delay 0-5 s: rises steeply from 0 near 0.1-0.2 s, roughly half decayed near 1 s, about 80% by 2 s, near 100% at about 4 s. Fig 5.7, unloaded elbow flexion, delay 0.1-0.25 s: close to linear from 0% at 0.1 s to 100% at 0.25 s.\n    24\t\n    25\t**Internal conflict, recorded:** the Fig 5.6 caption reads \"based on the data of Wilson et al, 1991\", but the text gives the 4 s bench-press result to Wilson et al (1990) and the 0.37 s chest/arms result to Wilson et al (1991) (PDF pp. 281-282 / printed pp. 270-271). The curve's near-total decay at about 4 s matches the text's 1990 sentence, not the 0.37 s result. Which year is right is not resolvable from the book.\n    26\t\n    27\tSource note: Wilson, G.J., Elliott, B.C. & Wood, G.A. (1991). The effect on performance of imposing a delay during a stretch-shorten cycle movement. *Medicine and Science in Sports and Exercise*, 23(3), 364-370 (abstract checked on the journal's page, journals.lww.com). Its abstract reports twelve experienced male weight lifters doing bench press lifts at 95% of maximum, with pauses imposed between the downward and upward phases; the benefit of prior stretch decayed with pause duration, fitted by \"a negative exponential equation with a half-life of 0.85 s.\" The abstract does not give the 4 s figure. Inference: the 0.85 s half-life matches the Fig 5.6 curve (about half decayed near 1 s), so this 1991 paper is probably the bench-press study, which would make the caption's 1991 right and the text's \"1990\" wrong. The 0.37 s chest-and-arms result the text gives to Wilson et al (1991) was not checked.\n    28\t\n    29\tThe authors' partial resolutions:\n    30\t- Fibre type (Bosco et al, 1982a): high-FT individuals show the maximum plyometric effect with a short eccentric phase, small range and brief coupling; high-ST individuals jump best with a longer eccentric phase, greater range and longer coupling, \"since the actin-myosin cross-bridging attachment time is of greater duration\" (PDF p. 282 / printed p. 271).\n    31\t- Joint-specific maximum delays \"probably\" exist, but the body has many reflexes acting \"at different rates\"; tonic (static) and phasic (dynamic) stretch reflexes differ (PDF p. 282 / printed p. 271).\n    32\t- The weightlifters' \"pre-stretch principle\" (hold an intense stretch \"for a couple of seconds and then thrust\") \"would seem\" to involve the more tonic reflex with longer coupling, and \"could certainly not be called plyometric\", while phasic reflexes are \"more likely\" behind classical plyometrics (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272, verified against the page image).\n    33\t\n    34\t**Why it works** — The authors offer fibre type and tonic versus phasic reflexes as explanations, with hedges (\"partial solution\", \"would seem\", \"more likely\"). They do not test either against the conflicting delay data.\n    35\t\n    36\t**When to apply** — When someone quotes one fixed \"the stretch benefit is gone after X ms\" number: the book shows the window depends on the task (unloaded elbow about 0.25 s; loaded bench press up to about 4 s). Inference: for loaded lifts a paused rep still keeps some prestretch benefit for a second or more, per Fig 5.6, while for unloaded fast rebounds the benefit is gone within a few tenths of a second, per Fig 5.7.\n    37\t\n    38\t**When NOT to apply** — Do not cite Fig 5.6 under a single Wilson year without noting the caption-text conflict. The figure values are read from small plots and are approximate. Fig 5.7 is Siff's own analysis: the book says it \"was derived from some of this work on upper-body non-impact plyometrics\", the research \"conducted in this field by the author in 1994\" (PDF p. 292 / printed p. 281), and gives no subject numbers or publication for it. The fibre-type and reflex explanations are proposals, not demonstrated here.\n    39\t\n    40\t**Example** — The authors' own: unloaded explosive elbow flexion loses its prestretch benefit over 0.1-0.25 s (Fig 5.7), while the bench-press benefit decays over about 4 s (Fig 5.6).\n    41\t\n    42\t**Related** — [[heuristic--match-plyometric-prescription-to-joint-angle-and-fiber-type]] (Ch1.7, the same Bosco et al 1982 fibre-type finding); [[principle--short-amortization-preserves-elastic-energy]]; [[framework--stretch-reflex-types-and-neuromuscular-control-of-flexibility]]; [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]]","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"85eef5f8-7a7d-4315-b4dc-2e45fcd062f7","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:26:43.743Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"=========== claim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: claim\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n     7\ttopic: plyometric safety\n     8\tcategory: [plyometrics, injury-risk, cartilage, overtraining]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Plyometric Injury Risk Comes From Prescription (Overload and Overuse), Not the Method; Regularly Impact-Loaded Cartilage Is Reported as Healthier\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — In \"Plyometric Training and Safety\" (PDF pp. 280-281 / printed pp. 269-270; both verified against the page images) the text argues:\n    17\t- The claim that \"plyometric drills on their own are dangerous and useless has not been proved experimentally or clinically\"; the danger is \"inferred from the magnitude of the impulsive peak forces which have been measured via force plate research\", and an experiment isolating plyometric effects with matched controls \"is very difficult to design.\"\n    18\t- Opponents imply running and jumping \"in the context of normal sport are perfectly safe, but in the context of special plyometric drills are dangerous\", and assume joints do not follow SAID (Specific Adaptation to Imposed Demands).\n    19\t- Cartilage: \"joints subjected to heavy impact are relatively free from osteoarthritis in old age and ... those subjected to much lower loading experience a greater incidence of osteoarthritis and cartilage fibrillation (Bullough et al, 1973; Kempson et al, 1975; Seedhom & Swann, 1985; Seedhom & Wright, 1988; Seedhom et al, 1977).\" Siff's former colleague Mark Swanepoel (University of the Witwatersrand) noted that fibrillation at any age increases going up from ankle to knee, hip and lumbar spine. Regularly impulse-loaded cartilage is \"mechanically much stiffer\", while cartilage \"heavily loaded infrequently softens due to decrease in proteoglycan production\" (Swanepoel, 1998).\n    20\t- \"it is not the inherent nature of plyometrics which may produce injury, but the manner in which it is used.\" Western practice earns much of the criticism: \"encyclopaedic compilations of drills offered in extended, isolated and highly repetitive forms\"; drills often prescribed without regard to \"loading imposed by other work or play ... or the use of formal restoration\"; and \"the athlete's sport alone may offer all or most of the plyometric training that is necessary.\"\n    21\t- \"overtraining comprises two distinct components: overload (the imposition of too great a force at any given time) and overuse (the imposition of too great a volume of exercise).\" An \"individually appropriate volume and intensity (e.g. via choice of drop height)\" with \"gradual progressive overload\" and \"adequ[ate] restoration regimes need pose no greater risks than the naturally occurring plyometric actions found in running a[nd] jumping\" (PDF pp. 280-281 / printed pp. 269-270; the right margin is cut in the scan on PDF p. 281, verified against the page image, so the bracketed letters are not printed).\n    22\t- On wording: \"it might be preferable to avoid use of the popular te[rm] plyometric *drills*, because drills tend to be associated with militarism and authoritarianism rather than wi[th] individual flow, sensitivity, flair and nuances of personal interpretation\" (PDF p. 281 / printed p. 270, verified against the page image; the bracketed letters are cut at the right margin). Voice: Siff, as for the rest of this passage.\n    23\t\n    24\tVoice: Siff (the passage names \"Siff's former colleague\").\n    25\t\n    26\t**Why it works** — The stated mechanism is adaptation (SAID) and the cartilage findings cited. Inference: overload maps to drop height and rebound effort, overuse to contacts per session and week, which gives two separate dials to manage.\n    27\t\n    28\t**When to apply** — Answering \"are depth jumps dangerous?\" for coaches or medical staff: the text's position is that risk is a dose question. Inference: in football, count the plyometric loading already present in practice (cutting, jumping, sprinting) before adding drills, as the text's \"sport alone may offer all or most\" warning suggests.\n    29\t\n    30\t**When NOT to apply** — The text argues mainly from absence of proof and from cartilage studies of everyday joint loading; it cites no controlled study showing plyometric drills are safe. The \"heavy impact joints are free from osteoarthritis\" sentence compares joints and loading histories, not plyometric training programs, so do not cite it as evidence that depth jumps protect joints. The cited studies were not checked for this entry.\n    31\t\n    32\t**Example** — The text's own: the Nicol et al (1997) finding, that many repetitions do not improve fatigue resistance in basketball rebounding, as an example of a Western volume habit that is \"untrue\" (PDF p. 280 / printed p. 269; see [[claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance]]).\n    33\t\n    34\t**Related** — [[claim--collagen-loading-curve-and-injury-risk]]; [[claim--differential-tissue-adaptation-rates]]; [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]; [[claim--squat-1-5-to-2-bodymass-rule-applies-to-maximal-depth-jumps-only]]\n=========== heuristic--resisted-and-water-plyometrics\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: heuristic\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n     7\ttopic: resisted and aquatic plyometrics\n     8\tcategory: [plyometrics, resisted-training, aquatic-training, safety]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Resisted Plyometrics Only Occasionally and Never With Heavy Weights or Stiff Cords in Depth Jumps; Water Plyometrics Are a Safer Submaximal Option\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — Under \"Resisted and Water Plyometric Training\" (PDF pp. 292-293 / printed pp. 281-282; both verified against the page images):\n    17\t- Resistance can be imposed in the amortisation phase, the concentric rebound or both: light weights, or \"elastic cords attached to a harness located near the waist or hips.\" Both \"increase the landing impact and ostensibly increase the strength of reflex muscle contraction\", but \"also increase the risk of impact and overload injuries and should be applied occasionally with great caution. Even normal running or hurdling with very light weights constitutes a form of loaded plyometric training.\"\n    18\t- \"No resistance methods should not use heavy weights or stiff elastic cords during depth jumps, since this increases the coupling time and imposes unnecessarily large loads on the spine and the tendons of the lower extremities, especially during the landing or contact phase\" (sic: the sentence runs across the page break as \"No resistance / methods should not use\", PDF pp. 292-293 / printed pp. 281-282. Inference: the intended sense is \"should not\").\n    19\t- Water or \"aqua plyometrics\" decrease landing force and increase resistance in the recoil. They do \"not offer maximal or shock method plyometrics\" but serve as preparatory or submaximal plyometrics, \"especially for one-legged drills.\" Water depth sets the resistance: \"chest or shoulder high water offering greater resistance during the landing and take-off phases, less intense eccentric muscle activity, smaller impact forces and enhanced safety.\" Repeated aqua jumps for \"rebound explosive muscle endurance\" are \"considerably safer than their land-based equivalents, though they should not completely replace the latter, since they do not adequately develop the specific neuromuscular patterns or functional needs of explosive sports.\"\n    20\t\n    21\t**Why it works** — The authors' reasons are stated: heavy load lengthens the coupling time (taking the drill out of classical plyometrics) and loads spine and tendons; water reduces impact and eccentric intensity.\n    22\t\n    23\t**When to apply** — Inference: aqua jumps suit early return-to-training or single-leg plyometric progressions where impact must be limited, consistent with the authors' \"especially for one-legged drills.\" Light loads during running or hurdling are already loaded plyometrics, per the authors, so they belong in the plyometric count.\n    24\t\n    25\t**When NOT to apply** — Not a replacement for land plyometrics for explosive sport (the authors' own limit). \"Light\" and \"heavy\" weights and \"stiff\" cords are not quantified. The \"water plyos\" in aerobics classes the authors call pseudo-plyometric (PDF p. 284 / printed p. 273) differ from these structured aqua drills; do not conflate them.\n    26\t\n    27\t**Example** — The authors' own: chest- or shoulder-high water for one-legged preparatory jumps (PDF p. 293 / printed p. 282).\n    28\t\n    29\t**Related** — [[framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function]]; [[claim--thrown-load-height-decreases-with-load-increases-with-drop-height]] (Ch4.2.3, heavier loads not beneficial for kinetic-energy work); [[heuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus]]; [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]]\n=========== claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: claim\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"5.2.4\"\n     7\ttopic: decay of the prestretch benefit\n     8\tcategory: [stretch-shortening-cycle, coupling-time, research-conflict]\n     9\tconfidence: contested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# How Long a Prestretch Helps Varies From About 0.25 s to About 4 s by Task, and the Book's Own Figure Attribution Conflicts\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — After stating the about 0.15 s coupling guide, the authors say it \"requires some qualification\" and list conflicting results (PDF pp. 281-282 / printed pp. 270-271; Figs 5.6 and 5.7 verified against the page image):\n    17\t- Wilson et al (1990), bench press: \"the benefits of prior stretch may endure for as long as 4 seconds, at which stage it is suggested that all stored elastic energy is lost (see Fig 5.6).\"\n    18\t- Chapman and Caldwell (1985), forearm movement: benefits \"dissipated within 0.25 second, a figure which agrees with Siff's analysis of explosive rebound elbow flexion without additional loading (Fig 5.7).\"\n    19\t- Wilson et al (1991), rebound action of the chest and arms: \"no benefits of prior stretching are evident after 0.37 second.\"\n    20\t\n    21\tSummary in the text: \"delays of as long as a second or two can still produce significant augmentation of the subsequent concentric phase for some activities, but delays as short as 0.2 second are sufficient to dissipate the benefits of prior stretch during other activities.\"\n    22\t\n    23\tFigure readings (approximate, from the image). Fig 5.6, \"% decay\" against delay 0-5 s: rises steeply from 0 near 0.1-0.2 s, roughly half decayed near 1 s, about 80% by 2 s, near 100% at about 4 s. Fig 5.7, unloaded elbow flexion, delay 0.1-0.25 s: close to linear from 0% at 0.1 s to 100% at 0.25 s.\n    24\t\n    25\t**Internal conflict, recorded:** the Fig 5.6 caption reads \"based on the data of Wilson et al, 1991\", but the text gives the 4 s bench-press result to Wilson et al (1990) and the 0.37 s chest/arms result to Wilson et al (1991) (PDF pp. 281-282 / printed pp. 270-271). The curve's near-total decay at about 4 s matches the text's 1990 sentence, not the 0.37 s result. Which year is right is not resolvable from the book.\n    26\t\n    27\tSource note: Wilson, G.J., Elliott, B.C. & Wood, G.A. (1991). The effect on performance of imposing a delay during a stretch-shorten cycle movement. *Medicine and Science in Sports and Exercise*, 23(3), 364-370 (abstract checked on the journal's page, journals.lww.com). Its abstract reports twelve experienced male weight lifters doing bench press lifts at 95% of maximum, with pauses imposed between the downward and upward phases; the benefit of prior stretch decayed with pause duration, fitted by \"a negative exponential equation with a half-life of 0.85 s.\" The abstract does not give the 4 s figure. Inference: the 0.85 s half-life matches the Fig 5.6 curve (about half decayed near 1 s), so this 1991 paper is probably the bench-press study, which would make the caption's 1991 right and the text's \"1990\" wrong. The 0.37 s chest-and-arms result the text gives to Wilson et al (1991) was not checked.\n    28\t\n    29\tThe authors' partial resolutions:\n    30\t- Fibre type (Bosco et al, 1982a): high-FT individuals show the maximum plyometric effect with a short eccentric phase, small range and brief coupling; high-ST individuals jump best with a longer eccentric phase, greater range and longer coupling, \"since the actin-myosin cross-bridging attachment time is of greater duration\" (PDF p. 282 / printed p. 271).\n    31\t- Joint-specific maximum delays \"probably\" exist, but the body has many reflexes acting \"at different rates\"; tonic (static) and phasic (dynamic) stretch reflexes differ (PDF p. 282 / printed p. 271).\n    32\t- The weightlifters' \"pre-stretch principle\" (hold an intense stretch \"for a couple of seconds and then thrust\") \"would seem\" to involve the more tonic reflex with longer coupling, and \"could certainly not be called plyometric\", while phasic reflexes are \"more likely\" behind classical plyometrics (PDF p. 283 / printed p. 272, verified against the page image).\n    33\t\n    34\t**Why it works** — The authors offer fibre type and tonic versus phasic reflexes as explanations, with hedges (\"partial solution\", \"would seem\", \"more likely\"). They do not test either against the conflicting delay data.\n    35\t\n    36\t**When to apply** — When someone quotes one fixed \"the stretch benefit is gone after X ms\" number: the book shows the window depends on the task (unloaded elbow about 0.25 s; loaded bench press up to about 4 s). Inference: for loaded lifts a paused rep still keeps some prestretch benefit for a second or more, per Fig 5.6, while for unloaded fast rebounds the benefit is gone within a few tenths of a second, per Fig 5.7.\n    37\t\n    38\t**When NOT to apply** — Do not cite Fig 5.6 under a single Wilson year without noting the caption-text conflict. The figure values are read from small plots and are approximate. Fig 5.7 is Siff's own analysis: the book says it \"was derived from some of this work on upper-body non-impact plyometrics\", the research \"conducted in this field by the author in 1994\" (PDF p. 292 / printed p. 281), and gives no subject numbers or publication for it. The fibre-type and reflex explanations are proposals, not demonstrated here.\n    39\t\n    40\t**Example** — The authors' own: unloaded explosive elbow flexion loses its prestretch benefit over 0.1-0.25 s (Fig 5.7), while the bench-press benefit decays over about 4 s (Fig 5.6).\n    41\t\n    42\t**Related** — [[heuristic--match-plyometric-prescription-to-joint-angle-and-fiber-type]] (Ch1.7, the same Bosco et al 1982 fibre-type finding); [[principle--short-amortization-preserves-elastic-energy]]; [[framework--stretch-reflex-types-and-neuromuscular-control-of-flexibility]]; [[heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern]]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d385638f-3e65-479d-b729-4ed1382d31b8","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
