d[n].get_pixmap(dpi=130).save(f'_renders/r2_5b_p{n}.png')\nprint('ok')\n\"; wc -l _tmp/r2_5b_text.txt","description":"Render pages and extract text layer"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cffcm4JkzdftucW4H7Th2","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"f8017e8c-84cc-4f2c-9d80-254706ae1e30","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:27:02.111Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"f8017e8c-84cc-4f2c-9d80-254706ae1e30","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"0b81aa46-3ac1-41a7-bee9-ff3aba05ec92","agentId":"ac44742970c7341bd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018Uhd3QsnmZXUa1sgWNPvAP","type":"tool_result","content":"ok\n    1164 _tmp/r2_5b_text.txt","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"2c20ed91-0ed1-429a-895a-1753efd1a772","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:27:09.928Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"ok\n    1164 _tmp/r2_5b_text.txt","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--deniskin-depth-jumps-after-resistance-and-rest-as-a-training-means.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":13,"oldLines":7,"newStart":13,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," # Deniskin (1976): A Resistance Stage, Then Two Depth-Jump Stages, With Complete Rest Between Them"," ","-**What it is** — \"qualified athletes modelled different variants of weightlifting pre-competition training under laboratory conditions (Deniskin, 1976).\" The test movement was \"A leg extension movement involving the lifting of a 1RM ... starting with an initial knee angle of 90°.\" Stage 1 was \"intensive work with resistance, the purpose of which was to increase explosive strength.\" \"Only depth-jumps were used during the second and third stages.\" Two depth-jump stages were used because \"a preliminary investigation ... showed that the rise in functional state achieved after one stage is unstable.\" \"The fourth stage served as a control and served only to test performance.\" \"The subjects were given a complete rest between stages to enhance supercompensation\" (PDF p. 301 / printed p. 290). Fig 5.14 (verified against the page image) lays the stages out by week: resistance weeks 1-7, rest week 8, depth jumps weeks 9-15, rest week 16, depth jumps weeks 17-21, rest week 22, testing weeks 23-24. It plots Fmax (kgf), tmax (s, time to Fmax), starting strength (Ss) and explosive strength (Se). Read by eye from the image: Fmax rises from about 103 to about 110 kgf in stage 1 and reaches about 125 kgf by stage 4; tmax falls from about 0.42 s to about 0.27-0.32 s. Ss and Se jump after each rest week (weeks 8 and 16): Ss from about 500 at the end of stage 1 to about 565 at the start of stage 2, and from about 610 to about 665 into stage 3; Se from about 270 to about 330, and from about 400 to about 430. Both are at their highest early in stage 3 (Ss peaks at about 685 in week 19; Se is highest at about 430 in week 17). Both then decline through stage 3 (Ss to about 625, Se to about 380 by week 21) and sit below that peak at the stage-4 test (Ss about 580-625, Se about 375-400). Ss and Se values are read by eye from the image; the axis carries numbers but no unit.","+**What it is** — \"qualified athletes modelled different variants of weightlifting pre-competition training under laboratory conditions (Deniskin, 1976).\" The test movement was \"A leg extension movement involving the lifting of a 1RM ... starting with an initial knee angle of 90°.\" Stage 1 was \"intensive work with resistance, the purpose of which was to increase explosive strength.\" \"Only depth-jumps were used during the second and third stages.\" Two depth-jump stages were used because \"a preliminary investigation ... showed that the rise in functional state achieved after one stage is unstable.\" \"The fourth stage served as a control and served only to test performance.\" \"The subjects were given a complete rest between stages to enhance supercompensation\" (PDF p. 301 / printed p. 290). Fig 5.14 (verified against the page image) lays the stages out by week: resistance weeks 1-7, rest week 8, depth jumps weeks 9-15, rest week 16, depth jumps weeks 17-21, rest week 22, testing weeks 23-24. It plots Fmax (kgf), tmax (s, time to Fmax), starting strength (Ss) and explosive strength (Se). Read by eye from the image: Fmax rises from about 103 to about 110 kgf in stage 1 and reaches about 125 kgf by stage 4; tmax falls from about 0.42 s to about 0.27-0.32 s. Ss and Se jump after each rest week (weeks 8 and 16): Ss from about 500 at the end of stage 1 to about 565 at the start of stage 2, and from about 610 to about 665 into stage 3; Se from about 280 to about 340, and from about 400 to about 430. Both are at their highest early in stage 3 (Ss peaks at about 685 in week 19; Se is highest at about 430 in week 17). Both then decline through stage 3 (Ss to about 625, Se to about 380 by week 21) and sit below that peak at the stage-4 test (Ss about 580-625, Se about 375-400). Ss and Se values are read by eye from the image; the axis carries numbers but no unit."," "," **Why it works** — The authors' conclusion: \"the plyometric method produces an additional, significant increase in the level of explosive strength that was first attained with resistance training,\" which shows \"the use of these means in two appropriate stages: the first stage, which improves explosive strength, and the second stage, which stabilises the level of special fitness.\" On rest: \"There was a significant increase in all the control indicators after a period of complete rest following the first and second stages. This emphasizes that rest may be used as a training means. This is not a paradox, because growth and maintenance processes take place predominantly during sleep and other forms of passive or active rest\" (PDF p. 301 / printed p. 290). That sentence is the authors' mechanism; they cite nothing for it."," "]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--deniskin-depth-jumps-after-resistance-and-rest-as-a-training-means.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f8017e8c-84cc-4f2c-9d80-254706ae1e30","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
