{"parentUuid":"db14561b-10b0-4423-853a-e842ffd0d689","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac44742970c7341bd","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffcuERC5BAboDK2uqyiC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016KbEZ1nNtaFWbpUAGLNpKw","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/; for f in case-study--soviet-sport-specific-strength-endurance-prescriptions claim--zatsiorsky-and-hansen-show-strength-endurance-training-specificity heuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement; do echo \"=========== $f\"; sed -n '14,40p' $f.md; done","description":"Read three GO entries for spot check"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6048,"cache_read_input_tokens":176459,"output_tokens":939,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":668},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6048},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":939,"cache_read_input_tokens":176459,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6048,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6048,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_016KbEZ1nNtaFWbpUAGLNpKw":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/; for f in case-study--soviet-sport-specific-strength-endurance-prescriptions claim--zatsiorsky-and-hansen-show-strength-endurance-training-specificity heuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement; do echo \"=========== $f\"; sed -n '14,40p' $f.md; done","description":"Read three GO entries for spot check"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cffcu4ughYh87KW54SP35","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"99a9ba65-4211-42a5-b16f-effb4ad554e8","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:28:57.152Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"99a9ba65-4211-42a5-b16f-effb4ad554e8","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"0b81aa46-3ac1-41a7-bee9-ff3aba05ec92","agentId":"ac44742970c7341bd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016KbEZ1nNtaFWbpUAGLNpKw","type":"tool_result","content":"=========== case-study--soviet-sport-specific-strength-endurance-prescriptions\n# Sport-Specific Strength-Endurance Prescriptions: Skiers, Distance Runners, Skaters and Rowers\n\n**What it is** — The authors' examples of applying the general strength-endurance principles \"differently in each specific case\" (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286, verified against the page image):\n- **Skiers (Chistyakov, 1965):** in the preparatory period, weights \"up to 65% of 1RM\", combined with simulation exercises on a flat surface and roller-ski movements.\n- **Middle and long distance runners (Likachyevskaya & Kovalchuk, 1963):** a barbell of \"55-60% of bodymass\" lifted to head level \"8-10 times rhythmically\"; clean-and-jerks with \"60-85% of bodymass\"; kettlebell jumps from a squat with \"about one-third of bodymass for 18-20 repetitions\"; squats with \"55-60% of bodymass until complete fatigue.\" Also (Popov, 1966): \"60-80% of 1RM for multiple repetitions, jumping and hard running.\"\n- **Skaters (Mikhailov & Panov, 1975):** heavy weights \"(80-85% of 1RM)\" for \"several sets at a moderate tempo for 4-12 repetitions per set until the point of complete fatigue, with 2-4 minutes of rest between sets\"; light and moderate weights \"as rapidly as possible for 15-25 repetitions per set, for 3-5 sets with rest intervals of 5-8 minutes.\" Leg progression: \"increasing the number of barbell squats by 20-30 each week (beginning with 50 squats per set and over a period of 2-3 months increasing this to 200-300 repetitions), then increasing the amount of weight and starting a new cycle of strength training, but beginning with a smaller number of squats.\"\n- **Rowers:** men \"50-80% of 1RM\", women \"30-40% of 1RM\" (Ulrich et al, 1966). Women rowers with light weights \"(18-20kg) for many repetitions\" improved strength-endurance \"up to 40% from initial levels\" (Chumakova, 1964), more than with \"(35-50 kg) executed in sets of 2-3 repetitions\", \"and it is even greater (by 28%) than that produced by a combination of both methods.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors present these as applications of the general principles (load set by the dynamics of the sport exercise). No mechanism is given per sport.\n\n**When to apply** — As reference doses when designing strength-endurance work for cyclic athletes. Inference: the skater squat progression (50 to 200-300 reps per set over 2-3 months) is an extreme local-endurance model with no obvious football use; the rower result (light, many reps beating heavy, low-rep sets for strength-endurance) is the transferable point.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The skaters' 80-85% 1RM conflicts with the 75-80% 1RM ceiling stated a page earlier (see [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]]). Loads mix % of 1RM and % of bodymass. The Chumakova \"by 28%\" comparison is ambiguous (28 percentage points or 28% relative is not stated). No sample sizes, durations or outcome tests are given. These are Soviet-era single-source prescriptions.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: the skater squat progression from 50 to 200-300 reps per set before raising the weight and restarting with fewer reps (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]]; [[heuristic--strength-endurance-under-demanding-sport-like-conditions]]; [[claim--strength-endurance-hypertrophy-and-cyclic-endurance]]; [[framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial]]\n=========== claim--zatsiorsky-and-hansen-show-strength-endurance-training-specificity\n# Endurance Training at 60% Raised Isometric Endurance 84%, Dynamic Endurance 93% and Repeated-Isometric Endurance 219%, While Maximal-Resistance Training Left Endurance Unchanged\n\n**What it is** — Two experiments reported in 5.2.5 (PDF pp. 296-297 / printed pp. 285-286, verified against the page images):\n- **Zatsiorsky (1966), elbow flexors, 5 weeks:** \"isometric endurance increased 84% as a result of exercising 10 times a day for 5 weeks with the elbow flexors holding a 60% maximal contraction for as long as possible. Dynamic endurance increased by 93% as a result of performing these arm curls with a weight of 60% of 1RM at a rate of 28 repetitions per minute until it was impossible to increase the tempo further. At the same time, the ability to execute repetitive isometric contractions (60% of maximal strength for 5 seconds of tension and 2 seconds of rest) to failure increased by 219%.\"\n- **Hansen (1963):** \"A similar experiment with 10 dynamic movements executed daily with maximal resistance for 5 weeks resulted in increases in both dynamic and static strength, but dynamic and static endurance remained almost unchanged.\"\n- The authors' conclusion: \"This indicates that it is necessary to optimise the volume of work required to develop strength-endurance.\"\n\nReading note: in the image the \"28\" sits in the shaded page gutter; the first digit is faint but reads as 2.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give the results without a mechanism beyond the 75-80% ceiling statement that heavy work trains \"strength predominantly\" (see [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]]). Inference: the text's sentence \"At the same time, the ability to execute repetitive isometric contractions ... increased by 219%\" does not say which training group (isometric or dynamic) showed the 219%; the passage is ambiguous.\n\n**When to apply** — As the authors' evidence that endurance gains come from submaximal, long-duration work and not from maximal-resistance training. Inference: a maximal-strength block will not by itself raise local muscular endurance, per Hansen's result.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Single-muscle-group (elbow flexor) studies with no subject numbers or populations given. Inference: percentage gains in time-to-failure tests are not on the same scale as strength percentages, so do not compare them directly. Which group produced the 219% is unclear. Do not generalise to whole-body or sport endurance.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Zatsiorsky's 60% elbow flexor protocols versus Hansen's 10 daily maximal movements (PDF pp. 296-297 / printed pp. 285-286).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]]; [[framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial]]; [[principle--local-muscular-endurance-adaptation-is-specific-to-the-trained-muscles]]; [[claim--isometric-endurance-limits-and-recovery-phases]]\n=========== heuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement\n# Depth Jumps: Once or Twice a Week, Kept 3-4 Days From Technique Sessions, Every 10-14 Days In Season and Not Within 10 Days of Competition\n\n**What it is** — From the authors' depth-jump recommendations (PDF pp. 286-287 / printed pp. 275-276, verified against the page images):\n- **Weekly frequency (item 3):** depth jumps \"in the volume indicated should be done once or twice a week in training sessions devoted to special strength training\", which can also include \"localised exercises for other muscle groups and general developmental exercises executed with a small volume.\" But \"Well-conditioned athletes can execute depth-jumps 3 times per week for 2 sets of 10 jumps at the completion of technical training in the particular sport.\"\n- **Spacing from technique (item 4):** \"Depth-jumps strongly excite the nervous system. Therefore, they should be used not less than 3-4 days before a technique training session. Sessions devoted to general physical preparation with a small volume of work should be done after depth-jump workouts.\"\n- **Season (item 5):** \"Depth-jumps occupy a fundamental place in the second half of the preparatory period of the yearly cycle. However, they can function as a means of maintaining the achieved level of special strength fitness in the competition period. During the competition period they should be included once every 10-14 days, but no later than 10 days before a competition.\"\n- **Delayed effect (item 15):** integration \"depends on not disrupting the delayed training effect of prior strength work. It should be noted that the use of depth-jumps as a means of stimulation can delay this effect for 5-6 days.\"\n\n**Internal conflict, recorded:** item 4 keeps depth jumps at least 3-4 days before a technique session, while item 3 allows well-conditioned athletes three depth-jump sessions a week placed \"at the completion of technical training.\" Three sessions a week tied to technical training cannot all sit 3-4 days before the next technique session. Both are on PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275; the book does not reconcile them.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' stated reasons: strong excitation of the nervous system (item 4) and a 5-6 day delay of the training effect of prior strength work (item 15). Neither cites a study. Inference: the in-season 10-day cut-off before competition is consistent with item 15's 5-6 day delayed effect plus a margin; the book does not make that link.\n\n**When to apply** — Placing depth jumps in a football week or year. Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\"\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 105). Inference: the authors' in-season rule (one exposure every 10-14 days, none in the 10 days before a game) is far sparser than a weekly game schedule allows; read literally, a team playing every 7 days would have no legal in-season depth-jump day. That is a reason to treat the 10-day rule as written for track-and-field competition calendars, which the book does not say.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — These are experience-based rules with no population, drop height or study given. They are written for an annual cycle with a long preparatory period and spaced competitions (the authors' examples elsewhere in 5.2.4 are track-and-field jumpers). Do not quote the 3-4 day spacing without the item 3 exception. The 5-6 day delay applies to depth jumps used \"as a means of stimulation\"; the book does not say how it scales with volume.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: depth jumps central in the second half of the preparatory period, then once every 10-14 days in the competition period, last one at least 10 days out (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]; [[claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear]] (Ch4.1, the delayed training effect); [[framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy]]; [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]]","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"142af547-4fc7-4706-b40e-7e400a91c0aa","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:28:57.437Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"=========== case-study--soviet-sport-specific-strength-endurance-prescriptions\n# Sport-Specific Strength-Endurance Prescriptions: Skiers, Distance Runners, Skaters and Rowers\n\n**What it is** — The authors' examples of applying the general strength-endurance principles \"differently in each specific case\" (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286, verified against the page image):\n- **Skiers (Chistyakov, 1965):** in the preparatory period, weights \"up to 65% of 1RM\", combined with simulation exercises on a flat surface and roller-ski movements.\n- **Middle and long distance runners (Likachyevskaya & Kovalchuk, 1963):** a barbell of \"55-60% of bodymass\" lifted to head level \"8-10 times rhythmically\"; clean-and-jerks with \"60-85% of bodymass\"; kettlebell jumps from a squat with \"about one-third of bodymass for 18-20 repetitions\"; squats with \"55-60% of bodymass until complete fatigue.\" Also (Popov, 1966): \"60-80% of 1RM for multiple repetitions, jumping and hard running.\"\n- **Skaters (Mikhailov & Panov, 1975):** heavy weights \"(80-85% of 1RM)\" for \"several sets at a moderate tempo for 4-12 repetitions per set until the point of complete fatigue, with 2-4 minutes of rest between sets\"; light and moderate weights \"as rapidly as possible for 15-25 repetitions per set, for 3-5 sets with rest intervals of 5-8 minutes.\" Leg progression: \"increasing the number of barbell squats by 20-30 each week (beginning with 50 squats per set and over a period of 2-3 months increasing this to 200-300 repetitions), then increasing the amount of weight and starting a new cycle of strength training, but beginning with a smaller number of squats.\"\n- **Rowers:** men \"50-80% of 1RM\", women \"30-40% of 1RM\" (Ulrich et al, 1966). Women rowers with light weights \"(18-20kg) for many repetitions\" improved strength-endurance \"up to 40% from initial levels\" (Chumakova, 1964), more than with \"(35-50 kg) executed in sets of 2-3 repetitions\", \"and it is even greater (by 28%) than that produced by a combination of both methods.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors present these as applications of the general principles (load set by the dynamics of the sport exercise). No mechanism is given per sport.\n\n**When to apply** — As reference doses when designing strength-endurance work for cyclic athletes. Inference: the skater squat progression (50 to 200-300 reps per set over 2-3 months) is an extreme local-endurance model with no obvious football use; the rower result (light, many reps beating heavy, low-rep sets for strength-endurance) is the transferable point.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The skaters' 80-85% 1RM conflicts with the 75-80% 1RM ceiling stated a page earlier (see [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]]). Loads mix % of 1RM and % of bodymass. The Chumakova \"by 28%\" comparison is ambiguous (28 percentage points or 28% relative is not stated). No sample sizes, durations or outcome tests are given. These are Soviet-era single-source prescriptions.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: the skater squat progression from 50 to 200-300 reps per set before raising the weight and restarting with fewer reps (PDF p. 297 / printed p. 286).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]]; [[heuristic--strength-endurance-under-demanding-sport-like-conditions]]; [[claim--strength-endurance-hypertrophy-and-cyclic-endurance]]; [[framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial]]\n=========== claim--zatsiorsky-and-hansen-show-strength-endurance-training-specificity\n# Endurance Training at 60% Raised Isometric Endurance 84%, Dynamic Endurance 93% and Repeated-Isometric Endurance 219%, While Maximal-Resistance Training Left Endurance Unchanged\n\n**What it is** — Two experiments reported in 5.2.5 (PDF pp. 296-297 / printed pp. 285-286, verified against the page images):\n- **Zatsiorsky (1966), elbow flexors, 5 weeks:** \"isometric endurance increased 84% as a result of exercising 10 times a day for 5 weeks with the elbow flexors holding a 60% maximal contraction for as long as possible. Dynamic endurance increased by 93% as a result of performing these arm curls with a weight of 60% of 1RM at a rate of 28 repetitions per minute until it was impossible to increase the tempo further. At the same time, the ability to execute repetitive isometric contractions (60% of maximal strength for 5 seconds of tension and 2 seconds of rest) to failure increased by 219%.\"\n- **Hansen (1963):** \"A similar experiment with 10 dynamic movements executed daily with maximal resistance for 5 weeks resulted in increases in both dynamic and static strength, but dynamic and static endurance remained almost unchanged.\"\n- The authors' conclusion: \"This indicates that it is necessary to optimise the volume of work required to develop strength-endurance.\"\n\nReading note: in the image the \"28\" sits in the shaded page gutter; the first digit is faint but reads as 2.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give the results without a mechanism beyond the 75-80% ceiling statement that heavy work trains \"strength predominantly\" (see [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]]). Inference: the text's sentence \"At the same time, the ability to execute repetitive isometric contractions ... increased by 219%\" does not say which training group (isometric or dynamic) showed the 219%; the passage is ambiguous.\n\n**When to apply** — As the authors' evidence that endurance gains come from submaximal, long-duration work and not from maximal-resistance training. Inference: a maximal-strength block will not by itself raise local muscular endurance, per Hansen's result.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Single-muscle-group (elbow flexor) studies with no subject numbers or populations given. Inference: percentage gains in time-to-failure tests are not on the same scale as strength percentages, so do not compare them directly. Which group produced the 219% is unclear. Do not generalise to whole-body or sport endurance.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Zatsiorsky's 60% elbow flexor protocols versus Hansen's 10 daily maximal movements (PDF pp. 296-297 / printed pp. 285-286).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling]]; [[framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial]]; [[principle--local-muscular-endurance-adaptation-is-specific-to-the-trained-muscles]]; [[claim--isometric-endurance-limits-and-recovery-phases]]\n=========== heuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement\n# Depth Jumps: Once or Twice a Week, Kept 3-4 Days From Technique Sessions, Every 10-14 Days In Season and Not Within 10 Days of Competition\n\n**What it is** — From the authors' depth-jump recommendations (PDF pp. 286-287 / printed pp. 275-276, verified against the page images):\n- **Weekly frequency (item 3):** depth jumps \"in the volume indicated should be done once or twice a week in training sessions devoted to special strength training\", which can also include \"localised exercises for other muscle groups and general developmental exercises executed with a small volume.\" But \"Well-conditioned athletes can execute depth-jumps 3 times per week for 2 sets of 10 jumps at the completion of technical training in the particular sport.\"\n- **Spacing from technique (item 4):** \"Depth-jumps strongly excite the nervous system. Therefore, they should be used not less than 3-4 days before a technique training session. Sessions devoted to general physical preparation with a small volume of work should be done after depth-jump workouts.\"\n- **Season (item 5):** \"Depth-jumps occupy a fundamental place in the second half of the preparatory period of the yearly cycle. However, they can function as a means of maintaining the achieved level of special strength fitness in the competition period. During the competition period they should be included once every 10-14 days, but no later than 10 days before a competition.\"\n- **Delayed effect (item 15):** integration \"depends on not disrupting the delayed training effect of prior strength work. It should be noted that the use of depth-jumps as a means of stimulation can delay this effect for 5-6 days.\"\n\n**Internal conflict, recorded:** item 4 keeps depth jumps at least 3-4 days before a technique session, while item 3 allows well-conditioned athletes three depth-jump sessions a week placed \"at the completion of technical training.\" Three sessions a week tied to technical training cannot all sit 3-4 days before the next technique session. Both are on PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275; the book does not reconcile them.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' stated reasons: strong excitation of the nervous system (item 4) and a 5-6 day delay of the training effect of prior strength work (item 15). Neither cites a study. Inference: the in-season 10-day cut-off before competition is consistent with item 15's 5-6 day delayed effect plus a margin; the book does not make that link.\n\n**When to apply** — Placing depth jumps in a football week or year. Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\"\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 105). Inference: the authors' in-season rule (one exposure every 10-14 days, none in the 10 days before a game) is far sparser than a weekly game schedule allows; read literally, a team playing every 7 days would have no legal in-season depth-jump day. That is a reason to treat the 10-day rule as written for track-and-field competition calendars, which the book does not say.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — These are experience-based rules with no population, drop height or study given. They are written for an annual cycle with a long preparatory period and spaced competitions (the authors' examples elsewhere in 5.2.4 are track-and-field jumpers). Do not quote the 3-4 day spacing without the item 3 exception. The 5-6 day delay applies to depth jumps used \"as a means of stimulation\"; the book does not say how it scales with volume.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: depth jumps central in the second half of the preparatory period, then once every 10-14 days in the competition period, last one at least 10 days out (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest]]; [[claim--delayed-strength-training-effect-lag-before-gains-appear]] (Ch4.1, the delayed training effect); [[framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy]]; [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"99a9ba65-4211-42a5-b16f-effb4ad554e8","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
