{"parentUuid":"dff099f2-6dce-465f-ac74-ed8218653a60","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac44742970c7341bd","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffcw9z5yoE1ULummjvf7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011Z315mm8xobdrZKJ8xNVPo","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/; for f in claim--heavy-squats-and-light-fast-lifts-miss-the-eccentric-concentric-switch heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern heuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps; do echo \"== $f\"; grep -o \"Eric's baseline.\\{0,420\\}\" $f.md; done; sed -n '255,265p' /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/docs/method-baseline.md | cut -c1-600","description":"Show untagged baseline citations and line 261"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1620,"cache_read_input_tokens":187340,"output_tokens":312,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1620},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":312,"cache_read_input_tokens":187340,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1620,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1620,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_011Z315mm8xobdrZKJ8xNVPo":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/; for f in claim--heavy-squats-and-light-fast-lifts-miss-the-eccentric-concentric-switch heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern heuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps; do echo \"== $f\"; grep -o \"Eric's baseline.\\{0,420\\}\" $f.md; done; sed -n '255,265p' /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/docs/method-baseline.md | cut -c1-600","description":"Show untagged baseline citations and line 261"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cffcvyov4rtETQbZbtX1N","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"fb1815be-b7af-4f46-85d4-fc9cf22246c1","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:29:16.968Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"fb1815be-b7af-4f46-85d4-fc9cf22246c1","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"0b81aa46-3ac1-41a7-bee9-ff3aba05ec92","agentId":"ac44742970c7341bd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011Z315mm8xobdrZKJ8xNVPo","type":"tool_result","content":"== claim--heavy-squats-and-light-fast-lifts-miss-the-eccentric-concentric-switch\nEric's baseline: \"Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 104). Inference: this entry is the authors' case for why the jumps in that list are not interchangeable with the squats.\n== heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern\nEric's baseline: speed is taught \"as the quality with the shortest ground-contact-time demand (~0.08s male, ~0.10s female at max velocity)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 96). Inference: those sprint contacts sit under the authors' 0.15 s coupling guide, though contact time and coupling time are not the same interval (coupling is only the eccentric-to-concentric transition); a force plate contact time over 0.15 s does not by itsel\n== heuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps\nEric's baseline: the Vermeil hierarchy (work capacity, then strength, then power, then speed, then sport skill, \"each level a required foundation for the next\") is the department's programming \"compass\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 101). Inference: the authors' sequence (strength base, static jumps, low hops, then depth jumps) is the same ordering applied inside the power level. Inference: single-leg and change-of-direction dept\n    **Status: RESOLVED 2026-10-02 (Eric).** Daily jumps are collected to build the rolling baseline and SWC; Wednesday is the single decision read that changes the week.\n13. **\"We don't assess readiness\" against mRSI as the readiness marker.** Q22 says Applied Performance does not assess readiness in RTP; the SportSmith piece and Q2 describe mRSI readiness reads. Inference: Q22 is scoped to RTP, so these probably do not conflict, but the RTP templates should be checked.\n14. **\"Support\" role against \"it doesn't really end\".** The Playbook frames RTP input as support to Athletic Medicine and Athletic Performance decisions [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.6]. Q4 describes ongoing testing, progression and shared loading decisions with no end point. Inference: compatible (support can be continuous), but the Playbook wording understates the role.\n15. **The Q3 rule against current practice.** Eric's rule is that a test with no question or need has no place (Q3). Yet ACWR is still looked at (\"We dabble with ACWR\") though he does not \"trust it for anything\", and movement screens are \"just collected\" without changing decisions (Q12). Inference: by his own rule both are candidates to retire or to be given a question.\n16. **No true 1RM against %1RM prescription.** No sport tests true 1RM (Q10), while Prilepin's chart and the phase bands are written in %1RM [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.19; week1-lecture.md pp.43-48]. Inference: loads are percentages of projected maxes, so their error carries into every prescription. Not a contradiction in practice, but worth stating.\n    **Status: RESOLVED 2026-10-02 (Eric).** Keep %1RM and Prilepin prescription, stated explicitly as built on projected maxes (observation plus history, Q10), not tested 1RMs.\n17. **DSI as a plyo-vs-strength switch against the evidence.** Eric uses DSI to choose plyometric versus max-strength emphasis (Q21). The evidence check (`docs/synthesis/_work/dsi-evidence-check.md`) finds that IMTP PF explains most of DSI's variance, that the 0.60/0.80 cutoffs were never validated, and that the one controlled trial found a benefit only in its strength arm. Inference: keep the test, but decide on CMJ PF and IMTP PF alongside the ratio, and set cutoffs from the roster.\n18. **COD and FMS gates in the RTP templates against Q12/Q13.** The LE template gates Evaluation on the 10-5 COD test and on FMS > 14 (`docs/interview/2026-09-28-method-interview.md`), while Q13 says COD is not tested in most sports and Q12 says screens do not change decisions.\n    **Status: RESOLVED 2026-10-02 (Eric).** The gates stay in the RTP templates. The baseline names who runs them (Athletic Medicine or the sport coach), not Applied Performance, consistent with \"the template answers readiness\" (Q22).\n\n---","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"c13b1d02-2936-452f-9fc9-3c5c9e66301b","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:29:17.203Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"== claim--heavy-squats-and-light-fast-lifts-miss-the-eccentric-concentric-switch\nEric's baseline: \"Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 104). Inference: this entry is the authors' case for why the jumps in that list are not interchangeable with the squats.\n== heuristic--plyometric-training-coupling-under-0-15-s-feedforward-and-force-time-pattern\nEric's baseline: speed is taught \"as the quality with the shortest ground-contact-time demand (~0.08s male, ~0.10s female at max velocity)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 96). Inference: those sprint contacts sit under the authors' 0.15 s coupling guide, though contact time and coupling time are not the same interval (coupling is only the eccentric-to-concentric transition); a force plate contact time over 0.15 s does not by itsel\n== heuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps\nEric's baseline: the Vermeil hierarchy (work capacity, then strength, then power, then speed, then sport skill, \"each level a required foundation for the next\") is the department's programming \"compass\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 101). Inference: the authors' sequence (strength base, static jumps, low hops, then depth jumps) is the same ordering applied inside the power level. Inference: single-leg and change-of-direction dept\n    **Status: RESOLVED 2026-10-02 (Eric).** Daily jumps are collected to build the rolling baseline and SWC; Wednesday is the single decision read that changes the week.\n13. **\"We don't assess readiness\" against mRSI as the readiness marker.** Q22 says Applied Performance does not assess readiness in RTP; the SportSmith piece and Q2 describe mRSI readiness reads. Inference: Q22 is scoped to RTP, so these probably do not conflict, but the RTP templates should be checked.\n14. **\"Support\" role against \"it doesn't really end\".** The Playbook frames RTP input as support to Athletic Medicine and Athletic Performance decisions [baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md p.6]. Q4 describes ongoing testing, progression and shared loading decisions with no end point. Inference: compatible (support can be continuous), but the Playbook wording understates the role.\n15. **The Q3 rule against current practice.** Eric's rule is that a test with no question or need has no place (Q3). Yet ACWR is still looked at (\"We dabble with ACWR\") though he does not \"trust it for anything\", and movement screens are \"just collected\" without changing decisions (Q12). Inference: by his own rule both are candidates to retire or to be given a question.\n16. **No true 1RM against %1RM prescription.** No sport tests true 1RM (Q10), while Prilepin's chart and the phase bands are written in %1RM [01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md p.19; week1-lecture.md pp.43-48]. Inference: loads are percentages of projected maxes, so their error carries into every prescription. Not a contradiction in practice, but worth stating.\n    **Status: RESOLVED 2026-10-02 (Eric).** Keep %1RM and Prilepin prescription, stated explicitly as built on projected maxes (observation plus history, Q10), not tested 1RMs.\n17. **DSI as a plyo-vs-strength switch against the evidence.** Eric uses DSI to choose plyometric versus max-strength emphasis (Q21). The evidence check (`docs/synthesis/_work/dsi-evidence-check.md`) finds that IMTP PF explains most of DSI's variance, that the 0.60/0.80 cutoffs were never validated, and that the one controlled trial found a benefit only in its strength arm. Inference: keep the test, but decide on CMJ PF and IMTP PF alongside the ratio, and set cutoffs from the roster.\n18. **COD and FMS gates in the RTP templates against Q12/Q13.** The LE template gates Evaluation on the 10-5 COD test and on FMS > 14 (`docs/interview/2026-09-28-method-interview.md`), while Q13 says COD is not tested in most sports and Q12 says screens do not change decisions.\n    **Status: RESOLVED 2026-10-02 (Eric).** The gates stay in the RTP templates. The baseline names who runs them (Athletic Medicine or the sport coach), not Applied Performance, consistent with \"the template answers readiness\" (Q22).\n\n---","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"fb1815be-b7af-4f46-85d4-fc9cf22246c1","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
