{"parentUuid":"f2a5f8ef-9701-4478-a9d2-5df5bb85da01","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac44742970c7341bd","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffd1k92co4Qr6aiNdW3N","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Disagreement 1 holds. Now checking two more GO entries against the pages: depth-landings and squat 1.5-2."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":344,"cache_read_input_tokens":196677,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":344,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffd1aWLmmf4Ds3D4yfp8","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"735148d5-9007-4f4c-b26f-d66619c2bda0","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:30:19.416Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"735148d5-9007-4f4c-b26f-d66619c2bda0","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac44742970c7341bd","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffd1k92co4Qr6aiNdW3N","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DvrAp9i9hCBfYhwd5BrWUb","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/; sed -n '16,20p' claim--depth-landings-from-2-to-3-2-m-produce-forces-over-20-times-bodyweight.md | cut -c1-900; echo ----; sed -n '16,19p' claim--squat-1-5-to-2-bodymass-rule-applies-to-maximal-depth-jumps-only.md | cut -c1-1200; echo ----; sed -n '16,22p' heuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps.md | cut -c1-700","description":"Read GO entries depth-landings, squat rule, preparatory"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":344,"cache_read_input_tokens":196677,"output_tokens":593,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":244},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":344},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":593,"cache_read_input_tokens":196677,"cache_creation_input_tokens":344,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":344,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01DvrAp9i9hCBfYhwd5BrWUb":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/; sed -n '16,20p' claim--depth-landings-from-2-to-3-2-m-produce-forces-over-20-times-bodyweight.md | cut -c1-900; echo ----; sed -n '16,19p' claim--squat-1-5-to-2-bodymass-rule-applies-to-maximal-depth-jumps-only.md | cut -c1-1200; echo ----; sed -n '16,22p' heuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps.md | cut -c1-700","description":"Read GO entries depth-landings, squat rule, preparatory"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cffd1aWLmmf4Ds3D4yfp8","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"9ce33eb8-d245-49ad-a53c-6fd1da914b64","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:30:21.551Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"9ce33eb8-d245-49ad-a53c-6fd1da914b64","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"0b81aa46-3ac1-41a7-bee9-ff3aba05ec92","agentId":"ac44742970c7341bd","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DvrAp9i9hCBfYhwd5BrWUb","type":"tool_result","content":"**What it is** — \"*depth landings* (i.e. depth jumps without the rebound after landing) alone can also have a significant effect on concentric and eccentric strength. This was shown by Dursenev and Raevsky (1978) who investigated the effect of depth landings from heights of 2m or more onto soft gymnastic mats. Under these conditions, the subjects acquired the ability to display brief muscle tension (over periods of between 0.028-0.061 secs) of magnitude 1500-3500 kgf, i.e. over 20 times bodyweight for heights of up to 3.2 metres. The rationale for using this method was that weight training or other similar methods of resistance training cannot produce this intensity of voluntary effort.\" \"the maximum possible height which the athletes could contemplate using produced the greatest increase in strength, although they often hesitated to attempt these extremely stressful jumps\" (PDF p. 288 /\n\nThe authors' caveats on the same page: \"the drop heights studied were for experimental purposes alone and it would be most inadvisable to use drops of 3.2 metres for training.\" \"It appeared that the main consideration for enhancing strength is not the type of muscle contraction, but the intensity and speed of contraction elicited by the exercise.\" Increase drop height \"gradually from fairly low levels\", \"limit the number of drops per session\", \"land on suitably firm shock-absorbing surfaces\" and attend to landing technique.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' rationale: intensity of tension that resistance training cannot reach, with intensity and speed of contraction, not contraction type, as \"the main consideration.\" Inference: \"over 20 times bodyweight\" holds for a 75 kg subject at the low end (1500 kgf / 75 kg = 20); the book gives no subject masses, so the ratio is approximate.\n----\n**What it is** — \"Some authors rightly criticize the recommendation sometimes put forward that athletes should be able to squat 1.5 - 2 times bodymass in order to use certain plyometric drills, since they consider that this criterion is not based on research evidence and does not apply to all plyometric exercises. This recommendation is a safety measure based on force plate analysis of *maximal plyometrics* in the form of depth jumps, where the ground reaction force on the lower extremity can easily exceed 6 times bodyweight. *This guideline was never intended to apply religiously to submaximal plyometrics using small drops, skipping drills or less demanding actions.* This misunderstanding of Russian guidelines is largely a result of an incomplete grasp of the scientific principles on which the original shock method was based\" (PDF pp. 293-294 / printed pp. 282-283; p. 282 verified against the page image, p. 283 from the text layer).\n\nBoth halves are the authors': they agree the criterion is not research-based (\"rightly criticize\"), then defend it as a force-plate-based safety measure for maximal depth jumps.\n\n----\n**What it is** — The authors' preparation rules (pages verified against the page images):\n- \"Depth-jumps require special preliminary preparation such as a significant volume of barbell exercises and traditional jumping, short sprints and skipping over a period of several months\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275, item 1). Contra-indications: \"Muscle or tendon fatigue, pain or soreness, as well as incompletely healed injuries\" (same item).\n- \"depth-jumping is not a basic type of training to be indulged in lightly by athletes who are relatively unused to explosive or strongly resisted movements. For anyone who is not accustomed to movements such as weightlifting cleans, jerks, squats or hurdling, it is highly advisable that a preparatory programme using submaximal plyometrics, non-impact and non-plyometrics be followed\" first (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276).\n- **Eccentric and explosive-isometric strength first:** \"a primary requirement for anyone contemplating the use of classical plyometrics is a high level of eccentric strength and explosive isometric strength. Herein lies the value of small-range squats, power cleans, power snatches, cleans from the hang, snatches from the hang, push presses or jerks, push presses or jerks from racks, and the descent of flights of stairs.\" Front and back squats both, \"since they each model different types of jump (directly upwards versus forwards and upwards).\" \"Slow quasi-isometric squats over a moderate range with heavy weights should later lead to more rapid dips over a shallower range with lighter weights\n- **Static start before dynamic start:** \"it is initially useful to improve jumping ability from a static start, without exposing oneself to the greater musculotendinous stress of a dynamic start\": standing broad and vertical jumps without a dip, then the vertical jump test \"progressing from a static flexed knee start to a dynamic dip start with arms flung vigorously upwards\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277). Progress can be tracked with the Lewis formula ([[claim--lewis-formula-estimates-jump-power-from-bodymass-and-height]]).\n- **Low hops:** \"hopping on the spot at a frequency which feels most comfortable for the athlete, using a few sets of about 25 repetitions\", then small forward, backward, sideward, forward-backward and zig-zag hops, then vertical hops \"as rapidly as possible, again using a few sets of about 20 repetitions\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275, item 6). Rope skipping is useful, but \"long periods of skipping are not the appropriate form of preparatory drill\" because the aim is speed-strength, not endurance (item 7).\n- **Technique and complexity:** learn normal jumping first, with \"the correct lumbar-pelvic rhythm, as is also used during clean pulls or power cleans from the ground\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276, item 13). \"Great caution\" with depth jumps involving \"one-legged thrusts, changes of direction or complex variations, since the injury risk is significantly higher\" (item 14).","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"f538ab9d-f575-461a-bae6-e76aeede4903","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:30:21.783Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"**What it is** — \"*depth landings* (i.e. depth jumps without the rebound after landing) alone can also have a significant effect on concentric and eccentric strength. This was shown by Dursenev and Raevsky (1978) who investigated the effect of depth landings from heights of 2m or more onto soft gymnastic mats. Under these conditions, the subjects acquired the ability to display brief muscle tension (over periods of between 0.028-0.061 secs) of magnitude 1500-3500 kgf, i.e. over 20 times bodyweight for heights of up to 3.2 metres. The rationale for using this method was that weight training or other similar methods of resistance training cannot produce this intensity of voluntary effort.\" \"the maximum possible height which the athletes could contemplate using produced the greatest increase in strength, although they often hesitated to attempt these extremely stressful jumps\" (PDF p. 288 /\n\nThe authors' caveats on the same page: \"the drop heights studied were for experimental purposes alone and it would be most inadvisable to use drops of 3.2 metres for training.\" \"It appeared that the main consideration for enhancing strength is not the type of muscle contraction, but the intensity and speed of contraction elicited by the exercise.\" Increase drop height \"gradually from fairly low levels\", \"limit the number of drops per session\", \"land on suitably firm shock-absorbing surfaces\" and attend to landing technique.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' rationale: intensity of tension that resistance training cannot reach, with intensity and speed of contraction, not contraction type, as \"the main consideration.\" Inference: \"over 20 times bodyweight\" holds for a 75 kg subject at the low end (1500 kgf / 75 kg = 20); the book gives no subject masses, so the ratio is approximate.\n----\n**What it is** — \"Some authors rightly criticize the recommendation sometimes put forward that athletes should be able to squat 1.5 - 2 times bodymass in order to use certain plyometric drills, since they consider that this criterion is not based on research evidence and does not apply to all plyometric exercises. This recommendation is a safety measure based on force plate analysis of *maximal plyometrics* in the form of depth jumps, where the ground reaction force on the lower extremity can easily exceed 6 times bodyweight. *This guideline was never intended to apply religiously to submaximal plyometrics using small drops, skipping drills or less demanding actions.* This misunderstanding of Russian guidelines is largely a result of an incomplete grasp of the scientific principles on which the original shock method was based\" (PDF pp. 293-294 / printed pp. 282-283; p. 282 verified against the page image, p. 283 from the text layer).\n\nBoth halves are the authors': they agree the criterion is not research-based (\"rightly criticize\"), then defend it as a force-plate-based safety measure for maximal depth jumps.\n\n----\n**What it is** — The authors' preparation rules (pages verified against the page images):\n- \"Depth-jumps require special preliminary preparation such as a significant volume of barbell exercises and traditional jumping, short sprints and skipping over a period of several months\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275, item 1). Contra-indications: \"Muscle or tendon fatigue, pain or soreness, as well as incompletely healed injuries\" (same item).\n- \"depth-jumping is not a basic type of training to be indulged in lightly by athletes who are relatively unused to explosive or strongly resisted movements. For anyone who is not accustomed to movements such as weightlifting cleans, jerks, squats or hurdling, it is highly advisable that a preparatory programme using submaximal plyometrics, non-impact and non-plyometrics be followed\" first (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276).\n- **Eccentric and explosive-isometric strength first:** \"a primary requirement for anyone contemplating the use of classical plyometrics is a high level of eccentric strength and explosive isometric strength. Herein lies the value of small-range squats, power cleans, power snatches, cleans from the hang, snatches from the hang, push presses or jerks, push presses or jerks from racks, and the descent of flights of stairs.\" Front and back squats both, \"since they each model different types of jump (directly upwards versus forwards and upwards).\" \"Slow quasi-isometric squats over a moderate range with heavy weights should later lead to more rapid dips over a shallower range with lighter weights\n- **Static start before dynamic start:** \"it is initially useful to improve jumping ability from a static start, without exposing oneself to the greater musculotendinous stress of a dynamic start\": standing broad and vertical jumps without a dip, then the vertical jump test \"progressing from a static flexed knee start to a dynamic dip start with arms flung vigorously upwards\" (PDF p. 288 / printed p. 277). Progress can be tracked with the Lewis formula ([[claim--lewis-formula-estimates-jump-power-from-bodymass-and-height]]).\n- **Low hops:** \"hopping on the spot at a frequency which feels most comfortable for the athlete, using a few sets of about 25 repetitions\", then small forward, backward, sideward, forward-backward and zig-zag hops, then vertical hops \"as rapidly as possible, again using a few sets of about 20 repetitions\" (PDF p. 286 / printed p. 275, item 6). Rope skipping is useful, but \"long periods of skipping are not the appropriate form of preparatory drill\" because the aim is speed-strength, not endurance (item 7).\n- **Technique and complexity:** learn normal jumping first, with \"the correct lumbar-pelvic rhythm, as is also used during clean pulls or power cleans from the ground\" (PDF p. 287 / printed p. 276, item 13). \"Great caution\" with depth jumps involving \"one-legged thrusts, changes of direction or complex variations, since the injury risk is significantly higher\" (item 14).","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9ce33eb8-d245-49ad-a53c-6fd1da914b64","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
